Accumulative incidence of novel coronavirus diseases (COVID-19) in the EU/EEA and the United Kingdom increased rapidly between 1 January and 15 March 2020
Cumulative incidence of novel coronavirus disease (COVID-19) has shown similar trends in EU/EEA countries, as well as in the United Kingdom, confirming that the COVID-19 pandemic is developing rapidly, although different countries are at different stages.
Based on Italy’s experience, countries, hospitals and intensive care units need to be better prepared to welcome the surging number of COVID-19 patients, with intensive care tasks particularly important.
On December 31, 2019, a number of cases of pneumonia were reported in Wuhan, Hubei Province, China.
On January 9, 2020, China’s Centers for Disease Control and Prevention (CDC) reported that the pathogen was a novel coronavirus now known as Severe Acute Respiratory Syndrome Syndrome Coronavirus 2 (SARS-CoV-2).
Since then, the disease caused by SARS-CoV-2 infection has been named Coronavirus Disease (COVID-19).
The evidence to date shows that approximately 80% of cases of COVID-19 are mild, respiratory infections associated with or without pneumonia, and most are able to recover.
In about 14 percent of cases, COVID-19 progressed to severe illness and required hospitalization, while the remaining 6 percent progressed to critical care and required intensive care.
The mortality rate among hospitalized patients due to COVID-19 is about 4%.
In this study, we evaluated trends in the cumulative incidence of COVID-19 in various countries of the European Union (European Union, EU/European Economic Area, EEA) as well as the United Kingdom (UK) and compared them with those in Hubei Province of China.
We also compared the number of COVID-19 cases available in EU/EEA countries and in the United Kingdom from 31 January to 15 March 2020 with Italy.
COVID-19 cases in EU/EEA countries and the United Kingdom
The geographical distribution of COVID-19 has further expanded after China, and the dynamics of the COVID-19 pandemic in other parts of the world are similar to those in China.
On March 11, 2020, the Director-General of the World Health Organization (WHO) declared COVID-19 a pandemic.
In the March 5, 2020 issue of the journal European Monitoring, Spiteri et al. reported the first confirmed cases of COVID-19 in Europe based on the WHO’s case definition.
At EU/EEA, France reported the first three confirmed cases on January 24, 2020, with returnees from Wuhan, Hubei Province, China.
COVID-19 cases have been detected in all 30 EU/EEA countries and the United Kingdom as at 15 March 2020, with 39,768 cases and 1,727 deaths reported from 31 December to 15 March 2019, including 17,750 cases and 1,441 deaths in Italy alone.
Accumulated Quantity and Cumulative Incidence of COVID-19 Cases
The European Centre for Disease Control and Prevention (ECDC) updates the number of reported COVID-19 cases in every country in the world at 8:00 a.m., available only from official sources, such as national health ministries, national and regional health authorities, and WHO.
This data has been used to assess EU/EEA and UK COVID-19 trends and to compare them with Italy.
We calculated the 14-day cumulative incidence of COVID-19 cases per EU/EEA countries from 1 January to 15 March 2020, as an indicator of the prevalence of active COVID-19 cases, taking into account the natural course of COVID-19.
We also provided the total number of reported cumulative cases in each country as of 8:00 a.m. on March 15, 2020, and were compared with data from Italy from January 31 to March 15, 2020.
COVID-19 trends in EU/EEA countries and the United Kingdom
The trend in the 14-day cumulative incidence of COVID-19 cases in EU/EEA countries and the United Kingdom is generally consistent with that of Hubei Province (China) (Figure 1).
For the EU/EEA and the United Kingdom as a whole, COVID-19’s cumulative incidence has increased since around February 21, 2020, followed by a dramatic increase around February 28 (Supplementary Materials).
This is mainly due to the rapid increase in the number of reported cases in Italy, but the cumulative incidence of COVID-19 in all other EU/EEA countries and the United Kingdom has also shown a similar growth trend (Supplementary Materials).
Figure 2 shows the number of cumulative COVID-19 cases in EU/EEA countries as well as the United Kingdom from 31 January to 15 March 2020, compared with Italy.
As of 8:00 a.m. on March 15, 15 other EU/EEA countries, as well as the United Kingdom, had already reported the total number of cases comparable to Italy in less than 3 weeks.
Our results show that the number of COVID-19 cases reported by the EU/EEA and the United Kingdom is increasing rapidly.
The observed COVID-19 accumulative incidence trend indicates that the outbreak is developing at a similar rate in all countries.
Although countries are at different stages, national public health response measures vary from country to country, the definition of cases may vary from country to country, and there are differences in the choice of patients who must undergo a COVID-19-diagnosed test, including additional tests, but the situation is the same.
According to doctors in Italy’s affected areas in early March 2020, about 10% of COVID-19 patients need intensive care, and media reports indicate that hospitals and intensive care units in these regions have reached their maximum carrying capacity.
At the EU/EEA level, only 6% and 1% of COVID-19 cases that are currently available for hospitalization/or access to intensive care units (data are not shown).
However, this information should be collected in a systematic manner in order to supplement current monitoring data focusing on the number of reported cases and deaths.
According to a study conducted in 2010-11, there are significant differences in the number of intensive care and mid-care beds available in European countries, ranging from 29.2 in Germany to 4.2 in Portugal.
This means that countries may have more or less resources than Italy (12.5 intensive care and mid-care beds per 100,000 people in 2010-2011).
ECCD’s sixth update to the COVID-19 rapid risk assessment modeled scenarios related to saturation of healthcare capacity and estimated the relationship between the prevalence of hospitalized COVID-19 patients in each EU/EEA country as well as the United Kingdom and the risk of exceeding 90 per cent in intensive care beds.
Since cases have so far clustered in EU/EEA countries as well as certain areas of the UK, and hospitals and intensive care units typically provide services for population coverage in identified areas, it is best to provide information on cases and intensive care beds at the “Standard Statistical Geographical Unit 2” (NUTS-2) level.
Italy’s experience, as well as current trends in other countries, suggest that the EU/EEA, as well as the COVID-19 pandemic in the UK, are developing rapidly.
As a result, countries, hospitals and intensive care units should be prepared to respond to SARS-CoV-2’s continued community transmission and the need for health care, in particular the number of COVID-19 patients in intensive care, such as in the affected areas of Italy.
As a recent ECDC rapid risk assessment points out, fast, positive, and comprehensive approaches are critical to delaying the spread of SARS-COV-2, requiring a transition from containment to mitigation, because, if not implemented earlier, the rapid increase in the number of expected cases could give policymakers and hospitals not enough time to understand, accept, and adjust their responses.
The rapid risk assessment also lists public health measures to mitigate the effects of the pandemic.
There’s a brief window of opportunity, and countries are likely to step up their controls to slow the spread of SARS-CoV-2 and reduce pressure on their health care systems.
Otherwise, healthcare systems in other EU/EEA countries are likely to face a surge in patients in need of intensive care in the coming days and weeks.
The 2019 coronavirus disease (COVID-19), caused by Severe Acute Respiratory Syndrome (SARS) Coronavirus 2 (SARS-CoV-2), has so far infected more than 80,000 people in China and other parts of the world, resulting in more than 3,000 deaths and causing huge human disasters.
Similar to SARS-CoV, which infected tens of thousands of people in 2003, SARS-CoV-2 can also be spread by bats and cause similar symptoms through similar mechanisms.
Although COVID-19 has a lower severity and mortality rate than SARS, it is more transmissible, affecting more older people than young adults and more men than women.
In view of the rapid increase in publications related to new crown pneumonia, this article intends to conduct a timely and comprehensive review of this rapidly evolving research topic.
We will introduce the basic knowledge about epidemiology, etiology, virology, diagnosis, treatment, prognosis and prevention of the disease.
While many questions remain to be answered, we hope that this review will contribute to the understanding and eradication of this terrible disease.
The Spring Festival on January 25, 2020, has become an unprecedented and unforgettable memory of the Chinese people, and the outbreak of a new type of viral disease has forced everyone to leave their homes throughout the holidays and the weeks that followed.
This virus is highly homologous to coronavirus (CoV), which caused an outbreak of Severe Acute Respiratory Syndrome (SARS) in 2003; therefore, the World Health Organization (WHO) named it SARS-CoV-2 on February 11, 2020, and the associated disease is called Coronavirus Disease-19 (COVID-19).
The outbreak began in Wuhan, China, and quickly spread across the country before spreading to nearly 50 other countries around the world.
As of March 2, 2020, the virus has resulted in more than 80,000 confirmed cases of COVID-19, of which more than 40,000 patients have been discharged and more than 3,000 have died.
COVID-19 has emerged as “the number one public enemy” whose threats could outweigh terrorism.
Based on data from PubMed (https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pubmed/), more than 200 papers on COVID-19 have been published in less than two months since it was first reported to isolate virus sequences from multiple patients on January 7, 2020, covering virology, epidemiology, etiology, diagnosis and treatment.
This article aims to summarize the progress of research in this emerging subject area.
COVID-19 is used to comparing COVID-19 with SARS, as well as another disease caused by CoV, Middle East Respiratory Syndrome (MERS, 2012 outbreak).
We will also discuss what has been learned so far in the prevention and prognosis of the disease, as well as some pressing issues that have yet to be addressed.
In the past, CoV has long been believed to be a non-lethal pathogen for humans, which mainly causes about 15% of the common cold.
However, this century has encountered two highly pathogenic human CoVs, namely SARS-CoV and MERS-CoV, which erupted in China and Saudi Arabia in 2003 and 2012, respectively, and quickly spread to many other countries, with dire morbidity and mortality rates.
As a result, the COVID-19 outbreak is the third CoV outbreak recorded in human history.
As shown in Figure 1, Wuhan first reported a number of unexplained cases of pneumonia to the Chinese National Health Commission on December 31, 2019.
Seven days later, the CoV sequence was announced.
On January 15, 2020, Wuhan reported its first death.
At the same time, the epidemic quickly spread to neighboring cities, provinces and countries.
On January 20th, medical staff were reported to be infected, suggesting that there may have been human-to-human phenomena.
On January 23, the city of Wuhan was closed and all public transportation was closed.
On January 24, the first clinical study of new crown pneumonia reported that only 21 of the 41 confirmed cases were in direct contact with the Wuhan South China seafood market, which is believed to be the starting point for infection from unknown animal sources.
On January 30, the WHO declared the outbreak a “global health emergency.”
As of the time of publication of this report, the disease has spread throughout China and nearly 50 other countries around the world (Figure 2 (Fig.2)).
Due to the rapid development of the epidemic, the final scope and severity of this outbreak has yet to be determined.
On February 11, 2020, a multi-center study of 8,866 patients, including 4,021 confirmed COVID-19 patients, provided an updated description of the epidemic, as shown below (https://mp.weixin.qq.com/s/UlBi-HX_rHPXa1qHA2bhdA).
SARS-CoV-2 is capable of infecting people of all ages, but the main age group is 30-65 years old.
Almost half (47.7%) of those infected were over the age of 50, very few were under the age of 20, and only 14 were under the age of 10.
SARS-CoV-2 infects more men (0.31 per 100,000) than women (0.27 per 100,000).
COVID-19 is mainly agglomerated in and around Hubei.
COVID-19 takes an average of 5 (2-9) days from onset to diagnosis.
The average incubation period is 4.8 (3.0-7.2) days.
The average time from onset to death was 9.5 (4.8–13) days.
The basic regeneration number (R0) is 3.77 (95% CI: 3.51-4.05) and the adjusted R0 is 2.23-4.82.
The number of infected people has increased exponentially before January 23, 2020, coinciding with large-scale population movements ahead of China’s Spring Festival.
The mortality rate for confirmed cases was 1.44% (95% CI: 1.10-1.86%), and the adjusted mortality rate for all patients was 3.06% (95% CI: 2.02-4.59%).
The three main risk factors for COVID-19 are gender (male), age (≥60 years), and severe pneumonia.
CoV is a family of large-scale envelope viruses that contain a single chain of justice RNA.
They can be divided into four genuses, α, β, γ and δ, of which α-CoV and β-CoV are known to infect humans.
SARS-CoV and MERS-CoV’s envelope prickly glycoprotein (S) combined with their cell receptor angiotensin-converting enzymes 2 (ACE2) and dipeptide peptide enzyme 4 (DPP4) before membrane fusion occurs.
The viral RNA genome is released into the cytoplasm; after the viral genome replicates, the genomic RNA, together with the envelope glycoprotein and the nucleophausing protein, forms vesicles containing the virosomal, which are then fused with the plasma membrane and release the virus.
The first genome sequence of SARS-CoV-2 was reported on January 10, 2020.
SARS-CoV-2 was found to be a new type of β-CoV with more than 99.98 percent of the 10 sequenced samples taken from the Huanan Seafood Market in Wuhan, where the outbreak began.
Compared to MERS-CoV, SARS-CoV-2 is genetically similar to SARS-CoV.
Through the transmission electron microscopy, SARS-CoV-2 particles have been found in ultra-thin slices of the epithetic epithelium.
The study found that human ACE2 is a recipient of SARS-CoV-2 and SARS-CoV.
However, compared to SARS-CoV, SARS-CoV-2’s SARS-CoV-2’s S protein is less binding to human ACE2, which is consistent with SARS-CoV-2’s fact that the severity of SARS-CoV infection is lower than that of SARS-CoV.
SARS-CoV-2 can also form new short proteins encoded by orf3b, as well as secreted proteins encoded by orf8.
The orf3b of SARS-CoV-2 may play a role in viral pathogenicity and inhibit the expression of IFNβ; however, orf8 does not contain any known functional domains or basis.
On February 18, 2020, Zhou and others reported the cryo-electron microscopy (cryo-EM) structure of ACE2, a full-grown human ACE2, with a resolution of 2.9 Å.
They found that the complex, which has an open and closed conformation, is assembled into a dimer, and that the ACE2-B0AT1 complex can bind to two S proteins, providing evidence of CoV recognition and infection.
B0AT1 may become a therapeutic target for drug screening to inhibit SARS-CoV-2 infection.
Origin and intermediate host
It is known that both SARS-CoV and MERS-CoV originated from bats and were transmitted to humans through civets and camels, respectively.
Compared with the phylogenetics of SARS-CoV-2 and other CoVs, bats are considered to be the natural hosts of SARS-CoV-2, because this new virus has a 96% consistency with two types of SARS-like CoV from bats, called bat-SL-CoVZX45 and bat-SL-CoVZX21.
However, it is not clear what intermediate hosts help viruses infect humans across species barriers, and their route of transmission has yet to be clarified.
Ji et al. proposed that snakes are carriers of viruses from bats to humans, which involve homologous recombination inside the S protein.
According to one study, researchers in Guangzhou, China, believe that a CoV found in pangolins, a long-kissed ant-eater that is often used in traditional Chinese medicine, has a genetic homology of 99% from SARS-CoV-2, it is believed that pangolins are a potential intermediate host of SARS-CoV-2.
However, the 1% difference between the two genomes is still a huge difference; therefore, the decisive result has yet to be provided by concrete evidence (Fig. 33).
The physical and chemical properties of SARS-CoV-2 remain largely unclear.
SARS-CoV and MERS-CoV can survive in vitro for 48 hours in a dry environment and up to 5 days at 20 °C and 40%-50% humidity.
SARS-CoV-2 may have similar properties.
According to reports, SARS-CoV-2 is sensitive to ultraviolet rays and heat - 56 °C for 30 minutes; ether, 75% ethanol, chlorine-containing disinfectants, peracetic acetic acid, chloroform and other lipid solvents can be effectively inactivated viruses, but chlorine has not been effectively inactivated virus.
Humans generally lack immunity to SARS-CoV-2 and are therefore susceptible to this new virus.
At present, there are no detailed studies on the immune response of SARS-CoV-2.
Therefore, we can only refer to previous studies on other CoVs, notably SARS-CoV and MERS-CoV (Fig.4).
In general, after invading the host, the virus is first identified by the host’s inherent immune system through pattern recognition receptor (PRR), including Type C glactin-like receptors, Toll-like receptors (TLRs), NOD-like receptors (NLRs), and RIG-like receptors (RLRs).
The virus induces inflammatory factor expression through different pathways, dendritic cell maturation, and type I interferon (IFN) synthesis, which limits the spread of the virus and accelerates macrophages of viral antigens.
However, SARS-CoV’s N protein can help the virus get rid of the immune response.
Adaptive immune responses will join the fight against the virus shortly thereafter.
T lymphocytes, including CD4+ and CD8+ T cells, play an important role in antiviral defense.
CD4+ T cells stimulate B cells to produce virus-specific antibodies, while CD8+ T cells directly kill cells infected by the virus.
Assistant T cells produce pro-inflammatory cytokines to help these defensive cells.
However, CoV can inhibit T-cell function by inducing apoptosis of T cells.
Immunity, including supplements such as C3a and C5a and antibodies, is also critical to fighting viral infections.
For example, antibodies isolated from recovered patients are able to neutralize MERS-CoV.
On the other hand, the overreaction of the immune system produces a large number of free radicals locally, which can cause serious damage to the lungs and other organs and, in the worst cases, lead to multiple organ failure or even death.
SARS-CoV-2 infections, characterized by aggregative onset, are more likely to affect older adults and pregnant women with multiple comorbidities.
Typically, individuals exposed to a large number of viruses or with impaired immune function are more likely to have an infection than others.
According to a study of the first 425 cases in Wuhan, the average incubation period for SARS-CoV-2 is estimated to be 1-14 days, mostly 3-7 days.
However, a study of 1,099 cases showed an average incubation period of 3 days, ranging from 0 to 24 days.
As mentioned earlier, a recent study showed that according to the demographics of 8,866 cases, the incubation period was 4.8 (3.0–7.2 days).
It’s very important for the health sector to adjust effective isolation times based on the most accurate incubation period, which prevents asymptomatic infections from spreading the virus to others.
As a matter of practice, virus contacts or infected people usually need to be quarantined for 14 days.
Should the isolation period be extended to 24 days?
Fever is usually the main and first symptom of COVID-19, and it can have no other symptoms, or it can be accompanied by symptoms such as dry cough, shortness of breath, muscle pain, dizziness, headaches, sore throats, swelling, chest pain, diarrhoea, nausea, and vomiting.
Some patients experienced difficulty breathing and/or hypoxemia a week after onset.
Severes will rapidly progress into acute respiratory distress syndrome, pus toxicity shock, metabolic acidosis, and coagulation dysfunction.
Even if there is no pulmonary imaging abnormality, patients with fever and/or respiratory symptoms and acute fever should be screened for early diagnosis.
A demographic study conducted in late December 2019 showed that the percentages of various symptoms were: fever 98%, dry cough 76%, difficulty breathing 55%, diarrhea 3%; 8% of patients needed ventilation support.
Similar results have been reported in two recent studies on the incidence of family agglomeration and the spread of asymptomatic individuals.
By comparison, a demographic study conducted in 2012 showed that patients with MERS-CoV also had fever (98%), dry cough (47%), and difficulty breathing (55%).
However, 80% of these patients needed ventilation support, far more than COVID-19 patients, which coincided with a higher mortality rate than COVID-19 for MERs.
Diarrhoea (26%) and pharyngeal (21%) were also observed in MERS patients.
In patients with SARS, fever (99%-100%), dry cough (29%-75%), difficulty breathing (40%-42%), diarrhea (20-25%), and sore throat (13-25%) are the main symptoms, and about 14%-20% of patients require ventilation support.
As of February 14, there had been 66,576 confirmed cases of COVID-19 worldwide, with a mortality rate of 2%.
By comparison, as of November 2002, SARS had a 10 percent death rate among 8,096 confirmed cases.
For MERS, according to a June 2012 demographic study, the mortality rate of 2,494 confirmed cases was 37 percent.
An earlier study reported that SARS-CoV-2 had an R0 as high as 6.47, with a 95% confidence interval (CI) of 5.71-7.23, and SARS-CoV’s R0 at just 2 to 4.
Table 1.1 shows comparisons between SARS-CoV-2 and MERS-CoV and SARA-CoV in terms of symptoms, mortality and R0.
According to the above data, SARS-CoV-2's diffusion capacity is higher than MERS-CoV and SARS-CoV, but it is less deadly than the latter two.
As a result, controlling the SARS-CoV-2 epidemic is more challenging than MERS-CoV and SARS-CoV.
Concentrative morbidity usually occurs in the same family, or from the same meeting or transportation, such as cruise ships.
Patients usually have a history of travel or residence in Wuhan or other outbreak areas within the last two weeks prior to onset, or have a history of close contact with an infected person or patient.
However, it has been reported that people can carry the virus without symptoms for more than two weeks, and cured patients who have been discharged from the hospital can carry the virus again, issuing an alert for extended isolation.
The number of the patient's periphery white blood cells (especially lymphocytes) was normal or decreased in the early stages of onset.
For example, in 1,099 patients with COVID-19, lymphocyte reduction, elevated levels of aspartate aminotransferase, and viremia were found in 4×109/L lymphocyte counts.
In some patients, elevated levels of hepatase and muscle enzymes and myoglobin in the blood can be seen in some patients, and most patients can see elevated blood C-response proteins and blood subsidence.
Heavy-duty patients can see elevated D-dimer levels and increased lymphocyte counts in heavy-duty patients.
Most COVID-19 patients can see chest imaging abnormalities, which are characterized by bilateral flaked shadows or pulmonary grading glass shadows.
Patients often develop atypical pneumonia, acute pulmonary injury, and acute respiratory distress syndrome (ARDS).
When ARDS occurs, uncontrolled inflammation, fluid build-up, and active fibrosis can seriously damage gas exchange.
Types I and II alveolar epithelial cell dysfunctions reduce surfactant levels and increase surface tension, thereby reducing pulmonary dilation and increasing the risk of pulmonary collapse.
As a result, the worst chest imaging findings usually occur at the same time as the severity of the disease.
On February 18, 2020, the first pathological analysis of COVID-19 in the lungs of a patient who died of the disease found that alveolar epithelial cell shedding, transparent membrane formation, interstitial lymphocyte infiltration, and polynucleosomal cells were consistent with viral infections and pathological manifestations of ARDS, as well as in patients with SARS and MERS.
Detection of SARS-CoV-2 RNA by reverse transcription polymerase chain reaction (RT-PCR) has been used as the primary criterion for diagnosing COVID-19.
However, due to a higher false-negative rate that may accelerate the outbreak, China began to use clinical manifestations for diagnosis on February 13, 2020 (and no longer relying solely on RT-PCR).
A similar situation occurred during the diagnosis of SARS.
Therefore, the combination of medical history, clinical manifestations, laboratory tests, and imaging results is essential for an effective diagnosis.
On February 14, 2020, Zhang’s team reported a solution for detecting SARS-CoV-2 using CRISPR-based SHERLOCK technology, which does not require complex instruments to detect synthetic SARS-CoV-2 RNA fragments with concentrations ranging from 20 × 10-18 mol/L to 200 × 10-18 mol/L (10 to 100 copies per microliter) in less than an hour using a test paper.
If verified in clinical samples, then this new technology is expected to greatly improve the sensitivity and convenience of diagnosis.
Due to the lack of experience in the treatment of novel CoVs, doctors mainly provide support treatment for COVID-19 patients, while experimenting with various therapies that have been used or proposed for use in other CoV, such as SARS-CoV and MERS-CoV, and other viral diseases (Table (Table 2.2)).
These therapies include current and potential treatments such as antivirals, immunosuppressants, steroids, plasma from rehabilitated patients, traditional Chinese medicine, and psychological support.
It is even recommended to treat the plasma of recovered patients.
Pharmaceutical companies are racing to develop antibodies and vaccines against the virus.
SARS-CoV-2 primarily attacks the lungs at the beginning, and may also attack other organs that express ACE2, such as the gastrointestinal system and the kidneys, to a lesser extent.
Despite this, respiratory dysfunction and failure are the main threats to patients and the main cause of death.
Respiratory support is therefore essential for relieving symptoms and saving lives, including routine oxygen absorption, high-traffic oxygen absorption, non-invasive ventilation, and invasive mechanical ventilation, depending on the severity of the disease.
Patients with severe respiratory symptoms must be supported by external membrane oxygenation (ECMO), an improved cardiopulmonary bypass technique used to treat life-threatening heart or respiratory failure.
In addition, maintaining electrolyte balance, preventing and treating secondary infections and septic shock, as well as protecting vital organ functions, are also essential for patients with SARS-CoV-2.
It is known that overreaction of the immune system in patients with SARS and MERS can cause cytokine storms.
Cytokine storms are a form of systemic inflammatory response characterized by the release of a range of cytokines, including TNFα, IL-1β, IL-2, IL-6, IFNα, IFNβ, IFNγ and MCP-1.
These cytokines induce immune cells to release large amounts of free radicals, which are the leading causes of ARDS and multi-organ failure.
Immunosuppression is critical in the treatment of cytokine storms, especially in severe patients.
Corticosteroids and tocilizumab – an anti-IL6 monoclonal antibody – have been used to treat cytokine storms.
Other immunosuppressive therapies for cytokine storms include: modulating T-cell directed immune responses; blocking IFN-γ, IL-1, and TNF; inhibiting JAK; Bonatumab; cytokine signaling transduction inhibitors4; and HDAC inhibitors.
Steroids as immunosuppressants are widely used to treat SARS in order to reduce the severity of inflammatory damage.
However, high doses of steroids do not benefit severe lung damage in patients with SARS and COVID-19.
On the contrary, they can cause serious side effects, in particular ischemic bone necrosis, which can seriously affect prognosis.
Nevertheless, it is still recommended that patients with critically ill COVID-19 be cautiously treated with low-dose corticosteroids.
At the time of writing, there has not yet been proven to be effective antiviral therapies.
However, redcivir — a nucleotide analog — has been found to be effective in intravenous dosing for one COVID-19 patient in the United States.
Redesivir is a novel antiviral drug developed by Gilead, originally used to treat diseases caused by Ebola virus and Marburg virus.
It was also later shown that redesivir may have inhibited other single-stranded RNA viruses, including MERS and SARS viruses.
For these reasons, Gilead has made the drug available to China for two clinical trials of people infected with SARS-CoV-2, the results of which are expected.
In addition, it has been recommended to use barectinib, α-interferon, lopinavir/ritonavir, and ribavirin as potential therapies for patients with acute respiratory symptoms.
Diarrhea, nausea, vomiting, liver damage, and other adverse reactions may occur after combined treatment.
The interactions between these treatments and other drugs used by patients should be carefully monitored.
Plasma and Antibodies Production in Rehabilitated Patients
The collection of blood from patients recovering from infectious diseases to the treatment of other patients with the same disease or to protect healthy individuals from the disease has a long history.
In fact, patients who have recovered usually contain relatively high levels of antibodies against pathogens in their blood.
Antibodies are an immunoglobulin (Ig) produced by B lymphocytes to fight pathogens and other foreign bodies, which can identify unique molecules in pathogens and neutralize them directly.
Based on this principle, plasma is collected from the blood of a group of patients who have recovered COVID-19, which is then injected into 10 critically ill patients.
Their symptoms improved within 24 hours, while increasing blood oxygen saturation with reduced inflammation and viral load.
However, before specific therapies can be developed, it is necessary to verify and clarify to propose methods that can be used on a large scale.
In addition, considering the treatment effect, some shortcomings related to plasma should be carefully considered.
For example, antibodies can overstimulate the immune response and cause cytokine release syndrome, and this toxic reaction can be life-threatening.
The concentration of antibodies in the blood is usually low, and there is a high demand for plasma for the treatment of critically ill patients.
It’s hard enough to develop and produce specific antibodies fast enough to fight a global epidemic.
Therefore, the more critical and practical way is to isolate B cells from recovered patients and identify the genetic code that encodes effective antibodies, or to screen effective antibodies for proteins necessary for viruses.
In this way, we can easily scale up the production of antibodies.
In China, the use of TCM to treat a variety of diseases has been around for thousands of years.
However, its therapeutic effect depends largely on the combination of multiple ingredients in the prescription, which varies according to TCM’s theory of disease diagnosis.
Most active ingredients are still unknown or unclear, as it is difficult to extract and verify such ingredients or their best combinations.
At present, due to the lack of effective and specific therapies for COVID-19, TCM has become one of the main alternative treatments for patients with mild to moderate symptoms or who have recovered from the severe stage.
For example, it has been found that detoxification capsules and even flower-clearing capsules are effective in COVID-19 treatment.
In several provinces of China, 87% of patients treated with TCM, the highest cure rates observed in patients with COVID-19 were observed, including Gansu (63.7%), Ningxia (50%) and Hunan (50%), while only about 30% of COVID-19 patients in Hubei Province used TCM treatment with the lowest cure rate (13%).
However, this is only a rough comparison, as there should also be many other influencing factors included in the assessment, such as the number and severity of patients.
On February 18, 2020, Zhang and his colleagues published a study comparing Western medicine (WM) in combination with WM and TCM alone.
They found that the WM+TCM group’s body temperature recovery, disappearance of symptoms, and hospitalization were significantly shorter than those of a simple WM group.
What’s most impressive is that the rate of symptomaging in the WM+TCM group (from light to heavy) was significantly lower than that of the simple WM group (7.4% vs. 46.2%), and the mortality rate in the WM+TCM group was also lower than that in the simple WM group (8.8% versus 39%).
Nonetheless, TCM’s effectiveness and safety will still require higher-quality controlled trials, a wider range, and more centers to confirm.
Characterizing the mechanism of action of TCM therapy or its combinations and, if possible, elucidating its active ingredients will also attract attention.
The majority of patients suspected or diagnosed with COVID-19 will develop great fear from this highly contagious or even fatal disease, and those who are isolated will also feel distress, loneliness and anger.
In addition, symptoms of infection, such as fever, lack of oxygen and coughs, as well as treatments for adverse reactions, such as insomnia caused by corticosteroids, may increase anxiety and psychological distress.
A range of psychological responses have been reported in the early stages of the SARS outbreak, including persistent depression, anxiety, panic attacks, psychomotor excitability, psychotic symptoms, delirium, and even suicide.
As part of a public health response to the COVID-19 outbreak, mandatory close contact tracing and isolation can make people more anxious and guilty about the effects of contagion, isolation and stigma on their families and friends.
Mental health care should therefore be provided to COVID-19 patients, suspected cases, close contacts, and the public in need.
Psychological support should include the establishment of a multidisciplinary mental health team, clear communication through regular and accurate updating of SARS-CoV-2 outbreak information and treatment plans, the use of professional electronic devices and applications to avoid close contact.
Effective vaccines are essential for interrupting the chain of transmission from animal hosts and infected patients to susceptible hosts, and are often used as a complement to antiviral treatments when it comes to controlling outbreaks caused by emerging viruses.
Efforts have been made to develop S-protein-based vaccines to produce long-term and effective neutralizing antibodies and/or protective immunity against SARS-CoV.
The live attenuated vaccine has been evaluated in animal models of SARS.
However, prior to the initiation of clinical studies, it has not been determined that these vaccine candidates are effective in the body in older adults and lethal attack models, as well as their protective effects against zoonotic viral infections.
That’s probably because SARS disappeared 17 years ago and no new cases have been reported since.
In contrast, due to the persistence of zoonotic sources in the affected areas, sporadic cases and aggregations of MERS persisted in the Middle East and spread to other regions.
Vaccination strategies for MERS have been developed through the use of inactivated viruses, DNA plasmids, viral vectors, nanoparticles, virus-like particles, and recombinant protein subunits, some of which have been evaluated in animal models.
The development of a safe and effective vaccine against SARS-CoV-2 for non-immunized individuals is an urgent and critical task in controlling current outbreaks.
However, it is challenging to overcome this difficulty due to the length of time it takes to develop the vaccine (an average of 18 months) and the dynamic change of CoV.
COVID-19 is just beginning to demonstrate its complete clinical process in thousands of patients.
In most cases, patients can gradually recover without sequelae.
However, similar to SARS and MERS, people with severe COVID-19 have high rates of morbidity and mortality.
Therefore, the establishment of prognostic models of the disease is critical for health care settings to prioritize services, especially in areas with limited resources.
Based on clinical studies reported so far, the following factors may affect prognosis in COVID-19 patients or are related to prognosis (Table 33):
Age: Age is the most important factor that influences the prognosis of SARS, as well as COVID-19.
As mentioned earlier, in a study that included 8,866 cases, COVID-19 mainly occurred in the 30-65 age group, of which 47.7 percent of the patients were older than 50.
Patients who need intensive care are more likely to have underlying comorbidities and complications, and are significantly older than those who don’t require intensive care (mediatric age 66 versus 51), suggesting a prognosis factor in COVID-19 patient outcomes.
Gender: As mentioned earlier, there are more men than women infected with SARS-CoV-2 (0.31/100,000 versus 0.27 per 100,000).
Combinations and Complications: COVID-19 patients in intensive care are more likely to experience acute heart damage and arrhythmias.
Cardiac events are also the leading cause of death in patients with SARS.
SARS-CoV-2 can also be combined with ACE2-positive bile duct cells, which can cause abnormal liver function in patients with COVID-19.
It’s important to note that age is closely related to underlying diseases and can affect each other.
Abnormal results from laboratory tests: Levels of C-reactive protein (CRP) in the blood reflect the severity of inflammation or tissue damage, and have been seen as potential prognostic factors for disease, therapeutic response, and eventual recovery.
It has also been suggested that there is a correlation between CRP levels and the severity of COVID-19, as well as prognosis.
In addition, elevated lactic acid dehydrogenase (LDH), aspartate aminotransferase (AST), alanine aminotransferase (ALT), and creatine kinase (CK) may also help predict outcomes.
These enzymes are widely expressed in multiple organs, especially in the heart and liver, and are released during tissue damage.
As a result, they are traditional markers of heart or liver dysfunction.
Main clinical symptoms: Chest imaging tests and the temporal progression of clinical symptoms should be considered in conjunction with other issues to predict outcomes and complications of COVID-19.
Steroid use: As mentioned earlier, steroids are immunosuppressants and are often used as adjuvant treatments for infectious diseases in order to reduce the severity of inflammatory damage.
Since high-dose corticosteroids are widely used in patients with heavy SARS, many survivors suffer from ischemic bone necrosis, resulting in lifelong disability and poorer quality of life.
Therefore, if necessary, steroids should be used in small doses and for a short period of time in COVID-19 patients.
Psychiatric stress: As mentioned earlier, during the COVID-19 outburst, many patients are subjected to extreme stress because they often suffer from prolonged isolation and extreme uncertainty, as well as witnessing the deaths of relatives and patients.
Psychological counseling and long-term support must be provided to help these patients get rid of stress and return to normal life.
Based on demographic studies to date, the epidemiologic characteristics of COVID-19 appear to be different from those of SARS.
In addition to replication in the lower respiratory tract, SARS-CoV-2 can also be effectively replicated in the upper respiratory tract and cause mild or asymptomatic symptoms in the early stages of infection, similar to other CoVs that cause common colds.
Therefore, infected people will produce a large number of viruses in the course of daily activities in the early onset or incubation period, which will bring great difficulties to the control of the epidemic.
However, the spread of SARS-CoV is believed to occur when the patient is seriously ill, and most transmission does not occur at an early stage.
As a result, current COVID-19 outbreaks are more severe and difficult to control compared to SARS.
China is currently making huge efforts, including a blockade of Wuhan and surrounding cities, as well as continuous isolation of almost all people, with a view to disrupting the spread of SARS-CoV-2.
Although these measures have caused serious damage to China's economy and others, the number of new cases is decreasing, indicating that the pace of development of the epidemic is slowing down.
The most optimistic estimate is that this outbreak will end in March, and the decline phase will last for 3-4 months.
However, some experts are not so optimistic.
Paul Hunter et al. estimated that COVID-19 is significantly more contagious than SARS and won’t end in 2020.
Ira Longini and others have developed models that predict the consequences of the epidemic, arguing that SARS-CoV-2 could infect two-thirds of the world’s population.
A team in Canada reported that patients who recovered and were discharged two weeks ago detected SARS-CoV-2 in both nasal and pharyngeal swabs, indicating that the newly discovered virus could become a cyclical epidemic similar to influenza.
However, as the number of new cases has dwindled, there have been encouraging signs in China that the current strategy may already be working.
Ebola has been predicted to cause 1 million cases and 500,000 deaths.
However, through strict isolation, the disease was eventually brought under control.
Similar to SARS-CoV, SARS-CoV-2 may become less infectious and eventually disappear or become less pathogenic viruses to coexist with the human body.
A comparison of COVID-19 outbreaks with SARS and MERS is provided below (Fig.
SARS-CoV-2 is highly contagious and can be transmitted by coughing or sneezing, and may even be transmitted through direct contact with items contaminated with the virus.
This virus has also been found in feces, which increases the likelihood of fecal oral transmission.
In a recent study involving 138 cases, 41 per cent of cases may have been caused by in-hospital infections, including 17 patients who had previously suffered from other diseases and 40 healthcare professionals.
Therefore, more stringent precautions should be taken to protect the population, especially medical staff, social workers, family members, co-workers, and even spectators who have had contact with patients or infected people.
The first line of defense that can be used to reduce the risk of infection is the wearing of masks; the use of surgical masks and N95 dust masks (1860s series) can help control the spread of the virus.
Surgical masks can prevent droplets from potentially infected people from spreading through the air or adhesing to the surface of items that may be passed on to others.
However, only the N95 (1860s series) masks can prevent inhalation of viruses as small as 10 to 80 nm, and only 5% of viruses can fully penetrate; SARS-CoV-2 is similar in size to SARS-CoV, both about 85 nm.
Because virus particles can even penetrate five overlapping surgical masks, health care workers who come into direct contact with patients must wear an N95 (1860s series) mask instead of a surgical mask.
In addition to masks, medical staff should also wear isolation clothing to further reduce contact with the virus.
The virus can also be transmitted through the eyes.
On January 22, 2020, a doctor contracted SARS-CoV-2 while wearing an N95 mask; the virus may have entered its body through inflamed eyes.
Therefore, medical staff should also wear transparent masks or goggles when facing patients.
For the public affected by the epidemic or potentially affected by the epidemic, it is strongly recommended that everyone wash their hands more often than usual with sterilized soap, try to stay indoor self-quarantine, and limit contact with potentially infected people.
Three feet is believed to be the right distance for people to stay away from patients.
These measures are an effective way to reduce the risk of infection and prevent the spread of the virus.
Although SARS-CoV-2 entered the human world as a new type of virus, based on deep memories of the 2003 SARS outbreak, its highly homologousity with SARS-CoV reported on January 7, 2020, was supposed to cause high alarm in China.
However, until January 19, 2020, the director of the Wuhan Center for Disease Control and Prevention was still appeasing the public, saying that this new virus is not contagious, the possibility of human transmission is limited, and that the epidemic can be prevented and controlled.
This news obviously relaxed the public's vigilance, and the Spring Festival is approaching, and the key node to contain the epidemic in Wuhan was thus missed.
China's disease control agencies are likely to learn this painful lesson and make significant improvements in the future.
For example, these institutions should (1) be more cautious when making announcements, because each word is critical to citizens and can change their attitudes and decisions; (2) be more sensitive and responsive to abnormal information from hospitals, rather than waiting for formal reports from doctors or officials; (c) more tightly control potential outbreaks at an early stage, rather than trying to appease the public; and (4) conduct more frequent targeted and effective exercises to raise public awareness of epidemics and regularly test and improve social response systems.
The COVID-19 outbreak caused by the new virus SARS-CoV-2 began at the end of December 2019.
In less than two months, it has spread across China and nearly 50 other countries around the world.
Because the virus is very similar to SARS-CoV, and the symptoms between COVID-19 and SARS are similar, the COVID-19 outbreak looks like a recurrence of SARS.
However, there are some significant differences between COVID-19 and SARS, which are critical to controlling outbreaks and treating patients.
COVID-19 affects more seniors than young people, men more than women, and the severity and mortality of older patients are also higher than that of younger patients.
Mortality rates for SARS are higher than COVID-19 (10.91% vs. 1.44%).
Even if there are no symptoms, COVID-19 patients still transmit the virus, and patients with SARS usually transmit the virus when they are in serious condition, which makes it much more difficult to control COVID-19 transmission than SARS.
This partly explains why the spread of SARS-CoV-2 is much faster and more widespread than SARS-CoV.
In some COVID-19 patients, routine RNA tests for SARS-CoV-2 may be negative.
On the other hand, cured patients may turn the sun again.
These findings greatly increase the risk of virus transmission.
Considering that studies of COVID-19 are so rapid, the following key issues remain to be addressed:
Where does SARS-CoV-2 come from?
Despite the discovery of 96 percent of genetic homology between SARS-CoV-2 and two bat-like COVs, we still can’t conclude that SARS-CoV-2 comes from bats.
Which animal spreads the virus from its primordial host—supposed as a bat—to an intermediate host to a human?
If we don't know the answers to questions 1 and 2, we can't effectively cut off the route of transmission, and the outbreak can recur at any time.
Although molecular models and biochemical analyses have shown that SARS-CoV-2 binds to ACE2, how exactly does the virus enter airway cells and cause subsequent pathological changes?
Does the virus also bind cells expressing ACE2 in other organs?
If there is no clear answer to these questions, we will not be able to achieve rapid and accurate diagnosis and effective treatment.
How long will this epidemic last?
How does the virus evolve during human-to-human transmission?
Is it going to be a global pandemic, disappearing like SARS, or will it recur like the flu on a regular basis?
Finding answers to the above and many other questions is crucial, but it can take longer.
However, regardless of the cost, we have no choice but to stop the epidemic as soon as possible and bring our lives back to normal.
The Origins of Zoonotic Diseases of Human Coronaviruses
For thousands of years, mutants and resilience have been driving the co-evolution of Coronaviruses (CoVs) and their hosts, including humans.
Prior to 2003, it was known that there were two types of human coronavirus (HCoV) that can cause minor illnesses, such as the common cold.
Severe Acute Respiratory Syndrome (SARS) and Middle East Respiratory Syndrome (MERS) outburst the situation, revealing the destructiveness and lethality of HCoV infections.
SARS-CoV-2, which appeared in central China at the end of 2019, once again brought CoV into the spotlight, surprisingly, it was highly transmissible compared to its sister virus, SARS-CoV.
HCoV infection is a zoonotic disease, so it will be helpful to learn about the origin of HCoV’s zoonotic disease.
Most HCoVs are derived from bats and are non-pathogenic.
Some of HCoV’s intermediate storage hosts are also known.
The identification of animal hosts has direct significance for the prevention of human diseases.
Studying the interactions of CoV hosts in animals may also give us a deeper understanding of the pathogenesis of human CoVs.
In this review, we provide an overview of the seven HCoV’s existing knowledge, highlighting their history of discovery, the origin of zoonosis, and interspecies transmission.
It’s important that we compare and control different HCoVs from the perspective of viral evolution and genome recombination.
In this context, we discussed the current 2019 coronavirus disease (COVID-19).
In addition, this article focuses on the requirements for the successful completion of host conversion and the impact of viral evolution on the severity of the disease.
CoV belongs to the Coronavirus Section and consists of a set of just single-stranded RNA viruses with envelopes.
These viruses have the largest genomes of RNA viruses, containing between 2.6 and 32,000 bases, known as "coronavirus" (CoV) because they are called "coronaviruses" under an electron microscope.
Structurally speaking, CoV has a non-segmented genome that shares similar tissues.
About two-thirds of the genome contains two large overlapping open reading boxes (ORF1a and ORF1b), which are translated into pp1a and pp1ab-replicating enzyme polyproteins.
These polyproteins are further processed to produce 16 non-structural proteins, known as nsp1 to 16.
The rest of the genome contains ORFs of structural proteins, including spikes (s), envelopes (E), membranes (M), and nucleoproteins (N).
Many lineage-specific auxiliary proteins are also encoded by CoV in different lineages.
Depending on the protein sequence, CoV is divided into four genuses (α-CoV, β-CoV, γ-CoV, and δ-CoV), of which β-CoV contains most HCoV and is subdivided into four lineages (A, B, C, and D).
Systematic evidence shows that bats and rodents are the genetic sources for most α-CoV and β-CoV, while birds are the primary hosts of γ-CoV and δ-CoV.
For thousands of years, CoV has been crossing the species barrier, some of which have become important human pathogens.
So far, there are seven species of coronavirus (HCoV) known to infect people.
HCoV-229E and HCoV-NL63 are α-CoV.
The other five types of β-CoV include HCoV-OC43, HCoV-HKU1, Severe Acute Respiratory Syndrome Coronavirus (SARS-CoV), Middle East Respiratory Syndrome Coronavirus (MERS-CoV), and SARS-CoV-2.
HCoV-229E, HCoV-OC43, HCoV-HKU1 and HCoV-NL63 usually cause mild symptoms, such as the common cold and/or diarrhea.
In contrast, SARS-CoV, MERS-CoV, and newly discovered SARS-CoV-2 are highly pathogenic, resulting in severe lower respiratory infections in relatively many patients, and are more likely to be developed into Acute Respiratory Distress Syndrome (ARDS) and extrapulmonary manifestations.
In the mid-1960s, the first strain of HCoV-229E B814 was isolated from nasal secretions in common cold patients.
Since then, humans have accumulated more knowledge through extensive research into the HCoV-229E and HCoV-OC43 viruses, which can cause self-limiting symptoms.
In fact, prior to the SARS outbreak, the concept of infection with HCoV, often harmless, had been widely accepted.
The SARS outbreak in 2003 was one of the most devastating events in current history, infecting more than 8,000 people, with a crossy fatality rate of about 10 percent.
Ten years later, after the Middle East Respiratory Syndrome (MERS) outbreak, the epidemic continued to spread in the Arabian Peninsula and spread sporadically to other parts of the world.
The 2019 new HCoV virus (2019-nCoV, later renamed SARS-CoV-2) is a persistently circulating pathogen of the 2019 Coronavirus Disease (COVID-19), having captured more than 3,120 lives and infected more than 91,000 people as of March 3, 2020.
Alarms have sounded, and the world must be prepared for an upcoming SARS-CoV-2 pandemic.
All seven HCoVs have zoonotic origins from bats, mice, or domesticated animals.
There’s multiple evidence that the evolution of all HCoV originated in bats, where viruses are well-adapted and pathogenic, but exhibit great genetic diversity.
COVID-19 has brought great medical, scientific, social and ethical challenges to China and the world.
Tracing HCoV’s zoonosis origins provides a framework for understanding the natural history, drivers, and limitations of viral species jumping.
In addition, this will guide or promote the search for SARS-CoV-2 storage hosts, intermediate hosts, and expanded animal hosts, which will have important implications for the prevention of future viral spills.
In this review, we provide an overview of HCoV’s zoonotic origins, interspecies transmission, and pathogenesis.
In particular, we highlight and explore a common topic, namely that HCoV’s parent viruses are usually not pathogenic in their natural storage hosts, but are pathogenic after cross-species transmission to new hosts.
In addition, we reviewed the evolution of HCoV, in which the increase in propagatability is often accompanied by a decrease in pathogenicity.
In this context, we also discussed the outcomes of the ongoing SARS-CoV-2 outbreak.
Since the end of the 1930s, animal CoV has been widely known.
Prior to the first separation of the HCoV-229E strain B814 from nasal secretions of common cold patients, the doctors had isolated different CoVs in a variety of infected animals, including turkeys, mice, cows, pigs, cats, and dogs.
In the past few decades, seven types of HCoV have been discovered.
A chronological summary of HCoV’s discovered history (Table 1) provides us with a wealth of information and guidance.
In 1966, the first HCoV-229E strain was isolated from the respiratory tract of patients with upper respiratory tract infections and subsequently adapted for growth in the WI-38 pulmonary cell line.
Patients infected with HCoV-229E have common cold symptoms such as headaches, sneezing, discomfort, sore throat, etc. 10 to 20% of patients have fever, cough and other symptoms.
In the second half of 1967, HCoV-OC43 was separated from the organ culture of the mouse brain and from subsequent successive generations.
The clinical characteristics of HCoV-OC43 infection are similar to HCoV-229E infection, and are not very different from those of other respiratory pathogens, such as influenza A and rhinovirus.
The HCoV-229E and HCoV-OC43 are distributed around the world, mainly in temperate winters.
Usually, the incubation period of these two viruses is less than one week, after which the onset is about 2 weeks.
A study of human volunteers showed that healthy individuals infected with HCoV-229E developed a mild common cold.
Only only a small number of immunocompromised patients exhibited severe lower respiratory tract infections.
SARS, also known as "atypical pneumonia", is the first well-documented pandemic caused by HCoV in human history, and its pathogen is SARS-CoV, the 3rd HCoV discovered.
The first case of SARS can be traced back to Guangdong Province, China, at the end of 2002.
The SARS outbreak resulted in 8,096 reported cases and 774 deaths, spreading to many countries and continents.
With the exception of super-spreaders, it is estimated that each case may result in about 2 secondary cases, with an incubation period of 4 to 7 days and a peak viral load on the 10th day of onset.
Patients infected with SARS-CoV initially showed muscle pain, headaches, fever, discomfort and chills, followed by late symptoms such as difficulty breathing, coughing, and respiratory distress.
Decreased lymphocytes, abnormal hepatic function checks, and increased creatine kinase are common laboratory tests for SARS.
In addition, diffuse alveolar damage, epithelial proliferation, and increased macrophages were observed in patients with SARS.
About 20-30% of patients then require intensive care and mechanical ventilation.
In these severe patients, in addition to the lower respiratory tract, multiple organs, including the gastrointestinal tract, liver and kidneys, may also be infected, and are often accompanied by cytokine storms, which can be fatal in patients with low immune function.
The virus was first isolated from an open pulmonary biopsy from a relative of a patient at the source of travel from Guangzhou to Hong Kong.
Since then, people have been working on HCoV research.
HCoV-NL63 separated from a 7-month-old baby in the Netherlands at the end of 2004.
It was initially discovered that the virus was circulating in young children, the elderly, and immunocompromised patients with respiratory diseases.
In diseases caused by HCoV-NL63, rhinitis, conjunctivitis, fever and bronchitis are common.
Another independent study showed that the same virus was isolated from nasal specimens of an eight-month-old boy with pneumonia in the Netherlands.
Although the virus was found in the Netherlands, it is actually distributed across the globe.
It is estimated that HCoV-NL63 accounts for about 4.7% of common respiratory diseases, and its incidence peaks in early summer, spring and winter.
HCoV-NL63 is associated with obstructive laryngitis (also known as grumb laryngitis).
In the same year, HCoV-HKU1 was separated from a 71-year-old man who was hospitalized in Hong Kong for pneumonia and bronchitis.
In addition to community-acquired pneumonia and bronchitis, HCoV-HKU1 has also been reported in relation to acute asthma attacks.
Similar to HCoV-NL63, HCoV-229E and HCoV-OC43, HCoV-HKU1 has been found worldwide to cause mild respiratory diseases.
All of these four community acquired HCoVs have been well adapted to humans and are often less likely to have mutations that cause highly pathopathic diseases. There have also been accidents, and for unexplained reasons, such as HCoV-NL63's more virulent rare subtype, which has recently been reported to cause severe lower respiratory tract infections in China.
Typically, when these HCoVs acquire the ability to spread and survive effectively in humans, their toxicity or pathogenicity is also reduced.
MERS-CoV was first isolated from the lungs of a 60-year-old patient with acute pneumonia and kidney failure in Saudi Arabia in 2012.
Although the majority of laboratory-confirmed cases are from the Middle East, imported cases have been reported in various European countries and Tunisia, with occasional secondary transmission to close contacts.
In 2015, secondary transmission broke out again in South Korea, with 186 confirmed cases.
MERS is clinically similar to that of SARS and is characterized by progressive acute pneumonia.
Unlike SARS, many MERS patients also experience acute kidney failure, a feature that has so far been unique to MERS in diseases caused by HCoV.
More than 30 percent of patients experienced gastrointestinal symptoms such as diarrhea and vomiting.
As of February 14, 2020, more than 2,500 laboratory-confirmed cases had been reported, with a case fatality rate of 34.4 percent, making MERS-CoV one of the most destructive viruses known to humans.
In mid-to-late December 2019, patients with concentrated pneumonia were found in Wuhan, Hubei Province, China, which was confirmed by a retrospective analysis to be associated with SARS-CoV-2 infection.
The World Health Organization has declared a continuing outbreak of lower respiratory tract infections caused by SARS-CoV-2 to be a public health emergency of international concern, naming the disease COVID-19.
As of March 3, 2020, 90,053 cases have been confirmed worldwide, with a crude case fatality rate of 3.4%.
It is worth noting that the case fatality rate is 4.2% in Hubei, China, and 1.2% outside Hubei.
Like SARS-CoV and MERS-CoV, SARS-CoV-2 can lead to severe respiratory infections, characterized by fever, cough, and breathing difficulties.
Some patients also have diarrhea.
Pneumonia is one of the most severe symptoms that can rapidly develop into acute respiratory distress syndrome.
Although SARS-CoV and SARS-CoV-2 are very similar due to 82% high nucleotide sequence homology, they are clustered into different branches in phylogenetic trees.
Compared with SARS-CoV and MERS-CoV, SARS-CoV-2 is significantly less pathogenic but more contagious.
SARS-CoV-2 asymptomatic infection has been reported, which may have accelerated the rapid spread of the virus around the world.
Comparing SARS-CoV-2 with six other HCoVs, we can find very interesting similarities and differences.
First of all, the incubation period and duration of HCoV disease are very similar.
At this point, SARS-CoV-2 is in line with the overall trend of six other HCoVs.
Secondly, the severity of COVID-19 symptoms is between SARS-CoV and four community acquired HCoVs (i.e. HCoV-229E, HCoV-OC43, HCoV-HKU1 and HCoV-NL63).
On the one hand, SARS-CoV-2 infection exhibits features that are more common in community-acquired HCoV infections, including non-specific, mild, and even asymptomatic manifestations.
On the other hand, a small number of severe cases of COVID-19 are also found in cases of SARS-CoV infection, albeit at a slightly lower rate.
Third, the spread of SARS-CoV-2 also shows interesting patterns of community acquisition HCoV and SARS-CoV.
On the one hand, SARS-CoV-2’s transmissibility is at least as high as community-acquired HCoV.
On the other hand, whether SARS-CoV-2’s transmissibility, like SARS-CoV and MERS-CoV, declines after human transmission, remains to be verified.
Finally, like other HCoVs, SARS-CoV-2 can be detected in stool samples.
At least in some cases, whether SARS-CoV-2’s fecal-oral transmission plays as important a role as in SARS-CoV remains to be clarified by future studies.
It’s also very important to observe whether SARS-CoV-2 is seasonal like community-acquired HCoV.
However, features such as SARS-CoV-2’s transmissibility, pathogenicity and continuous transmission from person to generation will have an important impact on the ultimate fate of the current COVID-19 outbreak.
All four community-acquired HCoVs that cause mild symptoms have adapted well to humans.
From another point of view, humans have also adapted well to these four HCoVs.
In other words, both sides may be survivors of the ancient HCoV pandemic.
HCoV, which causes serious diseases in humans, and people suffering from severe HCoV diseases are dead.
To do this, HCoVs must replicate enough in humans to accumulate adaptive mutations that combat host limiting factors.
In this sense, the longer the SARS-CoV-2 epidemic lasts, the more people are infected, and the more likely it is to fully adapt to humans.
If it adapts well, then its spread in humans will be difficult to stop through isolation or other infection control measures.
Over the years, these four community-acquired coronaviruses have spread in the population, resulting in people with normal immune function suffering from the common cold.
These viruses don’t need animal hosts.
In contrast, highly pathogenic SARS-CoV and MERS-CoV have not adapted well to humans and have not been able to spread continuously in humans.
They need to survive and thrive in zoonotic hosts, and may use one or more intermediate hosts and expand their hosts to find opportunities to spread to vulnerable human targets.
SARS-CoV-2 has characteristics similar to SARS-CoV/MERS-CoV and four community acquired HCoVs.
It’s as highly contagious as community-acquired HCoV, at least for now.
However, it is more pathogenic than community-acquired HCoV and is less pathogenic than SARS-CoV or MERS-CoV.
It remains to be seen whether it can be fully adapted to humans and circulated within humans without a host or intermediate animal host.
Before discussing the animal origins of HCoV, discussing the definition and characteristics of HCoV's evolutionary hosts, natural hosts, storage hosts, intermediate hosts, and expanding hosts has some guiding significance for us.
If an animal has a highly homologous ancestor at the level of nucleotide sequences, the animal will become an evolutionary host to HCoV.
Ancestoral viruses are usually highly adaptable and non-pathogenic in this host.
Similarly, the storage host will continue to carry HCoV for a long time.
In both cases, the host is naturally infected and is the natural host of HCoV or its progenitor virus.
In contrast, if HCoV has just resided in an intermediate host soon or immediately in the human body, then it will not be able to adapt well to the new host, and is usually pathogenic.
This intermediate host can act as a source of zoonose for human infection, and by allowing the virus to replicate briefly, and then spread it to humans in order to increase the scale of human infection, thus amplifying the role of the host.
If HCoV does not maintain its propagation within an intermediate host, a terminal infection may occur.
Conversely, HCoV can also adapt to intermediate hosts and even establish long-term locality.
In this case, the intermediate host becomes a natural storage host.
A retrospective analysis of epidemiological data found a history of contact between SARS-indexed cases and hunting animals.
Subsequent seroprevalence surveys showed higher levels of immunoglobulin G (IgG) against SARS-CoV in animal traders compared to the general population.
In the live animal market, civets (flower-noodle beavers) and a cat were first found to carry a SARS-CoV-like virus that is almost identical to the SARS-CoV virus.
There were no further SARS reports after removing all civets in the market, a fact that indirectly supported the above view.
However, the report said that farm civets, which are not exposed to live animal markets, tested negative for SARS-CoV, suggesting that civets may only be intermediate amplified hosts, rather than natural hosts of SARS-CoV.
It’s important to note that because 80 percent of the different animals on the market in Guangzhou have SARS-CoV antibodies, it’s impossible to rule out the possibility that a variety of small mammals may also act as an intermediate-expansion host for SARS-CoV.
All of these animals appear to be terminal hosts of SARS-CoV.
Subsequent studies of SARS-CoV natural animal hosts have found a closely related bat coronavirus, known as SARS-Rh-BatCoV HKU3 (SARSr-Rh-BatCoV HKU3), which survives in Chinese chrysanthemum bats.
These bats tested positive for SARS-CoV antibodies and SARSr-Rh-BatCoV HKU3’s genome sequences.
The virus and other bat coronaviruses have 88-92% nucleotide sequence homology with SARS-CoV.
These studies laid the foundation for the new concept that bats are hosts of emerging human pathogens.
In addition, the researchers identified a variety of SARS-like coronaviruses (SL-CoVs) from bats, but no virus can be isolated as a living virus other than a designated WIV1.
Human angiotensin-converting enzyme 2 (ACE2) is a known receptor for SARS-CoV.
Experiments have shown that WIV1 extracted from bat stool samples uses bats, coves, and human ACE2 as their receptors for entering the cells.
It is interesting to note that Serum in patients with SARS recovery was able to neutralize WIV1.
WIV1, so far, is the closest ancestor to the bat SARS-CoV, with 95 percent nucleotide sequence homology.
Although these two viruses are highly homologous, WIV1 is generally believed to be not a direct parent virus of SARS-CoV, and bats are not a direct reservoir host of SARS-CoV.
The phylogenetic analysis classifies MERS-CoV into the same group as bat CoV-HKU4 and bat CoV-HKU5.
Bats CoV-HKU4 and MERS-CoV invade the virus using the same host receptor dipeptide peptide enzyme4 (DPP4).
MERS-CoV’s RNA-dependent RNA polymerase sequences are phylogenically closer to the corresponding RNA in bats from Europe and Africa, β-CoV.
So far, living MERS-CoV has not been found in wild bats.
MERS-CoV has only 87 percent nucleotide sequence homology with its close-in bat CoV-HKU25.
As a result, bats may not be a direct storage host for MERS-CoV.
On the other hand, studies in the Middle East have shown that seropositive serotypes for MERS-CoV-specific neutralizing antibodies are the same as camels originating in the Middle East in several African countries.
Live MERS-CoV isolated from the snal swabs of a syrup camel is the same virus found in the human body, which further indicates that the camel is the true host of MERS-CoV storage.
It should also be noted that in camels infected with MERS-CoV experimentally, the general symptoms are mild, but a large amount of virus shedding is observed.
It is worth noting that infected camels not only transmit the virus through the respiratory tract, but also transmit the virus through the fecal oral route, which is also the main way for bats to spread the virus.
However, the problem persists, as many MERS confirmed cases do not have a history of contact with camels prior to the onset of symptoms, which may be attributed to human-to-human or unknown routes of transmission, including unrecognized animal species carrying MERS-CoV.
SARS-CoV-2 has 96.2 percent nucleotide homologity in the bat CoV RaTG13, which is separated from the chrysanthemum bat.
As in the case of SARS-CoV and MERS-CoV, the sequence difference between SARS-CoV-2 and RaTG13 is too large to determine the parent relationship.
In other words, unless almost the same bat coronavirus is found in the future, bats may not be a direct storage host for SARS-CoV-2.
Presumably, SARS-CoV-2’s intermediate animal host should be sold and slaughtered among wild animal species sold and slaughtered at the South China Seafood Wholesale Market, and many of the initial cases of COVID-19 are associated with it, suggesting the possibility of animal-to-human transmission.
Several recent studies based on macrogenome sequencing have shown that an endangered small mammal, known as pangolins (Malay pangolins), may also be carrying the ancestor β-CoV associated with SARS-CoV-2.
These new pangolin-coronavirus genomes are homologous with SARS-CoV-2 nucleotide sequences of 85-92%.
However, they are also closely related to RaTG13, which is about 90% homology at the nucleotide sequence level.
In phylogenetic trees, they are classified as two SARS-CoV-2-like virus-like strains, one of which has a more similar receptor binding domain (RBD) to SARS-CoV-2 and has 97.4 percent amino acid sequence homology.
In stark contrast, RBD for SARS-CoV-2 and RaTG13 differs even more, although the whole genome has higher sequence homology.
An earlier study of diseased pangolins also reported the detection of viral overlapping groups from lung samples, and the results were similar to SARS-CoV-2.
The study used different assembly methods and manual processing to obtain some of the genome sequence of about 86.3% of the whole genome of the virus.
We can’t rule out the possibility that pangolins are one of SARS-CoV-2’s intermediate animal hosts.
However, due to differences in β-CoV sequences associated with SARS-CoV-2, SARS-CoV-2, there is currently no evidence that SARS-CoV-2 originates directly from pangolins.
In addition, the distance between SARS-CoV-2 and RaTG13 is even shorter than the distance between SARS-CoV-2 and β-CoV associated with pangolin SARS-CoV-2.
The evolutionary path of SARS-CoV-2 in bats, pangolins and other mammals remains to be determined.
Although the highest sequence homology was found in the RBD between SARS-CoV-2 and pangolins, SARS-CoV-2-associated β-CoV, SARS-CoV-2, and RaTG13 had the highest whole-genome homology.
It can be highly speculated that the highly similarity between SARS-CoV-related β-CoV and SARS-CoV-2 RBD is caused by selectively mediated convergent evolution.
The opposite view is that β-CoV and RaTG13 associated with pangolin SARS-CoV-2 reorganize in a third wildlife species.
As the driving force of evolution, recombination is widespread in β-CoV.
With regard to the direct zoonotic origin of SARS-CoV-2, it is currently inconclusive.
In addition to the highly pathogenic HCoV, the researchers also studied the zoonotic origins of HCoV-229E, HCoV-OC43, HCoV-NL63 and HCoV-HKU1.
Systematic evidence suggests that both HCoV-NL63 and HCoV-229E may have originated from bat coronavirus, while progenitory viruses of HCoV-OC43 and HCoV-HKU1 have also been found in rodents.
According to the report, bat coronaviruses found in North American trichromatic bats called ARCoV.2 (Appalachian Ridge CoV) are strongly associated with HCoV-NL63.
HCoV-229E, on the other hand, is genetically linked to another bat coronavirus found in Ghana, known as Hipposideros/GhanaKwam/19/2008, while camel animals are also suspected of being their intermediate hosts.
For the sake of clarity, Figures 1 and Table 2 summarize existing knowledge about known animal origins of HCoV.
The phylogenetic analysis provides evidence for the interspecial propagation events of HCoV throughout history.
Around 1890, when HCoV-OC43 infected humans across species from domestic animals, there was a pandemic of respiratory infectious diseases.
It is not yet clear that HCoV-229E's interspecial transmission history is unclear.
Bat α-CoV, which is closely related to HCoV-229E, has been discovered.
There is an alpaca α-CoV between them.
There are several pieces of evidence that support the direct transmission of the virus from bats to humans.
First of all, humans, rather than alpacas, may come into contact with bats in a common niche.
On the contrary, humans are in close contact with alpacas.
Secondly, bat α-CoV associated with HCoV-229E has diversity and non-pathogenicity in bats, while alpaca α-CoV causes outbreaks of respiratory diseases in infected animals.
In the end, no alpaca α-CoV was found in wild animals.
Therefore, it cannot be ruled out the possibility that alpacas will receive HCoV-229E-related α-CoV from humans.
In fact, bats are a direct source of human-pathogenic viruses, including rabies virus, Ebola virus, nipa virus, and Hendra virus.
Therefore, it is not surprising that bats spread the HCoV-229E directly to humans.
Or, while bat α-CoV acts as a gene pool of HCoV-229Es, alpacas and sedimum camels are likely to transmit the virus to an intermediate host to humans, which is exactly the case with MERS-CoV.
MERS-CoV is a good illustration of transmission from bats to deypthical camels as well as from sedrums to human species.
The evolutionary origins of Bat’s MERS-CoV have been known in their initial identification, and subsequent findings reinforce this argument.
Obviously, bats provide a rich reservoir of virus types for interspecial exchange and interspecial transmission of genetic fragments.
Long and dense groups, close social interactions, and strong flight capabilities are all favorable conditions for bats to become ideal “virus spreaders.”
MERS-CoV, on the other hand, has been in the body for several decades.
It adapts well to these camels, which have also changed from intermediate hosts to stable natural storage hosts.
MERS-CoV causes very mild disease in these animals and maintains a relatively low mutation rate.
Its sporadic propagation to humans is an accident, and due to its inability to spread continuously, humans are still terminal hosts of MERS-CoV.
Compared with the role of camels in MERS-CoV propagation, the role of pangolins in SARS-CoV-2 propagation, if any, is different.
In particular, pangolin β-CoV is highly pathogenic in pangolins.
They may be terminal hosts of SARS-CoV-2-associated β-CoV, similar to civets in SARS-CoV.
In future studies, it is necessary to identify or rule out several possibilities for SARS-CoV-2 transmission from animals to human species.
First of all, bats may be the reservoir host of SARS-CoV-2, which is almost identical to SARS-CoV-2-related viruses.
Humans may share ecological niches with bats through slaughter or coal mining.
Secondly, pangolins may be one of the intermediate expanding hosts of new intrusions of SARS-CoV-2-related viruses.
Humans are infected with the virus by slaughtering and eating game.
Many mammals, including domestic animals, may be susceptible to SARS-CoV-2.
There is a need for antibody investigations of livestock and wild animals.
Third, as mentioned above, SARS-CoV-2 may reorganize and adapt in a third species that has come into contact with both bats and pangolins.
Research on the animal origins of SARS-CoV-2 continues.
In addition to different types of animal hosts, the three main factors of the virus have also played an important role in promoting coronavirus across interspecial barriers.
First of all, they have a higher rate of mutations in RNA replication.
Compared with other single-stranded RNA viruses, the mutant rate of the coronavirus is estimated to be “medium” to “high,” with an average replacement rate of about 10 to 4 per site per site2 per year, depending on the adaptive phase of the new host of the coronavirus.
Coronavirus has proofreading active ribonucleic acid exacease, and removing it will lead to very high variability and attenuation, and even cannot survive.
Interestingly, Remdesivir is known to inhibit coronavirus replication by inhibiting this ribonucleic acid exocithase and RNA-dependent RNA polymerase.
Redesivir is one of the most promising anti-SARS-CoV-2 drugs to be tested in clinical trials.
However, the mutation rate of the coronavirus is about 1 million times higher than that of its host.
In addition, if the coronavirus does not adapt well to the host, its mutation rate is usually very high.
Compared to SARS-CoV, which has a high mutation rate, SARS-CoV-2 has a significantly lower mutation rate, indicating that it is more adaptable to humans.
It can be assumed that it has adapted to another host that is close to humans.
In addition to SARS-CoV-2, this also applies to MERS-CoV, which is well adapted to dromedary camels.
In theoretically speaking, large genetic drift may cause vaccines and antivirals against SARS-CoV-2 to quickly fail.
Secondly, the larger RNA genome in coronavirus has additional plasticity in the modification of genetic mutations and recombinations, thus increasing the possibility of synergistic evolution between species, which is conducive to the emergence of the new coronavirus when the conditions are suitable.
The large number of unique open reading boxes and protein functions encoded towards the end of the genome 3' provides evidence for this.
Third, coronaviruses randomly and frequently switch templates through a unique “replication selection” mechanism during RNA replication.
In hosts that serve as a hybrid container, chain exchange occurs frequently during CoV RNA transcription.
The highly homologous full length and subgenome RNA can recombinate to produce new coronaviruses.
Evidence of natural recombinant phylogenics has been found in both HCoV-HKU1 and HCoV-OC43, as well as animal coronaviruses such as bat SL-CoV and bat CoV-HKU9.
Virus-host interactions associated with propagation
In addition to the above three virus factors, the interaction of the virus with the host receptor is another key factor that affects interspecies transmission.
This article uses the reorganization of SARS-CoV as a typical example, which shows evidence of positive selection in interspecial propagation events.
Based on a comparative analysis of isolated strains of SARS-CoV between humans and civets, the researchers believe that SARS-CoV can be quickly adapted to different hosts, particularly through mutations in the S protein RBD.
Typically, RBD in the coronavirus S protein interacts with cell receptors and is rigorously screened by host antibody responses.
In SARS-CoV, RBD is located in the 318th to 510th amino acids of the S1 fragment, which binds to the human ACE2 and its co-receptors to allow the virus to enter.
SARS-CoV’s RBD recognizes ACE2 receptors in a variety of animals, including bats, civets, mice, and cats, allowing the virus to spread between species.
In fact, only six amino acid residues were observed in RBD that isolated different amino acid residues from human and civet virus strains, four of which were located in the receptor-binding sequence that interacted with the ACE2 receptor.
There are K479N and S487T mutations in the RBD of civeted SARS-CoV, which may increase the affinity of fibrous protein interactions with human ACE2 receptors.
In other words, the replacement of these two amino acids may be crucial for viruses to adapt to humans.
It is worth noting that SARS-CoV-2 has the same cellular receptor as SARS-CoV.
SARS-CoV-2 and SARS-CoV have a 30 percent difference in the S protein S1 unit, which means that the binding affinity of its S protein to human ACE2 may change.
Indeed, one study showed that this binding affinity was 10 to 20 times higher than the affinity between human ACE2 and SARS-CoV S proteins.
Determining the need for any other co-receptor in SARS-CoV-2 transmission would also make sense.
Interestingly, HCoV-NL63 is also combined with ACE2, but combined with different parts of S.
In addition, there are many other HCoV receptors, such as HCoV-229E’s aminopeptide enzyme N and HCoV-OC43’s 9-O-acetylate salivanic acid.
They may also explain why these coronaviruses successfully adapted to humans after spreading across species from animal hosts.
In addition to cell receptors, the results of interspecial propagation of HCoV are also influenced by other host-dependent and limiting factors.
The differences between these host proteins in humans and natural hosts such as bats, dromons, and rodents may constitute barriers to interspecies propagation.
HCoVs must deprive host-dependent factors and break host constraints in order to successfully achieve interspecial propagation.
In this regard, molecular determinants in this important area of virus-host interactions remain to be determined and characterized.
Unbiased whole-genomic screenings using state-of-the-art CRISPR techniques for host-dependent and restrictive factors of SARS-CoV-2 are likely to come to fruition.
The Emergence of the New HCoV: Back to Origin
The diversity of bat coronaviruses provides ample opportunities for the emergence of new HCoVs.
In this sense, bat coronavirus acts as a gene pool for HCoV.
In addition, rapid mutations and genetic recombination are also the drivers of HCoV evolution and two important steps in this process.
For example, the acquisition or loss of new protein-coding genes could revolutionize the phenotype of viruses.
In SARS-CoV auxiliary proteins, SARS-CoV-associated bat viruses have been isolated, but they have been found to encode different ORF8 proteins, so ORF8 is considered to be important for adaptation to humans.
At the beginning of the human epidemic, 29 nucleotide deletion features of SARS-CoV were found in isolated strains.
This absence divides ORF8 into ORF8a and ORF8b, and is considered an adaptive mutation that promotes host conversion.
In addition, SARS-CoV may have undergone recombination with the α- and γ-CoV lineages, in which a large number of smaller recombinant regions are found in RNA-dependent RNA polymerases.
Recombination sites are also found in nsp9, most nsp10, and some nsp14.
Similarly, studies have shown that the popularity of MERS-CoV has experienced recombinant events between different lineages, which occurred in Saudi Arabia’s symber camels.
In addition to SARS-CoV and MERS-CoV, recombinant events have also been observed in other HCoV, in which HCoV and other animal coronaviruses recombinate in their non-structural genes.
It should also be noted that artificial selection may cause unexpected changes in the viral genome, which is likely due to reducing the selective pressure exerted by the host immune system that the virus faces.
An example of these effects is that in the HCoV-229E prototype virus strain, the absence of dinucleotides led to the absence of full-long ORF4.
Although complete ORF4 can be observed in bats and camel viruses associated with HCoV-229E, alpaca α-CoV shows a single nucleotide insertion, which causes a migration mutation.
Last but not least, the evolution of the new HCoV is also driven by storage host selection pressures.
After bats are infected with the coronavirus, asymptomatic or only mild symptoms are detected, indicating that the coronavirus and bats adapt to each other.
From an anatomical and physiological point of view, bats seem to be well adapted to the coronavirus.
For example, defects in the activation of bat pro-inflammatory reactions effectively reduce pathological changes caused by the coronavirus.
In addition, due to the positive regulation of the inhibitory natural killer cell receptor NKG2/CD94, and low levels of molecular expression of the main histocompatibility complex, the natural killer cell activity of bats is inhibited.
In addition, high levels of active oxygen (ROS) produced by high metabolic activity in bats can inhibit coronavirus replication and can affect proofreading through ribonucleic acid exocytase, thus providing a highly pathogenic pressure when the virus strain enters a new host.
More pathogenic coronavirus strains may also have evolved through recombination, leading to the acquisition of new protein or protein signatures to suit the host.
As a result, it’s no accident that three new HCoVs have emerged in the past two decades.
Coronaviruses do not cause disease or cause mild symptoms in storage hosts such as bats and camels.
They make a lot of replication and don’t cause a strong host immune response.
That’s why there will be asymptomatic carriers and what causes cases of serious human infections.
The emergence of severe symptoms is mainly due to the excessive activation of the immune response and cytokine storms, so the stronger the immune response, the more severe the damage to the lungs.
By contrast, among asymptomatic carriers, the immune response has been decoupled from coronavirus replication.
The same strategies that dischain the immune response may play a positive role in anti-SARS-CoV-2 treatments.
Interferon reactions are particularly strong in bats.
Therefore, it should be beneficial to use type I interferon at least in the early stages of human SARS-CoV-2 infection.
In addition, there are defects in the activation of NLRP3 inflammatory bodies in bats.
Based on this reasoning, the use of MCC950 to suppress NLRP3 inflammatory bodies may be useful in the treatment of COVID-19.
The emergence of SARS-CoV-2 follows the general themes on which SARS-CoV and MERS-CoV arose.
Although bat β-CoV has been found to be 95% nucleotide homology with SARS-CoV, there is also a bat coronavirus with SARS-CoV-2 nucleotide homology of up to 96%.
Although civets and other animals in the market have been found to carry the same virus as SARS-CoV, it has not yet been determined that the direct intermediate host of SARS-CoV-2 has not yet been established.
Pangolin β-CoV, which is distinctly homologous with SARS-CoV-2, has now been found, suggesting that pangolins may have been one of the intermediate hosts, or that pangolin β-CoV may have contributed genetic fragments to the final version of SARS-CoV-2.
Despite questions, there is no evidence that SARS-CoV-2 was intentionally or unintentionally manufactured.
Due to the recent outbreak of SARS-CoV-2, the coronavirus has regained the spotlight.
The study of coronaviruses in bats and other animals has dramatically changed our understanding of the origins of HCoV zoonosis and the importance of animal hosts in human transmission.
There is substantial evidence that SARS-CoV, MERS-CoV, and SARS-CoV-2 originated in bats and were transmitted to humans through intermediate hosts.
Given that SARS-CoV infection stems from contact between humans and civets on the market, shutting down wet markets and killing civets can effectively end the SARS epidemic.
Similarly, in view of the discovery of multiple lineages of pangolin β-CoV, which is closely related to SARS-CoV-2, pangolins should be removed from the wet goods market to prevent the spread of zoonotic diseases.
However, it is still necessary to further study whether and how SARS-CoV-2 is transmitted to humans through pangolins and other mammals.
MERS-CoV, on the other hand, has been around for a long time in sedium camels.
These camels are an important means of transportation and the main source of meat, milk, leather and wool products for the locals.
They are widely distributed in the Middle East and Africa.
Therefore, it is not possible to follow the Chinese wildlife market to prevent the spread of SARS-CoV and SARS-CoV-2 and sacrifice all camels to control MERS.
In order to stop the recurrence of MERS, comprehensive measures should be taken in conjunction with other infection control measures to develop an effective anti-MERS-CoV vaccine for camels.
Since we are unable to eliminate these viruses, new genotypes may emerge, leading to outbreaks.
A variety of zoonotic coronaviruses circulate in the wild.
In particular, there is a high diversity of bat coronaviruses with zoonotic potential.
There are many opportunities for evolution and recombination of these zoonotic coronaviruses, leading to the emergence of new coronaviruses that are more transmissible and/or deadly in humans in the future.
In some parts of China, the culture of eating wild animals should be abandoned in order to reduce unnecessary contact between humans and animals.
After the severe trials of SARS, MERS and COVID-19, we should develop better prevention and response plans.
In fact, many viruses have been present on Earth for a long time.
They stay in their natural hosts until there is an opportunity to overflow.
Although bats have many characteristics conducive to the spread of the virus, if people are educated away from bats and other wild species, then human exposure to bats and other wild species will be greatly reduced.
In order to gain a better understanding of the ecology of the coronavirus and its natural hosts, it is necessary to conduct continuous monitoring of mammals, which will prove to be very useful for preventing the spread of animals to humans as well as future outbreaks.
All in all, the most effective way to prevent viral zoonoses is for humans to stay away from the natural host of zoonotic viruses.
There are still a number of shortcomings in the SARS-CoV-2 mystery of zoonotic origins.
First of all, it makes sense to examine the circumstances under which bats and pangolins can share the same niche if they transmit an ancient SARS-CoV-2 virus to pangolins.
Second, if bats play a more direct role in human transmission, then how humans come into contact with bats has yet to be determined.
Third, if a third mammal acts as a true intermediate host, it must be clear how it interacts with different species, including humans, bats, and pangolins.
Finally, because many mammals, including livestock, can be susceptible to SARS-CoV-2, surveillance and experimental infection studies should be conducted.
If it is bats, pangolins, or other mammals, it is expected that SARS-CoV-2 will be found in natural hosts in the future, or almost the same parental virus.
Continuing research in this area will shed light on the evolutionary pathways of SARS-CoV-2 in animals, which have important implications for the prevention and control of COVID-19 in humans.
It is necessary to update the diagnostic criteria for COVID-19 “suspected cases” and “confirmed cases”.
On February 6, 2020, our team released the 2019 Guidelines for Diagnosing and Diagnosing of New Coronavirus (2019-nCoV) Infections, through which we provide experience and reference for the global response to the epidemic.
However, the 2019 Coronavirus Disease (COVID-19) is a new type of disease, and based on ongoing research results and clinical practical experience, our awareness of the disease is gradually improving; as a result, diagnosis and treatment strategies are also being continuously updated.
In this letter, we responded to an opinion on the above-mentioned guidelines and provided the latest "Suspected Cases" and "Confirmed Cases" diagnostic criteria according to the latest COVID-19 Diagnosis and Treatment Program (7th Edition) issued by the National Health and Health Commission of the People's Republic of China.
In December 2019, the 2019 novel coronavirus (2019-nCoV) outbreak, which has now been officially named Coronavirus Disease (COVID-19), has been named severe acute respiratory syndrome type 2 coronavirus (SARS-CoV-2).
On March 11, 2020, the World Health Organization (WHO) listed COVID-19 as a global pandemic.
To combat SARS-CoV-2 infection, our team developed guidelines for quick advice and published them online in the Journal of Military Medical Research on February 06, 2020.
Since its release, it has attracted widespread attention.
However, please note that COVID-19 is a new type of disease, and based on ongoing research results and clinical practical experience, our awareness of the disease is gradually improving; as a result, diagnosis and treatment strategies are also being continuously updated.
For example, the National Health Commission of the People's Republic of China (http://www.nhc.gov.cn/) has released seven versions of the COVID-19 Diagnosis and Treatment Plan between January 16, 2020 and March 3, 2020, some of which have undergone substantial changes.
Now, our guidelines have received input from Zhou and others, who have come up with a simple scoring protocol based on their clinical experience.
Their work provides new evidence for our guidelines, as well as valuable references for this global pandemic.
We recognize and express our gratitude for the important work they have done.
However, their proposals will also need to be updated based on the latest COVID-19 Diagnosis Program (Trial Seventh Edition), as well as recent studies.
According to the seventh edition (March 3, 2020), the diagnosis of suspected cases needs to combine one epidemiological historical feature with two clinical manifestations for comprehensive analysis, and if there is no clear epidemiological history, three clinical manifestations need to be met:
Epidemiological history: (1) a history of travel or residence within 14 days of symptoms in and around Wuhan or other communities that have reported COVID-19 cases; (2) a history of contact with cases of SARS-CoV-2 (positive nucleic acid); (3) a history of contact with patients with febrile or respiratory symptoms from communities with COVID-19 in and around Wuhan within 14 days prior to the onset of symptoms; (ii) a history of contact with patients with fever or respiratory symptoms in a community with COVID-19.
Clinical manifestations: (1) fever and/or respiratory symptoms; (2) imaging characteristics of COVID-19 infection; and (3) normal or decreased total number of white blood cells in the early stage of onset, and decrease in lymphocyte counts.
Diagnosis of confirmed cases should be based on suspected cases and have any of the following etiological or serological evidence: (1) SARS-CoV-2’s real-time PCR tested positive; (ii) whole-genome sequencing of the virus showed highly homologousity with known novel coronaviruses; (3) specific IgM and IgG antibodies against SARS-CoV-2 in serum tests were positive; or SARS-CoV-2-specific IgG antibodies rose from negative to positive during the recovery period.
We can see that the second (January 18, 2020) and third (January 22, 2020) added real-time PCR nucleic acid testing in respiratory or blood specimens.
The fourth edition (January 27, 2020) and the fifth edition (February 8, 2020) added ethology testing of blood specimens; the seventh edition added serological evidence.
These modifications are based on the ongoing work of researchers studying the best nucleic acid testing kits to achieve a rapid diagnosis, as well as respiratory specimens, including blood collection, which increases the availability of different specimens and provides evidence that positive results from specific antibodies are included in the diagnostic criteria.
In addition, more and more pieces of evidence remind us of asymptomatic and asymptomatic patients
As a result, flowcharts for Zhou and others should be updated because they classified people without clinical symptoms as “low risk.”
In addition, the scoring system needs to be validated in further clinical practice and research.
In conclusion, we would like to show more direct evidence and call on readers to comment.
For the diagnosis of "suspected cases" and "confirmed cases", we recommend referring to and following the latest guidelines in the country where the case is located.
Our team will also promptly update our guidelines to help.
Bangladesh reports 5 new COVID-19 deaths, a single-day high
Yesterday, Bangladesh confirmed five new COVID-19 deaths on the same day.
This is the highest number of single-day deaths caused by the virus.
As of yesterday, among the records of cases of infection reported by the Bangladesh Epidemiology, Disease Control and Research (IEDCR), there were 114 confirmed cases and 33 were cured after rest at home.
There have been 17 cumulative deaths.
At an online news conference, IEDCR chief Dr Meerjady Sabrina Flora said four men and one woman were among those deaths.
According to Dr. Meerjady, there were two cases between the ages of 60 and older, two between the ages of 51 and 60, and one between the ages of 41-50.
She also said that two of those deaths were from Dhaka.
The World Health Organization (WHO) on March 11 declared COVID-19 a global pandemic (pandemic).
A hospital official revealed to local news outlet Anadolu Agency that one of the dead patients was Jalal Saifur Rahman, director of the Bangladesh Anti-Corruption Commission, who had been treated at Kuwait Maitree Hospital.
On Saturday, Bangladesh’s Minister of Road Transport and Bridge, Obaidul Quader, announced through an online video that public transport downtime would extend beyond the initial plan until this Saturday.
The suspension of public transportation began on March 26 and was originally scheduled to end on Saturday, April 4.
Basic supplies such as medical care, fuel and food are still transportable normally.
On March 8, of the first confirmed cases of COVID-19 recorded in Bangladesh, two returned from Italy, and one patient was the wife of one of the confirmed patients.
As of March 19, all three patients had recovered.
SARS-CoV-2 infects more than one million people worldwide
According to Johns Hopkins University, the total number of SARS-CoV-2 coronavirus infections worldwide exceeded 1 million on Thursday.
At least 52,000 deaths have been linked to a disease triggered by the coronavirus, COVID-19.
On the same day that the milestone occurred, Malawi confirmed the country's first case of coronavirus infection and the first coronavirus-related death in Zambia.
As of Thursday, North Korea said it was one of the few countries not infected with the coronavirus as of Thursday.
As of yesterday, the World Health Organization (WHO) had reported 105,1635 confirmed cases, including 79,332 cases in the 24 hours prior to 10 a.m. (0800 UTC) on April 4.
In the United States, more than 244,000 cases of coronavirus have been recorded, resulting in at least 5,900 deaths.
CBS News, citing data from Johns Hopkins University, reported that a coronavirus infection killed more than 1,000 people in the U.S. on Wednesday.
Around the world, more stringent measures have been announced to curb the spread of the disease in various countries.
On Thursday, Moscow Mayor Sergei Sobyanin extended the lockdown until May 1.
President Vladimir Putin announced that Russians across the country will continue to be paid during the shutdown until April 30.
Portugal’s parliament voted to extend the state of emergency by 15 days, with 215 votes in favour, 10 abstentions, and 1 against.
Saudi Arabia has extended the curfew in the holy city of Mecca and Medina to a full-day curfew; previously, it was only effective between 3 p.m. and 6 a.m.
Thailand plans to impose a curfew between 10 p.m. and 4 a.m.
Ohio Gov. Mike DeWine has announced that the state has extended the home order until May 1.
Australian stores have lowered the cap on the number of toilet papers per transaction.
On Sunday and Saturday nights, Australia’s chain of stores, Woolworths and Coles, lowered the purchase limit for each of their stores to two packs and one pack, respectively across the country.
ALDI also introduced a one-pack of restrictions on purchases on Monday.
These restrictions are posted in the form of a message on a cash register or posted to the chain’s Facebook page.
Buyers are hoarding goods for fear of COVID-19 in case they need to self-isolate.
On Wednesday, Woolworths also limited the number of toilet paper purchases for home delivery to each single package.
Prior to these changes, Woolworths and Coles introduced restrictions on the purchase of 4 packages per transaction on March 4 and 5, respectively.
Coles reported in a March 8 press release that “many stores are still selling out within an hour of arrival” after imposing a four-pack limit, saying demand was “unprecedented,” while ALDI said in Tuesday’s Facebook post that it was “unprecedented.”
According to a Woolworths spokesman, last week’s sales “surrected increase.”
Last week, Costco stores in Canberra will also be allowed to limit the number to two packs.
To further alleviate the shortage, Coles ordered larger packages from suppliers and increased the frequency of deliveries, Woolworths ordered additional inventory, while ALDI prepared inventory in advance for the scheduled Wednesday special offer.
Russell Zimmerman, executive director of the Australian Retailers Association, said retailers were trying to increase their inventory, but local council restrictions on delivery times made it difficult.
He expects production costs to rise as suppliers try to meet demand, while preferential activity will decrease.
On Tuesday, ALDI announced that some stores will not be able to hold special offers on Wednesday after releasing inventory early.
In a report by News.com.au, Dr Gary Mortimer, a retail expert at the Queensland University of Technology, said the store replenishes inventory every night.
He said that toilet paper is a large item, so there is a small amount of inventory, and, once sold out, it will leave huge shelf space, exacerbating the feeling of shortage of materials.
“Coles and Woolworths say you probably won’t be so panicky if there’s a lot of stuff on the shelves, goods like reel paper and hand sanitizer that can [be bought] and there’s plenty,” Russell Zimmerman told ABC News.
Who Gives a Crap, a manufacturer of recycled toilet paper, said last Wednesday that they were no longer in stock.
Kimberly-Clark, which makes comfortable toilet paper, and Solaris Paper, which makes Sorbent, both emphasize that they are working around the clock on weekends to guarantee supply.
Real estate website Domain.com reported that due to the small number of auctions held by buyers on Labor Day long weekends, some realtors at auctions in Melbourne will provide free toilet paper to the first bidder.
NT News, a daily newspaper printed in the city of Darwin, contained eight-page inserts in its Thursday edition, intended to be used as toilet paper after cropping.
ABC Australia reported on March 3 that stores were initially reluctant to impose restrictions, saying they did not plan to impose purchase restrictions.
Russell Zimmerman added that the demand for other goods is also high, including masks, hand sanitizer, dried goods, hand sanitizer and flour.
Similarly, outside of Australia, Ocado, a British online supermarket, restricted the purchase of Andres toilet paper to 2 packs for 12 rolls each on Sunday night.
The World Health Organization has declared COVID-19).
On Wednesday, the World Health Organization (WHO) announced that the ongoing outbreak of COVID-19, a disease caused by the coronavirus SARS-CoV-2, was a pandemic.
Although the term “pandemic” refers only to the extent of the spread of the disease, not the level of risk to specific cases, the WHO points out the need to urge governments to take measures:
“All countries/regions will still be able to change the course of this pandemic.
If each country tests, tests, treats, isolates, tracks and mobilizes its people as a response,” WHO Director-General Tedros Adhanom Ghebreyesus said.
“We are deeply concerned about the alarming level of proliferation and seriousness and the alarming level of inaction.”
According to Dr. Tom Frieden, former director of the U.S. Centers for Disease Control and Prevention, the pandemic was “unprecedented.”
He commented on CNN in February, saying, “With the exception of the flu, it has not been found which respiratory virus has continued to spread around the world after its emergence.”
Ghebreyesus expressed similar views and said, “We’ve never seen a pandemic triggered by the coronavirus before.”
“Similarly, we’ve never seen a controlled viral pandemic before,” he continued.
Before entering the new state of the pandemic, the WHO determined in January that the outbreak was a public health emergency of international concern.
Dr Anthony Fauci, director of the National Institute of Allergy and Infectious Diseases, said that to the outbreak, “most importantly, it’s still getting worse.”
As of Thursday, the Associated Press reported at least 126,000 cases of COVID-19 worldwide, resulting in more than 4,600 deaths.
2019-2020 Coronavirus Epidemic is the ongoing spread of the 2019 novel coronavirus disease (COVID-19), caused by severe acute respiratory syndrome coronavirus type 2 (SARS-CoV-2).
This outbreak was confirmed in Wuhan, China in December 2019, was listed as a public health emergency of international concern on January 30, 2020, and an epidemic on March 11, 2020.
As of April 10, 2020, 210 countries and regions around the world have reported about 1.16 million COVID-19 cases and about 97,000 deaths.
There are about 364,000 people cured.
China’s case fatality rate is about 4 percent, compared with between 0.08 percent (New Zealand) and 13.04 per cent (Algeria) in all regions of the world.
Common symptoms include fever, coughing, and shortness of breath.
It may also be accompanied by complications such as pneumonia and acute respiratory distress syndrome.
The incubation period ranging from contact with the patient to the onset varies from 2 to 14 days, usually about 5 days.
At present, there is no vaccine or specific antiviral treatment plan.
Clinical treatment is based on symptomatic support treatment. The recommended preventive measures include frequent hand washing, occulting the mouth when coughing, maintaining a safe distance from others, monitoring and self-isolation of suspected infected people.
National authorities have taken measures such as travel restrictions, quarantines, curfews, workplace risk prevention and control, and facility closures.
As a result of the severe global socio-economic impact of the epidemic, sports, religious, political and cultural activities have been postponed or cancelled, and panic shopping has exacerbated a large-scale supply shortage.
About 99.4% of primary and secondary school students are affected in 193 countries and regions around the world.
Due to the spread of false virus information online, there have been incidents of xenophobia and discrimination against Chinese people, other East and Southeast Asian blood and appearance groups, as well as groups in areas with severe epidemics.
However, the decline in mobility and the closure of heavy industries have led to a decline in the air pollution index and a decrease in carbon emissions.
On December 31, 2019, the health department in Wuhan (Hubei Province), China, reported a number of unexplained cases of pneumonia, and launched an investigation in early January 2020.
Most of these cases are related to the South China Seafood Wholesale Market, so the new coronavirus is believed to be zoonotic.
The virus that triggered the outbreak, known as SARS-CoV-2, is a newly discovered virus that is closely related to bat coronavirus, pangolin coronavirus, and SARS-CoV. The earliest-known patients were later confirmed on December 1, 2019, and there is no obvious connection with a number of cases in the South China seafood market that have emerged.
Two-thirds of the early cases reported in December 2019 were linked to the South China seafood market.
On March 13, 2020, the South China Morning Post published an unconfirmed report, saying that the first case may be traced back to November 17, 2019, when the patient was a 55-year-old man from Hubei Province. On February 26, 2020, according to the World Health Organization, the number of new reported cases in China decreased, but the number of new cases reported in Italy, Iran and South Korea suddenly increased significantly, and the number of new cases outside China exceeded the number of new cases outside China for the first time.
There may be a large number of unreported cases, especially cases of mild illness.
As of 26 February, there were relatively few reported cases among adolescents, and only 2.4% of the world's 19-year-old and below were reported. Patrick Varance, Britain's chief scientific adviser, estimated that about 60% of the UK's population would need to be infected with the new coronavirus in order to achieve effective herd immunity.
The number of cases refers to the number of people who have been tested for COVID-19 and are confirmed to be positive according to the official scheme.
As of March 23, the proportion of groups tested in the world did not exceed 3% of the total population, and many countries have also issued official policies that do not detect people with mild diseases, including Italy, the Netherlands, Spain and Switzerland.
According to a study published on March 16, as of January 23, an estimated 86% of COVID-19 infected people in China were undetected, and these unrecorded infections were the source of infection in 79% of recorded cases.
According to a statistical analysis published on March 30, the actual number of infections in Italy far exceeded the number of reported cases.
The basic number of COVID-19 infections (R0) was originally estimated to be between 1.4 and 2.4.
A study published by the U.S. Centers for Disease Control and Prevention suggests that R0 may be 5.7.
Most COVID-19 patients can recover.
Patients who were unable to recover ranged from onset to death for 6-41 days, the most common of 14 days.
As of April 10, 2020, about 97,000 people had died from COVID-19.
As of Feb. 5, about 80 percent of deaths in China were among people over the age of 60, and 75 percent of deaths were accompanied by underlying diseases such as cardiovascular disease and diabetes. Official statistics for COVID-19 deaths typically refer to deaths who test positive for the new coronavirus according to official schemes.
Actual COVID-19 deaths may far exceed statistics, as the data may not include untested patients, such as those who died at home, nursing homes, and elsewhere.
According to some data from Italy, the actual number of deaths during the epidemic is 4-5 times that of the official statistics of new crown virus pneumonia deaths.
A U.S. Centers for Disease Control and Prevention (CDC) spokeswoman said: "We know [the reported deaths] are lower than the actual number, and rumors that U.S. data are undervalued also confirms this statement. Underestimation often occurs in epidemic areas, such as H1N1 the 2009 swine flu epidemic. On January 9, 2020, China's first confirmed death occurred in Wuhan.
On February 1, the first death outside China occurred in the Philippines, and on February 14, the first death outside Asia occurred in France.
As of February 28, more than a dozen deaths have occurred in Iran, South Korea and Italy outside China.
As of March 13, more than 40 countries and territories on various continents except Antarctica have reported deaths. There are several methods commonly used to quantify mortality.
These numbers vary from region to region, change over time, and are affected by population characteristics such as the amount of testing, the quality of the health system, treatment options, the timing of the first outbreak, and age, gender, and overall health status. The case fatality rate is divided by the number of confirmed deaths over a given period of time.
According to statistics from Johns Hopkins University, as of April 10, 2020, the global case fatality rate was 6.0% (97,039/1,617,204).
The case fatality rate is also different in different regions.
In China, the estimated mortality rate dropped from 17.3 per cent (for patients who became ill between January 1 and 10 of 2020) to 0.7 per cent (patients after 1 February 2020). Other measures include case mortality (CFR) and infection mortality (IFR), which reflects the number of disease deaths as a percentage of the number of confirmed cases, while the latter reflects the number of deaths from the disease as a percentage of the number of infections (including those diagnosed and undiagnosed).
There is no time limit for these statistics, and it tracks the entire process from infection to case resolution in a particular population.
Many scholars have tried to calculate the above data for specific groups of people.
According to estimates by Oxford University’s Center for Evidence-based Medicine, the overall mortality rate from the outbreak is between 0.1% and 0.39%.
The upper limit of estimates for this range is in line with Germany’s first randomized detection of COVID-19, as well as a statistical analysis study to detect the impact on CFR estimates.
The World Health Organization says the outbreak is manageable.
Explosion peaks and final duration cannot be determined and varies by region.
Maciej Boni, from Penn State University, said: “If left unchecked, outbreaks of infectious diseases often enter a period of stagnation after their existing hosts run out and then begin to tend to ease.”
But at the moment it is almost impossible for us to reasonably predict when the inflection point will occur.”
Zhong Nanshan, a senior medical adviser to the Chinese government, believes that if all countries are mobilized to adopt the World Health Organization's recommendation to take measures to stop the spread of the virus, "it can be expected to end the epidemic in June."
On March 17, Adam Kucharski from the London School of Hygiene and Tropical Medicine said that SARS-CoV-2 “could flare up repeatedly for one to two years.”
Research led by Neil Ferguson at Imperial College has shown that “it can take 18 months or more before the vaccine is put into use” and it’s important to maintain physical distance and other measures.
William Schaffner, from Vanderbilt University, said: “This coronavirus is highly transmissible, and I don’t think it’s likely to disappear entirely” and “could evolve into a seasonal disease with an outbreak once a year.”
The pathogenicity of another outbreak depends on herd immunity and degree of mutation.
COVID-19’s symptoms are relatively non-specific, and some infected people may be asymptomatic.
The two most common symptoms were fever (88%) and dry cough (68%).
Less common symptoms include fatigue, sputum in the respiratory tract (sputum), loss of olfactoriness, shortness of breath, muscle and joint pain, sore throats, headaches, chills, vomiting, chopping, diarrhoea, or vincing. According to the World Health Organization, about one in six patients will become seriously ill and have difficulty breathing.
The U.S. Centers for Disease Control and Prevention (CDC) lists breathing difficulties, persistent chest pain or stress, abrupt awareness blurring, difficulty in wakingness, and facial or lip development as an urgent symptom; if these symptoms occur, it is recommended to seek medical attention immediately. Further development of the disease can lead to severe pneumonia, acute respiratory distress syndrome, sepsisis, infectious shock, and death.
Some infected people may be asymptomatic and have no clinical manifestations, but the test results are positive, so the researchers recommend close observation and examination of close contacts of confirmed patients to rule out the possibility of infection.
China estimates that the rate of asymptomatic infections ranges from very low to 44%.
The incubation period (the interval from infection to onset) is generally 1-14 days; it is most common for 5 days. With regard to uncertainty, it can be illustrated by an example: the proportion of patients who lost their sense of smell in COVID-19 was initially estimated at 30% and later decreased to 15%.
Some details about the route of transmission of the disease have not yet been determined.
At present, it is considered that the disease is mainly transmitted through close contact and droplets produced when coughing, sneezing, or speaking; close contact refers to 1-2 meters (3-6 feet).
The study found that non-obstructed coughs could cause droplets to spread as far as 4.5 meters (15 feet) to 8.2 meters (15-27 feet).
It has also been suggested that droplets produced by people talking stay in the air for a long time can also lead to the spread of the virus. Although the virus is usually not transmitted through the air, people may also produce droplets when exhaling or speaking.
This droplet may fall to the mouth and nose of the surrounding people, and even be sucked into the lungs.
Medical procedures such as intubation and cardiopulmonary resuscitation (CPR) can cause respiratory secretions to fog, resulting in airborne transmission.
In addition, if a person touches the surface contaminated by the virus (including the skin) and then touches the mouth of his or her nose, it may also lead to the spread of the virus.
There are also concerns that the virus may spread through feces, but this risk is considered lower.
The Chinese government has denied the possibility of SARS-CoV-2 fecal oral transmission. The virus is the most contagious three days after onset, but it is also contagious before and after onset.
Positive results can be detected as early as three days prior to onset, indicating that the new coronavirus was contagious before obvious symptoms appeared.
Although there have been very few laboratory-confirmed asymptomatic case reports, some countries have detected asymptomatic infections during contact tracing investigations.
According to the European Center for Disease Control and Prevention (ECDC), although the spread of the new coronavirus is not yet completely clear, the average person can infect 2-3 people. The survival time of the virus on the surface of the object ranges from a few hours to a few days.
Specifically, it is known that the new coronavirus survives up to three days on plastic (polypropylene) and 304 stainless steel, one day on cardboard and up to four hours on copper.
However, its survival time is also affected by humidity and temperature. There have been cases where pets and other animals have tested positive for COVID-19.
There is currently no evidence that animals transmit the virus to humans, but British official agencies recommend that people wash their hands after touching the animals, just like touching other surfaces that may have been touched by an infected person.
SARS-CoV-2 is a new type of virus that was first isolated from three pneumonia patients with a group of acute respiratory diseases in Wuhan.
All characteristics of this new SARS-CoV-2 virus are present in the relevant coronaviruses in nature. Before the virus enters the human body, it can be killed with household soap, because the soap is able to dissolve the protective cystic membrane of the virus. SARS-CoV-2 is closely related to the original SARS-CoV.
It is currently considered a zoonotic disease.
Genetic analysis revealed that this coronavirus is genetically linked to the β coronavirus genus Sarbecovirus (cluster B) and two clusters of bat-derived virus strains.
Coronavirus and other bat coronavirus samples (BatCov RaTG13) were 96 percent consistent across the genome level.
In February 2020, Chinese researchers found that viruses in pangolins and viruses in the human body are only one amino acid in some parts of the genome sequence.
As of today's whole-genome comparison, it has been found that up to 92% of the genetic material between pangolin coronavirus and SARS-CoV-2 is the same, which is not enough to prove that pangolins are intermediate hosts.
Whether infected with the virus can be initially diagnosed by symptoms, but it can eventually be confirmed by reverse transcription polymerase chain reaction (rRT-PCR) or CT imaging of infected secretions.
A comparison study of PCR and CT conducted in Wuhan showed that CT is significantly more sensitive than PCR, but the specificity is low, and many of the imaging characteristics coincide with other pneumonia and disease development processes.
As of March 2020, the American Radiological Society recommended that “CT should not be used to screen for COVID-19 or as the first test to diagnose the disease.”
At present, the World Health Organization has announced a number of nucleic acid detection programs for SARS-CoV-2, and the first program was released on January 17.
Nucleic acid detection uses real-time reverse transcription polymerase chain reaction (rRT-PCR).
Respiratory samples or blood samples can be used for testing.
Results can generally be obtained within a few hours to a few days.
In general, this test is carried out via nasopharyngeal swabs, and can also be carried out through pharyngeal swabs. Many laboratories and companies are developing serological tests for detecting antibodies.
As of April 6, 2020, none of these tests have been proven to be sufficiently accurate to be approved for widespread use.
In the United States, serological tests developed by Cellex have been approved for use only by accredited laboratories in emergency situations.
Typical imaging characteristics of X tablets and computed tomography (CT) in patients with the disease include peripheral grinding glass shadow asymmetry and thoracic cavity fluid accumulation.
The Italian Radiological Society is in the process of compiling an international online database of images of confirmed cases.
Due to overlap with other viral infections, such as adenoviruses, images that have not been confirmed by PCR have limited specificity in identifying COVID-19.
A large Chinese study that compared chest CT results with PCR results found that although the specificity of the image to identify the infection was low, the detection was faster and more sensitive, so it could be considered as a means of screening in popular areas.
AI-based convolutional neural networks have been developed, which can be combined with X tablets and CT to detect the image characteristics of the virus.
Measures to prevent the spread of the disease include good personal hygiene habits, frequent handwashing, avoid washing hands to directly touch the mouth of the eyes and nose, coughing or sneezing with paper towels to cover the mouth and nose, used paper towels directly thrown into the garbage can.
Suspected infected people should wear medical surgical masks when entering public places.
It is also recommended to take measures to maintain physical distance to prevent the spread of the virus. Many governments have restricted or recommended avoiding travel to and from the affected countries and regions without necessity.
However, the virus has now reached the community transmission stage in most parts of the world.
This means that the virus is spreading within the community, and some community residents have no way to know where or how they are infected. Healthcare workers who come into contact with suspected infected people should take standard precautions, contact precautions, and eye protection. Tracking contacts is an important means for health regulators to determine the source of infection and prevent further transmission.
The government’s use of mobile phone location data for tracking purposes has raised concerns about privacy, and more than 100 organizations, including Amnesty International, have issued statements calling for restrictions on such oversight.
At present, a variety of mobile applications have been put into use or entered the proposal for voluntary use, and as of April 7, 2020, more than a dozen expert groups are working on solutions with privacy protection features, such as using Bluetooth to record the distance between users and other mobile phones.
Once the user has been in close contact with a patient who has tested positive for COVID-19, they will receive informational reminders. False statements to prevent infection continue to spread, such as rinsing their noses and rinsing their mouthwashes, which are ineffective.
There is currently no COVID-19 vaccine, but many organizations are actively developing it.
It is recommended to stop the spread of the disease by washing hands frequently.
The U.S. Centers for Disease Control and Prevention recommends that people often wash their hands with soap and tap water for at least 20 seconds, especially after going to the toilet or when there is obvious dirt on their hands, before meals, and after rubbing their nose, coughing, or sneezing.
This is because before entering the human body, the virus can be destroyed by simply using household soap to destroy its protective scales, thus killing the virus.
The U.S. Centers for Disease Control and Prevention (CDC) also recommends that if you don’t have soap and running water around you, you can wash your hands with at least 60 percent of your alcohol.
The World Health Organization recommends that people avoid touching their eyes, noses or mouth without washing their hands.
The surface of the object can be disinfected with a variety of solutions (the disinfectant works within one minute of the action of the stainless steel surface), including 62-71% ethanol, 50-100% isopropanol, 0.1% sodium hypochlorite, 0.5% hydrogen peroxide, and 0.2-7.5 percent polyvinoxone iodine.
Other solutions, such as ammonium benza chloride and chlorhexidine gluconate, are less effective.
The U.S. Centers for Disease Control and Prevention recommends that if there are suspected or confirmed cases of new coronavirus pneumonia in offices or facilities such as day care facilities, all areas should be disinfected, including offices, bathrooms, public areas, shared electronic devices such as tablets, touch screens, keyboards, remote controls, and ATMs used by patients.
WHO advises people to cover their noses with their elbows or paper towels when coughing or sneezing, and to dispose of all contaminated paper towels immediately.
It is recommended that suspected infected people wear medical surgical masks, because wearing masks can reduce the amount of droplets exhaled when speaking, sneezing and coughing, and shorten the transmission distance.
The World Health Organization has issued a description of the use and use of masks.
Stephen Griffin, a virologist at the University of Leeds, said: "Wearing masks can reduce people's tendency to touch their faces, which is also a major source of infection in the absence of hand hygiene." In addition, it is also recommended that people responsible for taking care of suspected infected people wear masks.
The World Health Organization recommends that healthy people only wear masks in high-risk areas, such as those responsible for caring for COVID-19 patients, but the organization also recognizes that wearing masks can help avoid touching faces.
Some countries and regions have begun to encourage the general public to wear masks.
The U.S. Centers for Disease Control and Prevention (CDC) recommends the wearing of non-medical masks made with fabric. China explicitly recommends that healthy citizens also wear disposable medical masks, especially when in close contact with others within (1 meter (3 feet).
Hong Kong, China, is advised to wear masks when taking public transportation or entering crowded places.
Thai health officials advised people to make their own masks and wash them every day.
In the Czech Republic and Slovakia, it is strictly forbidden not to wear masks or to block the mouth and nose to enter public places.
On March 16, Vietnam asked everyone to wear masks to enter public places to protect themselves and others.
The Austrian government requires everyone to wear masks when entering the grocery store.
Israel requires all residents to wear masks in public.
Taiwan has produced 10 million masks per day since mid-March, and on April 1, they were required to wear masks on trains and intercity buses.
Panama requires the wearing of masks and advises residents who can't buy masks to make their own masks.
Masks have also been widely used in Japan, South Korea, Malaysia and Singapore.
Infection prevention and control measures related to maintaining social distance (also known as maintaining physical distance) are designed to minimize close contact between people, thereby slowing the spread of the disease.
Measures include quarantines; travel restrictions; school closures, shutdowns, and closures of stadiums, theaters, or shopping malls.
In terms of maintaining social distance, the measures that individuals can take include staying at home, restricting travel, avoiding access to crowded areas, switching to contactless greetings, and maintaining physical distance from others.
Today, many governments have mandated or advised areas affected by the outbreak to maintain social distancing.
With regard to the largest crowd-aggregation scale, the initial recommendation of U.S. government agencies and the World Health Organization was 250 (in areas where COVID-19 was not transmitted), which was then quickly reduced to 50, and later to 10.
On March 22, 2020, more than two people were banned from public gatherings in Germany. The elderly and those suffering from underlying diseases such as diabetes, heart disease, respiratory diseases, high blood pressure, and compromised immune systems are at higher risk of serious illness and complications. The German Center for Disease Control and Prevention advised such people living in the affected areas not to go out as much as possible.
The use of the term "maintaining social distance" implies a requirement for people to be completely isolated from society, without encouraging people to use other methods to maintain human-to-human connection. Some authorities have also issued sexual health guidelines applicable during the pandemic.
Suggestions include having sex only with partners who live together and are uninfected or asymptomatic.
COVID-19 is recommended for self-isolation at home for confirmed patients and suspected infected people.
Health institutions have issued detailed instructions on correct self-isolation. Many governments have mandated or recommended self-isolation for all residents of affected areas.
In addition, the most severe self-isolation instructions have also been issued to high-risk groups.
People who may be exposed to COVID-19, or who have recently been to a country or region that have experienced widespread transmission will be advised to self-quarantine for 14 days from the time they may be exposed to the virus.
Epidemic prevention and control strategies include containment/suppression and mitigation.
Containment is suitable in the early stages of the outbreak, with the aim of tracking and isolating infected people, while taking other infection prevention and control and vaccination measures to stop the spread of the disease to other populations.
If it is no longer possible to curb the spread of the disease, efforts should be made to transition to the mitigation stage: take measures to slow the spread of the disease and reduce the impact of the disease on the health system and society.
Containment and mitigation measures can take place simultaneously.
Repression requires more extreme prevention and control measures to reverse the pandemic by reducing the number of basic regenerations to less than 1. Part of the effort to control infectious disease outbreaks is to try to reduce the peak of the epidemic, which is what we ket known as the flat epidemiological curve.
This reduces the risk of the health service system and buys more time for the development of vaccines and treatment programs.
Non-pharmaceutical interventions that can control the outbreak include individual preventive measures, such as maintaining hand hygiene, wearing masks, and self-isolation; community measures aimed at maintaining a physical distance, such as suspension of schools and the cancellation of large-scale gatherings; community actions designed to encourage acceptance and participation in such interventions; and environmental measures such as surface cleanliness. In the face of significantly aggravated outbreaks, China has immediately taken more severe containment measures, such as the closure of cities and the imposition of strict travel bans.
Various measures have also been taken in other countries and regions to limit the spread of the virus.
South Korea has introduced large-scale screening and local isolation measures, and issued an alert on the activity trajectories of infected people.
Singapore provided financial support for self-isolating infected persons and imposed huge fines on those who refused to self-isolate.
Taiwan has increased mask production and imposed penalties on suppliers of medical supplies that have been sold. Simulations in the United Kingdom and the United States have shown significant challenges in alleviating (slowing but not stopping the spread of the epidemic) and curbing (reversing the spread of the epidemic).
The best mitigation policies could reduce peak medical demand by two-thirds, halving deaths, but still resulting in hundreds of thousands of deaths and the collapse of health systems.
The suppression may be the preferred measure, but it needs to be maintained until the virus is stopped in the population (or until the vaccine is introduced, whichever comes first), otherwise once the measures are relaxed, the transmission will rebound quickly.
Long-term interventions aimed at curbing pandemics come with social and economic costs.
No specific antiviral drugs have yet been approved for COVID-19, but development efforts are ongoing, including trials of existing drugs.
Taking over-the-counter cold medicines, rehydration fluids, and rest may help relieve symptoms.
Depending on the severity of the condition, oxygen therapy, intravenous fluids, and respiratory support may be required.
The use of steroids can make the situation worse.
Several compounds previously approved for the treatment of other viral diseases are currently being studied to determine whether they are suitable for the treatment of COVID-19.
The World Health Organization also noted that a number of “traditional home remedies” can relieve symptoms caused by SARS-CoV-19.
According to the World Health Organization, improving processing capacity and adapting medical systems to meet the needs of COVID-19 patients is the basic outbreak response.
The ECDC and the World Health Organization Regional Office for Europe have issued guidelines for hospital and primary health care services to transfer resources at multiple levels, including focusing laboratory services on COVID-19 testing, eliminating selective procedures as much as possible, segregating and isolating COVID-19-positive patients, and improving intensive care by training personnel and increasing the number of ventilators and beds available.
Speculations about the origin of the first case (the so-called Patient Zero) vary widely.
At present, the first known case of novel coronavirus pneumonia may be traced back to December 1, 2019, located in Wuhan, Hubei, China.
Within a month, the number of new coronavirus cases in Hubei Province gradually increased.
Most of these patients are related to the South China Seafood Wholesale Market, which also sells live animals, a theory that the new coronavirus originates from some of these animals; in other words, it originates from zoonotic diseases. On December 26th, Zhang Jixian, a doctor at Hubei Chinese and Western Medicine Hospital, first discovered and treated a group of unexplained patients with pneumonia, and reported to the Center for Disease Prevention and Control in Jianghan District of Wuhan on December 27.
On December 30, a group of doctors at Wuhan Central Hospital alerted his colleagues about a “SARS-like coronavirus.”
Eight doctors, including Li Wenliang, were admonished by the police for spreading “rumors,” while another doctor, Ivan, was admonished by his superiors for sounding the alarm.
The Wuhan Municipal Health Commission then issued an announcement on December 31 and reported it to the World Health Organization.
After receiving numerous reports of unexplained cases of pneumonia, the Wuhan health department launched an investigation in early January. In the early stages of the outbreak, the number of cases doubled about every seven and a half days.
From the beginning of January to the middle of January 2020, the virus spread to other provinces in China due to the large population movement of the Chinese Spring Festival and the influence of Wuhan as a transportation hub and a major railway hub.
On January 20, China reported nearly 140 new confirmed cases in one day, including two in Beijing and one in Shenzhen.
According to later official data, as of January 20, 2020, 6,174 people had developed symptoms. As of March 26, the number of confirmed cases in the United States exceeded that of China and Italy, making it the country with the largest number of confirmed cases in the world. As of April 9, 2020, the cumulative number of confirmed cases worldwide exceeded 1.61 million; the number of deaths exceeded 97,000, and more than 364,000 cases were cured.
Confirmed cases have been reported in about 200 countries and territories around the world.
Affected by the European pandemic, many countries in the Schengen Area have adopted prevention and control measures to restrict the free movement of people and establish border controls.
State-level responses include containment, such as isolation (also known as stay-at-home orders, in-site shelters or lockdowns) and curfews. As of April 2, there were nearly 300 million people in the United States, or about 90 per cent of the total population, in some form of lockdown, more than 50 million in the Philippines, about 59 million in South Africa, and 1.3 billion people in India.
On March 26, 1.7 billion people around the world were in some form of lockdown, two days later that number rose to 2.6 billion, or about one-third of the world’s population.
The first confirmed case of COVID-19 dates back to December 1, 2019, in Wuhan; an unconfirmed report speculates that the earliest cases date back to November 17.
On December 26, Dr. Zhang discovered a number of unexplained cases of pneumonia, and his hospital reported the situation to the Center for Disease Control and Prevention in Jianghan District of Wuhan on December 27.
On December 27, 2019, a group of SARS-like coronaviruses was found in initial genetic testing of patient samples.
The Wuhan Municipal Health Commission issued an announcement on December 31.
It was reported to the World Health Organization on the same day.
After these notices were issued, several doctors in Wuhan were admonished by the police for "rumours" for walking through the epidemic.
China’s National Health Commission initially claimed there was no “clear evidence” that there had been.
In late January, the Chinese government launched a radical movement, and later, General Secretary of the Central Committee of the Communist Party of China Xi Jinping called it a “people’s war” to contain the spread of the virus.
On January 23, the government announced that it would pull up an anti-epidemic line in Wuhan and prohibit people from entering and leaving Wuhan, which later extended to 15 other cities in Hubei, affecting a total of about 57 million people, which can be called "the largest isolated incident in human history."
The use of private cars in the city is prohibited.
In many places, celebrations of the Chinese Lunar New Year (January 25) have also been cancelled.
The government also announced the construction of a temporary hospital, Vulcan Mountain Hospital, which was completed and put into use within 10 days.
Subsequently, another hospital, Raytheon Mountain Hospital, was built to receive more patients.
In addition to building new hospitals, China has transformed 14 other venues in Wuhan, such as convention centers and stadiums, into cabin hospitals. On January 26, the government took further measures to contain the COVID-19 epidemic, including issuing health declaration forms to travelers and extending the Spring Festival holiday.
National universities and primary and secondary schools have also been completely suspended.
A number of measures have also been taken in Hong Kong and Macao, China, in particular with regard to primary and secondary schools and universities.
Many places in China have implemented remote home office measures.
In addition to Hubei Province, travel restrictions have also been imposed on other identities.
Public transportation has been adjusted, and museums across China have also been temporarily closed.
In many cities, public facilities are regulated, and it is estimated that about 760 million people (more than half of the population) have been restricted by some form of outdoor activity. After the outbreak entered the global phase in March, the Chinese authorities took strict measures to prevent the virus from being "imported" from other countries and regions.
For example, Beijing imposes a 14-day mandatory quarantine on all people entering Beijing from abroad. In the five days leading up to March 23, there was only one local case in mainland China, which was transmitted by a traveler returning to Guangzhou from Istanbul.
On March 24, 2020, Chinese Premier Li Keqiang reported that the spread of local cases has basically been curbed, and China's epidemic has been controlled.
On the same day, after two full months of the lockdown was imposed, the Chinese Ministry of Foreign Affairs announced on March 26, 2020 that from March 28, foreigners with valid Chinese visas and residence permits had been suspended, and the relevant details were not disclosed about when the policy ended.
Those who wish to enter China are required to apply for a visa from the Chinese Embassy or Consulate.
The Chinese government encouraged businesses and factories to resume work on March 30 and provided them with a monetary stimulus package. The State Council announced that April 4 would be designated as a national day of mourning, and a three-minute national mourning campaign was launched at 10:00 on the same day. This day coincided with the Qingming Festival, when the central government urged families to work together through the Internet to mourn their deceased relatives, maintain physical distance, and avoid the second outbreak of COVID-19.
COVID-19 confirmed cases occurred in South Korea on January 20, 2020.
South Korea's health agency reported a significant increase in confirmed cases on Feb. 20, largely due to the holding of a new gathering of religious movements in Daeqiu, South Korea.
Believers in Xintiandi from Wuhan to Daeqiu are suspected to be the source of the outbreak.
As of February 22, 1,261 out of 9,336 members of the church, or about 13 percent, reported symptoms. South Korea declared its highest alert level on February 23, 2020.
On February 28, more than 2,000 confirmed cases were reported in South Korea, rising to 3,150 on February 29.
Three soldiers tested positive for the virus, and all South Korean military bases have since been quarantined.
The flight schedule has also been adjusted due to the impact of the epidemic. South Korea has launched what is considered the world's largest and best-organized virus prevention and control program, that is, screening a large number of people for the virus, isolating all infected people, and tracking and isolating close contacts of infected people.
Screening methods include the mandatory requirement for new arrivals from abroad to self-report their symptoms through a mobile app, get rid of virus tests, get results the next day, and improve their testing capacity, which can accommodate up to 20,000 people per day.
Although the city has not been quarantined, South Korea's plan is seen as a success in epidemic prevention and control. South Korean society initially showed a polarized attitude towards President Moon Jae-in's crisis response.
Many South Koreans have signed the petition, some have called for the impeachment, accusing the government of mishandling the epidemic, and some have praised his coping strategies.
On March 23, it was reported that South Korea had the lowest single-day total number of new cases in four weeks.
On March 29, it was reported that from April 1, all newly arrived overseas personnel will be quarantined for two weeks.
According to media reports on April 1, South Korea has received requests for virus testing assistance from 121 different countries and regions.
On February 19, Iran announced the emergence of the first confirmed cases of SARS-CoV-2 in Koum, and later, according to Iran's Ministry of Health, two patients died on the same day.
The early measures announced by the government included the cancellation of concerts and other cultural events, sporting events and Friday prayers, and the total suspension of universities, higher education institutions and primary and secondary schools.
Iran allocated five trillion riyals to fight the virus.
On February 26, 2020, Iranian President Hassan Rouhani said that there were no plans to lock down areas affected by the epidemic, but would only isolate infected individuals.
In March, Iran announced plans to restrict intercity travel, but congestion continued until the Persian New Year.
Shiite shrines in the Qom region have been open to pilgrims until March 16, 2020. In February, Iran became a center for the spread of the virus after China.
Many parties believe that the extent of Iran's outbreak has been masked, including that as of February 28, the retrospective results of cases in more than a dozen countries pointed to Iran, indicating that Iran's actual outbreak is more serious than the 388 cases reported by the government as of that day.
Iran’s parliament was forced to shut down, and 23 of its 290 MPs reportedly tested positive for the virus on March 3.
On March 12, Human Rights Watch urged Iranian prisons to unconditionally release human rights defenders detained for peacefully expressing dissent and to temporarily release all eligible prisoners.
It noted that the virus is at greater risk of transmission in closed institutions such as detention centers and that they lack adequate health-care measures.
On March 15, the Iranian government reported 100 deaths in a single day, the highest single-day deaths reported in the country since the outbreak began.
As of March 17, at least 12 current or former Iranian politicians and government officials had died from the disease.
As of March 23, there were 50 new confirmed cases per hour in Iran, and one new case of coronavirus death every 10 minutes.
According to World Health Organization officials, the actual number of cases in Iran may be five times higher than the number reported.
There is also the view that U.S. sanctions on Iran may affect the country's financial resources in responding to the epidemic.
The United Nations High Commissioner for Human Rights has called for the easing of economic sanctions on countries most affected by the pandemic, including Iran.
On January 31, two Chinese tourists were tested positive for SARS-CoV-2 in Rome, and the epidemic was confirmed to spread to Italy.
Subsequently, the number of cases in the country rose sharply, prompting the Italian government to suspend all flights to and from China and declare a state of emergency.
Beginning with 16 confirmed cases in Lombardy on February 21, a number of unlinked cases of COVID-19 were detected. On 22 February, a new decree to contain the spread of the epidemic was announced, including the isolation of more than 50,000 people in 11 different cities in northern Italy.
Prime Minister Giuseppe Conte said: "It is strictly forbidden to enter and leave the affected areas.
On March 4, due to 100 deaths in the country, the Italian government ordered that schools at all levels across the country should be completely suspended.
All major sporting events, including the Italian Football League, will be held in spectator-free mode until April, but on March 9, all sporting events have been completely suspended for at least one month.
On March 11, Prime Minister Conte ordered the discontinuation of almost all commercial activities except for supermarkets and pharmacies. On March 6, the Italian Society of Anesthesia Analgesic Surgery (SIAARTI) announced medical ethical advice on possible triage programmes.
On March 19, Italy reported 3,405 deaths from the pandemic, surpassing China to become the country with the highest number of new coronavirus deaths in the world.
On March 22, it was reported that Russia had sent nine military aircraft carrying medical equipment to Italy.
As of April 5, there had been 128,948 confirmed cases, 15,887 deaths and 21,815 cured cases in Italy, most of which occurred in Lombardy.
CNN has reported that Italy's large elderly population and so far unable to detect the dual factors of all infected people may be in some of the reasons for the high mortality rate.
Britain's initial response to the virus is believed to be one of the most relaxed in affected countries. It wasn't until 18 March 2020 that the British government began to implement various forms of social distancing or large-scale isolation for its citizens.
The British government has been criticized for its lack of speed and strength in responding to public concerns. On March 16, British Prime Minister Boris Johnson issued a statement recommending the cancellation of any non-necessary travel and social contacts, advising people to stay at home as much as possible and avoiding places such as bars, restaurants and theatres.
On March 20, the government announced that it should close all leisure places, including bars and gyms, as soon as possible, and promised to bear up to 80% of workers' wages, with a monthly ceiling of 2,500 pounds, to prevent job losses under the crisis. On 23 March, the British Prime Minister announced more stringent measures to maintain social distance, banning gatherings of more than two people, and not doing any travel and outdoor activities unless it was absolutely necessary.
Unlike previous measures, the announced restrictions are enforced by the police through fines and dispersing assemblies.
In addition to supermarkets, pharmacies, banks, hardware stores, gas stations and garages that have been identified as "essential" businesses, most of the other businesses have been ordered to shut down.
The first confirmed case of COVID-19 was detected on January 20 in the northwestern state of the Pacific, and the patient returned from Wuhan on January 15.
On January 29, the White House established the Coronavirus Task Force.
On January 31, the Trump administration declared a public health emergency and imposed entry restrictions on travelers from China.
On January 28, 2020, the U.S. Centers for Disease Control and Prevention (the U.S. government's main public health agency) announced that it had developed its own test kits.
Despite this, the U.S. was still running slowly in testing, masking the real situation of the epidemic at that time.
There were defects in the test kits produced by the federal government in February, until the end of February, when the federal government approved the use of non-governmental kits (from academia, companies and hospitals), and it was not until the beginning of March to determine the test eligibility restriction standard (after requiring medical instructions), all of these factors led to the U.S. pre-testing obstruction.
On Feb. 27, The Washington Post reported that fewer than 4,000 U.S. tests were performed.
On March 13, the Atlantic reported that the total number of tests performed by the United States was less than 14,000.
On March 22, the Associated Press reported: "Many patients with symptoms and orders waited a few hours or a few days to get tested." On February 29, after Washington reported the first death in the United States, Governor Jay Inslee declared a state of emergency, and other states quickly followed suit.
In mid-March, schools at all levels across the country were closed. On March 6, 2020, some epidemiologists at Imperial College in London predicted the impact of the new coronavirus on the United States.
On the same day, President Trump signed the Coronavirus Prevention and Response Supplemental Appropriations Act, which provided $8.3 billion in emergency funding for federal agencies to respond to the outbreak.
Enterprises impose travel restrictions on employees, cancel meetings, and encourage employees to work from home.
On March 11, Trump announced a 30-day travel ban on most European countries except the United Kingdom from March 13.
The next day, Trump expanded restrictions to include Britain and Ireland.
On March 13, Trump declared a national emergency and began to use federal funds to deal with the epidemic crisis.
Since March 15th, many businesses across the U.S. have shut down or shortened their business hours to ease the spread of the virus.
As of March 17, confirmed cases had been reported in 50 states and the District of Columbia in the United States. As of March 23, it was reported that there were 10,700 confirmed cases of novel coronavirus in New York City, surpassing the total number of cases in South Korea.
On March 25, the governor of New York said that maintaining social distancing appeared to have begun to play a role, and that the doubling of cases had been increased from 2.0 days to 4.7 days.
As of March 28, New York City had 32,308 confirmed cases and 672 deaths due to the virus. On March 26, it was reported that the number of confirmed new coronavirus infections in the United States exceeded that of any other country, including China and Italy. As of April 8, the cumulative number of confirmed cases in the United States reached 400,335 cases and 12,841 deaths.
According to media reports on March 30, U.S. President Trump decided to extend the implementation of social distancing guidelines to April 30.
On the same day, a medical ship of about 1,000 beds was anchored in New York.
On April 3, the U.S. recorded 884 new coronavirus deaths in 24 hours, the highest on record.
On April 3, the total number of cases in New York State exceeded 100,000. The White House instructed health officials and scientists to coordinate virus-related public statements and publications with Vice President Mike Pence's office as a way to downplay the threat and the transmission of information, which has been criticized by the public.
People's overall approval of Trump's crisis prevention and control measures has been polarized due to partisan differences.
Some U.S. officials and commentators have criticized the U.S. for relying on key supplies such as imports of basic medical supplies from China.
Analysis of air travel patterns was used to map and predict propagation patterns and was published in the Journal of Travel Medicine in mid-January 2020.
According to information released by the International Air Transport Association in 2018, the number of passengers departing from Wuhan to Bangkok, Hong Kong, Tokyo and Taipei is the largest.
It is also reported that Dubai, Sydney and Melbourne are also popular destinations for travellers from Wuhan.
Among the 20 most popular destination cities, Bali is reported to be in the worst prepared status and Australia's cities are in the best prepared state. Australia released the new coronavirus (COVID-19) contingency plan on February 7.
There is not enough knowledge about COVID-19, and Australia will focus on border control and communication in its response to the epidemic.
On March 21, Australia declared a “human biosecurity emergency.”
Due to the effective isolation of public transportation in Wuhan and other parts of Hubei Province, some countries and regions have planned to withdraw their own citizens and diplomats living in these areas, mainly through chartered flights in their own countries and licensed by the relevant Chinese authorities.
Among them, Canada, the United States, Japan, India, Sri Lanka, Australia, France, Argentina, Germany and Thailand were among the first to be evacuated.
Pakistan has said it will not withdraw from China.
On February 7, Brazil withdrew 34 Brazilian or family members, as well as four Poles, one Chinese and one Indian.
The Brazilian plane passed through Poland and made a short stay before flying to Brazil, where citizens of Poland, China and India disempliated.
Brazilian citizens who have been to Wuhan have been sent to a military base near Brasilia to be quarantined.
On the same day, 215 Canadians (the first of which withdrew 176 and the second chartered by the U.S. government) was withdrawn from Wuhan and sent to the Trenton Gard base for a two-week quarantine.
On February 11, another plane carrying 185 Canadians took off from Wuhan and landed at the Trenton Army base.
On 3 and 4 February, Australian authorities withdrew 277 citizens and sent them to the Christmas Island detention centre, which has been converted to isolation, for 14 days of isolation.
An evacuee plane from New Zealand landed in Auckland on February 5; passengers on board, including some from Australia and the Pacific, were sent to a naval base north of Auckland to be quarantined.
On February 15, the United States announced the withdrawal of Americans on the Diamond Princess cruise ship.
On February 21, a plane carrying 129 Canadian passengers landed in Trenton, Ontario, from passengers withdrawn from the Diamond Princess.
At the beginning of March, the Indian government began to evacuate from Iran. On March 14, the South African government chartered a South African Airlines aircraft to withdraw 112 South African citizens.
Passengers were medically investigated before take-off, and four South Africans showing signs of the new coronavirus were left behind to reduce the risk of transmission.
Only South Africans with negative test results were retracted.
All South Africans (including crew members, pilots, hotel workers, police and soldiers involved in the humanitarian mission) tested negative and, as a precaution, were sent to The Ranch Resort for 14 days of observation and isolation.
On March 20th, affected by the epidemic, the United States began to withdraw some of its troops from Iraq.
On February 5, China’s foreign ministry said 21 countries, including Belarus, Pakistan, Trinidad and Tobago, Egypt and Iran, had provided assistance to China.
Some Chinese students have also joined forces to help deliver relief supplies to virus-hit areas of China. A joint group in the Greater Chicago area reportedly delivered 50,000 N95 masks to hospitals in Hubei Province on Jan. 30. In coordination with FedEx, humanitarian aid organization Direct Relief delivered 200,000 masks and other personal protective equipment, including gloves and protective clothing, by an emergency airlift to the Wuhan Concorde Hospital on January 30.
On Feb. 5, Gates announced a donation of $100 million to the World Health Organization to fund vaccine development and treatment while protecting “high-risk populations in Africa and South Asia.”
According to Interaksyon, the Chinese government donated 200,000 masks to the Philippines on Feb. 6, following the delivery of 3.16 million masks to Wuhan by Philippine Senator Richard Gordon.
On February 19, the Singapore Red Cross announced that it would provide $2.26 million worth of aid to China.
Japan donated 1 million masks to Wuhan, Turkey distributed medical equipment to Wuhan, Russia sent more than 13 tons of medical supplies to Wuhan, Malaysia announced that it donated 18 million pairs of medical gloves to China, Germany sent 10,000 sets of protective clothing and other medical supplies to China, the United States donated 178 tons of medical supplies to China, and promised to provide additional $100 million in financial support to the affected countries and regions.
In March, China, Cuba, and Russia sent experts to Italy and sent medical supplies to help Italy cope with the new coronavirus outbreak.
Ma, an entrepreneur, shipped 1.1 million kits, 6 million masks and 60,000 protective clothing to Addis Ababa, the capital of Ethiopia, and was distributed by the African Union.
Ma later shipped 5,000 detection kits, 100,000 masks, and five ventilators to Panama.
In addition to this, Ma also donated medical supplies to Canada. The Netherlands, Spain, Turkey, Georgia and the Czech Republic expressed concerns about masks and kits made in China.
For example, Spain has withdrawn 58,000 Chinese-made coronavirus test kits with an accuracy rate of only 30 percent, and the Netherlands has recalled 600,000 defective Chinese masks.
Belgium has recalled 100,000 unusable masks, which are believed to come from China, but are actually from Colombia.
On the other hand, China's aid has gained popularity in Latin America and parts of Africa. On April 2, the World Bank launched an emergency support initiative for developing countries.
The World Health Organization praised the efforts made by the Chinese authorities in the prevention and control of the epidemic.
The World Health Organization noted the contrast between the SARS epidemic of 2002-2004 and the current crisis. During the SARS epidemic, the Chinese authorities were accused of concealing the truth, hindering prevention and containment efforts, and in this crisis, the central government "has begun to regularly update the situation before the Lunar New Year holiday to avoid panic."
On January 23, in response to the central government's decision to impose measures on the closure of the city of Wuhan, the representative of the World Health Organization said that although this is certainly not the recommendation of the World Health Organization, it also "fully reflects the determination to contain the spread of the epidemic from the areas where the epidemic is most concentrated", and called it "unprecedented in the history of public health". On January 30, the number of human-to-human phenomena and the number of cases in other countries was confirmed outside China.
The Director-General of the World Health Organization (WHO), Tedros, pointed out that public health emergencies of international concern are determined on the basis of “the risk of global transmission, especially to low- and middle-income countries with inadequate health systems”.
In response to the implementation of travel restrictions, Tedros said there was “no reason to take measures to interfere unnecessarily with international travel and trade” and that “the World Trade Organization does not recommend restrictions on trade and movement.”
On February 5, the World Health Organization called on the international community to contribute $675 million to provide funding for strategic readiness in low-income countries, saying that there is an urgent need to provide support to those countries that “do not have a system to test people infected with the virus.”
Tedros further issued a statement, declaring that "the weakest link determines how strong we are" and urging the international community to "immediately invest, otherwise the cost will be even greater in the future." On February 11, the World Health Organization confirmed at a press conference that the disease would be named COVID-19.
On the same day, Tedros said that UN Secretary-General António Guterres has agreed that "the United Nations will fully respond to the epidemic."
To this end, the United Nations launched the United Nations Crisis Management Group to coordinate the response of the United Nations as a whole. The World Health Organization (WHO) said it could "focus on the health response, while other agencies can also use its expertise to respond to the broader impact of the epidemic on society, economy and development."
On February 14, a joint Chinese mission led by the World Health Organization was launched to send international and WHO experts to China, to assist in responding to local epidemics and assessing the severity and contagiousness of the disease by convening seminars and meetings with key national institutions, and to assess the impact of “provincial and county-level response activities, including different situations in urban and rural areas.” On February 25, the World Health Organization announced that “the world should make greater efforts to fully prepare for a possible coronavirus pandemic,” noting that while it was too early to call it a pandemic, countries should still “go into a state of readiness.”
In response to Iran's growing epidemic, the World Health Organization sent a joint mission to the country to assess the situation. On February 28, WHO officials said that they had raised the global risk level of the coronavirus epidemic from "high" to "high", which is also the highest alert and risk level of the assessment method.
Mike Ryan, executive director of the World Health Organization’s health response program, warned in a statement: “It’s a reality check for governments around the world: wake up.
“The virus may be approaching, please be prepared” and urged governments to take the right response to avoid “worst cases” around the world.
Ryan further noted that current data is not enough for public health officials to announce access to a global pandemic, which would mean that “we basically acknowledge that everyone on the planet may be exposed to this virus.”
On March 11, the World Health Organization announced that the new coronavirus outbreak constitutes a global pandemic.
According to the Director-General of the World Health Organization (WHO), he expressed "deep concern over the spread and severity of the thought-provoking spread and the reality of inaction." The World Health Organization has come under serious criticism for its perceived mishandling of the global pandemic, including delays in declaring public health emergencies and classifying the virus as a global pandemic.
Among the more strongly opposed voices was a petition calling on the Director-General of the World Health Organization, Tedros Adhanom, to submit his resignation, which was signed by 733,000 people as of April 6.
Dozens of UN human rights experts stressed respect for everyone’s rights during the COVID-19 pandemic on March 26, 2020.
The Group notes that everyone is entitled to life-saving interventions, and that the Government should assume that responsibility.
The Expert Group stressed that a lack of resources or health insurance should in no way justify discrimination against specific groups.
The experts stressed that everyone enjoyed the right to health, including persons with disabilities, minors, older persons, internally displaced persons, the homeless, groups living in extremely poor conditions, detainees and refugees and other unspecified groups in need of government support.
International governmental organizations are actively responding to the economic and social impacts of the COVID-19 crisis.
The Organization for Economic Cooperation and Development (OECD) has launched a platform aimed at providing timely and comprehensive information and advice on policy responses from countries around the world.
From policies to strengthen health systems and the world economy to addressing the impact of lockdowns and travel restrictions, this digital center has a national policy tracking system designed to help countries learn from each other and promote global cooperation to address the challenges of the new coronavirus.
Due to the initial outbreak of the pandemic in Hubei Province of China, the United States, Michael Gove, Minister of the British Cabinet Office and Eduardo Bolsonaro, son of Brazilian President Jair Bolsonaro, criticized the Chinese government's handling.
Several CPC provincial administrators have been dismissed for mishandling anti-epidemic work in central China, reflecting the central government's dissatisfaction with the epidemic response of local political institutions.
Some commentators believe that this move is aimed at avoiding the public's dissatisfaction with Xi Jinping, the general secretary of the CPC Central Committee, due to the outbreak of the new coronavirus
Some Chinese officials, such as Zhao Lijian, have denied previously acknowledged that the COVID-19 outbreak began in Wuhan and supported conspiracy theories that the new coronavirus pneumonia originated in the United States or Italy.
U.S. U.S. President Donald Trump once referred to the new coronavirus as a “Chinese virus” or “Wuhan virus,” saying that China’s “censorship system has exacerbated the spread of the virus and turned it into the current global pandemic, which in turn has been perceived by some critics as a form of racism” and “deflected the focus on its government’s failure to contain the virus.”
The Daily Beast obtained a U.S. government telegram outlining a communications strategy apparently sent by the National Security Council, saying that “it’s all something that China doesn’t do.”
We’ve been told in any way, including press conferences and television appearances, to spread the message as much as possible.” Media outlets such as Politicians, Foreign Policy, and Bloomberg claimed that China’s assistance to affected countries was intended to promote its global influence.
Josep Borrell, the EU’s head of foreign policy, warned that there was a “geopolitical factor that involves vying for influence through good offices” and “generous politics”.
Borrel also said that “China, unlike the United States, is a responsible and reliable partner.”
China also called on the U.S. to lift sanctions on Syria, Venezuela and Iran, and reportedly provided assistance to the latter two countries.
On April 3, the 100,000 masks donated to Cuba were blocked by U.S. sanctions.
U.S. authorities have also been accused of diverting aid sent to other countries.
There have also been mask disputes between Germany, Austria and Switzerland, as well as between the Czech Republic and Italy.
In addition, Turkey intercepted hundreds of ventilators destined for Spain.
At the beginning of March, the Italian government criticized the EU's lack of support for Italy affected by the new coronavirus.
Maurizio Massari, Italy’s ambassador to the European Union, said: “Only China responded bilaterally.
Of course, this is not a good sign of European solidarity.”
On March 22, Russian President Vladimir Putin arranged for Russian troops to send military doctors, special disinfection vehicles and other medical equipment to Italy.
Italy's "News" quoted an anonymous "high-level political official" as saying that 80% of Russia's aid was "almost useless to Italy."
The source accused Russia of launching a “geopolitical and diplomatic” charismatic offensive.
The mayor of Lombardy, Attilio Fontana, and Italian Foreign Minister Luigi Di Maio dismissed the media reports and expressed their gratitude.
Russia also sent a cargo plane to the United States carrying medical aid.
Kremlin spokesman Dmitry Peskov said: “[Putin], when providing assistance to his American colleagues, believes that once U.S. manufacturers of medical equipment and materials have regained production capacity, they will pay off if necessary.”
The NATO Defenders 2020 military exercises planned in Germany, Poland, and the Baltics, the largest since the end of the Cold War, will be reduced in size.
Kate Hudson, secretary-general of the nuclear disarmament movement, criticized the "Defenders 2020" exercise: "At the moment of facing a public health crisis in the world, the exercise not only endangers the lives of the militaries of the United States and many European participating countries, but also endangers the residents of the countries where the exercises are carried out." The Iranian government has been severely affected by the epidemic, with about 24 parliamentarians and 15 other current or former politicians infected.
On March 14, 2020, Iranian President Hassan Rouhani wrote an open letter to world leaders asking for assistance. The letter noted that U.S. sanctions on Iran prevented Iran from accessing international markets, making it difficult for Iran to combat the epidemic. The outbreak prompted calls on the United States to adopt social policies common in other rich countries, including universal health care, universal child health care, paid family visits, and increased public health funding.
Political analysts expect that the outbreak could have a negative impact on whether Donald Trump can be re-elected in the 2020 presidential election, and diplomatic relations between Japan and South Korea have also worsened due to the global pandemic.
Japan announced that everyone entering from South Korea was required to undergo a two-week quarantine at government-designated locations, and South Korea subsequently criticized Japan's "murky and passive quarantine measures."
South Korean society initially showed a polarizing attitude towards President Moon Jae-in’s crisis response.
Many South Koreans have signed the petition, some have called for impeaging Moon, accusing the government of mishandling the outbreak, and others have praised his response strategy. The pandemic has prompted many countries to pass emergency legislation in response.
Some commentators worry that this could lead the government to tighten its grip on power.
In Hungary, parliament voted in favour of Prime Minister Viktor Orbán, who ruled indefinitely by decree suspending parliament and elections, which could also be punished if anyone was found to have disseminated false information about the virus and the handling of government crises.
Multiple supply shortages can be attributed to the new coronavirus outbreak, which stems from the increased use of equipment globally to combat the outbreak, panic shopping, and disruptions in factory and logistics operations.
The U.S. Food and Drug Administration has issued warnings about shortages of medicines and medical devices due to increased consumer demand and supply disruptions.
Panic shopping has occurred in many places, resulting in the emptiness of necessities such as food, toilet paper and bottled water on the shelves, resulting in a shortage of supply.
In particular, the technology industry has also been warning of delays in the delivery of electronic products.
World Health Organization Director-General Tedros said that the public demand for personal protective equipment has increased by 100 times.
This demand has led to a 20-fold increase in the price of such products than usual, and the supply of medical supplies has been delayed by 4 to 6 months.
In addition, there has been a global shortage of personal protective equipment. The World Health Organization has warned that this will pose a threat to health care workers.
In Australia, the pandemic has provided buyers with new opportunities to sell Australian products to China.
This activity led to a shortage of infant formula in some supermarkets, which was immediately banned by the Australian government. Although COVID-19 in northern Italy and Wuhan was very serious, there were no serious food shortages in these two places.
Measures taken by China and Italy in response to the hoarding of key products and illegal trade have been very successful, avoiding severe food shortages, which are expected to occur in Europe and North America.
Agricultural production in northern Italy has not plummeted, but industry representatives believe that prices are likely to rise.
The phenomenon of emptying food shelves is only temporary, and even in Wuhan, Chinese government officials have also released pork reserves to ensure the adequate nutrition of residents.
There are similar laws in Italy that require food producers to supply reserves for such emergencies.
Global economic damage has had an impact on China: On March 16, a media report said that in the first two months of 2020, China's economy suffered a huge impact due to the government's epidemic containment measures, and retail sales plummeted 20.5%.
Since mainland China is the world's main economic and manufacturing center, the virus outbreak is seen as posing a major threat to global economic stability.
Agathe Demarais of The Economist Intelligence Unit predicts that the market will remain in a state of instability until there is a clearer understanding of the possible consequences.
In January 2020, some analysts estimated that the negative impact of this outbreak on global economic growth may exceed the SARS epidemic of 2002-2004.
An expert at Washington University in St. Louis estimates that the impact of the outbreak on global supply chains will reach more than $300 billion, which may last up to two years.
According to reports, due to the decline in Chinese demand, the Organization of the Petroleum Exporting Countries (OPEC) is in a "difficult situation" after a sharp drop in oil prices.
On February 24, due to a significant increase in COVID-19 cases outside of mainland China, the global stock market fell.
On February 27, amid growing public concerns about the new coronavirus epidemic, various U.S. stock indexes, including the Nasdaq 100, the S&P 500, and the Dow Jones Industrial Average, saw their biggest declines since 2008, falling 1,191 points, the biggest one-day decline since the 2007-08 financial crisis.
The three major indexes fell more than 10 percent during the week.
On February 28, Scope Ratings GmbH confirmed China's sovereign credit rating, but maintained a negative outlook.
People's concerns about the new coronavirus have caused the stock market to plummet again, with the biggest drop occurring on March 16.
Many believe that a recession is a high probability event.
Economist Mohamed El-Erian praised central banks and states for taking timely and urgent measures.
Compared with the 2008 financial crisis, the central bank reacted more quickly.
Affected by travel bans, the closure of public places such as tourist attractions, and government recommendations to avoid the impact of travel, tourism has become one of the most affected industries.
Affected by this, many airlines have cancelled flights due to falling demand, including British Airways, China Eastern Airlines and Qantas Airlines, while British regional airline Flybe has unfortunately closed down.
The impact on the cruise industry has been unprecedented.
A number of railway stations and ferry ports have also been forced to close.
The epidemic coincides with the Spring Festival, due to the Chinese Spring Festival holiday, which was originally an important tourism season.
National and local governments have cancelled a series of events involving crowd gatherings, including the annual Spring Festival, where private companies also shut down shops and tourist attractions on their own, such as Hong Kong Disneyland and Shanghai Disneyland.
Many Lunar New Year activities and tourist attractions, including the Forbidden City and traditional temple fairs in Beijing, have been cancelled and closed to prevent large-scale crowd gatherings.
Among China’s 31 provinces, municipalities and autonomous regions, 24 regions have extended the New Year’s holiday until Feb. 10, requiring most businesses not to return to work until that date.
These areas account for 80 per cent of the country ' s gross domestic product and 90 per cent of exports.
Hong Kong, China, raised its infectious disease response level to the highest level and declared a state of emergency, with schools closed until March and New Year's celebrations cancelled. The retail industry was affected by the global impact, and store opening hours were shortened or temporarily closed.
Retail availability in Europe and Latin America declined by 40 percent.
Retail availability in North America and the Middle East declined by 50-60%.
In addition, the outbreak also led to a 33-43 percent drop in the shopping center’s passenger flow in March compared to February.
Mall operators around the world have taken additional steps, such as increasing sanitation facilities and installing thermal scanners to measure shoppers' body temperature, while also canceling some activities. According to estimates by the United Nations Economic Commission for Latin America, a pandemic-induced recession could result in an extremely poor population of 14 million to 22 million more in Latin America than in the absence of a pandemic.
In January and February 2020, at the peak of the epidemic in Wuhan, about 5 million people in China lost their jobs.
There are nearly 300 million migrant workers in China, many of whom are stranded in their homes in hinterlands or Hubei provinces. In March 2020, more than 10 million Americans lost their jobs and applied for government assistance.
According to estimates by the Federal Reserve Bank of St. Louis, the new coronavirus outbreak could lead to 47 million jobs in the U.S., with an unemployment rate likely to reach 32 percent. India's lockdown has led to tens of millions (daily paid) Indian migrant workers out of work. The Angus Reed Institute survey found that 44% of Canadian households have experienced some form of unemployment.
In the second half of March, 4 million French workers applied for temporary unemployment benefits, and 1 million British workers applied for general benefits. Nearly half a million companies in Germany applied a government-subsidized time-shift system for employees.
France and the United Kingdom have also adopted Germany's time-lapse work compensation system.
The performing arts and culture industry has also been hit hard by the epidemic, and the operation of organizations around the world, as well as employed employees and individuals, have not been spared.
Arts and cultural industry organizations strive to uphold their mission (often funded by the government), to showcase cultural heritage to the community, to maintain the safety of their employees and the public, and to support artists as much as possible.
By March 2020, museums, libraries, performing arts venues, and other cultural institutions around the world will be closed indefinitely to varying degrees, and original exhibitions, events and performances have been cancelled or postponed.
In order to deal with this situation, all parties have begun to vigorously provide alternative services through digital platforms. The recent outbreak will also lead to the cancellation of many religious services, major sports activities, and other social activities, such as music festivals, concerts, technical seminars, and fashion shows, and this negative impact will increase rapidly.
The film industry has also taken a hit. The Vatican has announced the cancellation of the commemoration of Holy Week in Rome, the last week of Christian Lente.
Many dioceses advised older Christians to stay at home without attending Mass on Sundays; some churches held liturgies by radio, live online, or on television, while others allowed worship in the car.
Due to the closure of its large and small churches in Roman Catholic districts, Christian pilgrims are not found in St. Peter's Square, and other religious groups have cancelled or restricted worship or limited public gatherings in churches, mosques, synagogues, temples and teachers.
Iran's health ministry announced the cancellation of Friday's prayers in areas affected by the outbreak, which was later forced to close, while Saudi Arabia banned foreign pilgrims and their residents from Mecca and Medina.
This pandemic has caused the worst damage to the world’s sporting agenda since World War II.
Most major sporting events have been cancelled or postponed, including the 2019-20 Champions League, 2019-20 Premier League, 2020 Champions League, 2019-20 NBA season and 2019-20 National Hockey Federation season.
The outburst disrupted plans for the 2020 Summer Olympics, which were originally scheduled for the end of July; the IOC announced on March 24 that it would “reschedule beyond 2020, but no later than the summer of 2021.” Casinos and other betting venues around the world have been closed, and live poker tournaments have been delayed or cancelled.
This, in turn, led many gamblers to turn to online gambling, and several online gambling sites reported a significant increase in their new registration rates. The entertainment industry has also been affected, with various music groups suspending or canceling tours.
Many large theaters, such as the Broadway theater, also suspended all performances.
Some artists have also managed to continue to create and share works over the Internet to replace traditional live performances, such as live concerts, or to create online “festivals” for artists to perform, distribute and promote their works.
On the Internet, many online fans with the theme of the new coronavirus have spread, and at the moment when uncertainty is spreading, many people have begun to turn to humor and divert attention.
Since the outbreak of COVID-19, there has been an increase in prejudice, xenophobia and racial discrimination against Chinese people, East Asians, and hotspots from Europe, the United States, and other countries.
Many countries and regions, especially in Europe, East Asia, North America, and the Asia-Pacific region, have experienced incidents of fear, suspicion and hatred.
Many reports in February, when most of the cases were still confined to China, recorded racist sentiments expressed by different groups around the world against the Chinese, arguing that they should be infected with the virus or accept so-called legitimate reprisals.
Some African countries have also experienced rising anti-China sentiment.
It is reported that many residents in Wuhan and Hubei have been subjected to geographical discrimination.
Both offline and online, there are voices to support the Chinese people and the residents of the severely affected areas.
As the epidemic spread to other hotspots, Italy became Europe's first COVID-19 country, and people from the country could also be affected by suspicion and xenophobia. Some citizens of Malaysia, New Zealand, Singapore, South Korea and other countries initially signed a petition calling for a ban on Chinese people entering their home countries in order to try to stop the spread of the disease.
In Japan, #ChineseDontComeToJapan, which was launched on Twitter, has spread widely.
Chinese people, as well as others in the United Kingdom and the United States, have reported an increase in racial abuse and attacks.
U.S. President Donald Trump has been criticized for referring to the new coronavirus as the “Chinese virus,” which critics say is racist and anti-China.
Ukrainian protesters attacked a bus from Wuhan to New Sanzari carrying Ukrainian and foreign evacuees.
Students from northeastern India (bordering China) and studying in major Indian cities have also reportedly been harassed by the new coronavirus outbreak.
Dilip Ghosh, chairman of the BJP West Bengal, said that the Chinese destroyed nature, “so God wants to retaliate against them”.
These remarks were later condemned by the Chinese consulate in Kolkata, calling it a "wrong mistake." In China, xenophobia and racial discrimination against non-Chinese residents have also been exacerbated by pandemics, with foreigners being described as "foreign garbage" that should be targeted for "garbage disposal".
Many newspapers with paywalls have removed paywall restrictions for some or all of their reports of the new coronavirus.
Many scientific publishers have publicly published scientific papers related to the epidemic.
Some scientists have also chosen to quickly share their findings on preprint servers such as bioRxiv.
Emerging Infectious Diseases – Infectious diseases caused by emerging pathogens, often in a different range or mode of transmission than in the past.
Globalization and Disease: An Overview of Globalization and the Spread of Disease.
Epidemics and Pandemic Inventories - List of Infectious Disease Deaths
Wildlife Smuggling and Zoonoids: Health Risks Related to Foreign Wildlife Trafficking
Laboratory testing methods for respiratory coronavirus disease (COVID-19) and related SARS-CoV-2 viruses include detecting the presence of the virus and detecting whether antibodies are produced after infection.
RT-PCR detects the RNA of the coronavirus and thus determines the presence of the virus in the sample.
This test is a specific detection of SARS-CoV-2 virus RNA.
It can be used to determine whether you have recently been infected or if you have an active infection.
Antibody testing (serum testing) can be used for diagnosis and group monitoring.
Antibody tests showed how many people had been infected with the virus, including those with mild symptoms that had not been reported or asymptomatic.
This test results can determine the accurate mortality rate of the disease, as well as the level of herd immunity in the population.
Due to the limited number of tests, as of March 2020, no country has been able to provide reliable data on the prevalence of the virus in its population.
As of March 23, there was no country that tested more than 3% of its population, and there was a huge difference in the number of tests completed by each country.
This difference may also have a serious impact on the reported case mortality rate, which in some countries can be grossly overestimated.
A real-time reverse transcription polymerase chain reaction (rRT-PCR) test using a swab in the throat can only be done within the first week after infection with the virus to obtain reliable results.
In general, the results can be obtained within a few hours to 2 days.
An RT-PCR test with a pharyngeal swab, which can only be completed within the first week of infection with the virus, will yield reliable results.
After that, the virus disappears in the throat, but continues to multiply in the lungs.
For infected people who are tested in the second week after infection, samples can be replaced with deep respiratory tract extracts obtained through sputum pipes, or the use of cough extracts (sputum).
RRT-PCR was released in January 2020 at the Charité Hospital in Berlin, one of the early PCR tests, and became the basis for 250,000 test kits distributed by the World Health Organization (WHO).
On January 23, 2020, the United Kingdom also developed a testing method. On January 28, 2020, South Korean company Kogenebiotech developed a PCR-based SARS-CoV-2 test kit (PowerChek Coronavirus).
It looks for all the "E" genes common to the β coronavirus, as well as RdRp genes specific to SARS-CoV-2. In China, BGI Group is one of the first companies to obtain emergency use approval from China's National Drug Administration for the production of PCR-based SARS-CoV-2 test kits. In the United States, the Centers for Disease Control and Prevention (CDC) has distributed real-time diagnostic panels for the 2019 novel coronavirus (2019-nCoV) to public health laboratories.
An earlier version of the test kit used three genetic tests, one of which resulted in an inability to determine the results due to reagent defects, while the Atlanta CDC experienced a detection bottleneck; this resulted in less than 100 samples successfully processed per day throughout February 2020.
The two-part test was not considered reliable until February 28, 2020, when state and local laboratories were allowed to begin testing.
This test was approved by the U.S. Food and Drug Administration under an emergency use authorization. The U.S. commercial laboratory began testing in early March 2020.
LabCorp has announced that RT-PCR-based COVID-19 testing will be available nationwide from March 5, 2020.
Quest Diagnostics has also announced nationwide COVID-19 testing starting March 9, 2020.
Quantity limits have not been declared; sample collection and processing must be performed in accordance with CDC requirements.
In Russia, COVID-19 testing is developed and produced by the National Center for Virology and Biotechnology (VECTOR).
On February 11, 2020, the test was registered with the Russian Federal Health Surveillance Agency. On March 12, 2020, the Mayo Clinic reportedly developed a kit to detect COVID-19 infection. On March 13, 2020, one of Roche Diagnostics tests was approved by the FDA, which can complete a large number of tests within 3.5 hours, so that a device can carry out about 4,128 tests within 24 hours.
On March 19, 2020, the FDA issued an Emergency Use Authorization (EUA) to Abbott’s m2000 system for testing to Abbott’s Abbot Laboratories; the FDA had previously issued similar authorizations to Hologic, LabCorp, and Thermo Fisher Scientific.
On March 21, 2020, Cepheid’s roughly 45-minute test also received an emergency use authorization from the FDA.
The FDA has approved a test that replaces PCR with nucleic acid isothermal amplification technology.
Because this method does not require a series of variable temperature cycles, positive results can be obtained in only 5 minutes, and negative results can be determined within 13 minutes.
There are currently about 18,000 such devices in the U.S., and Abbott is expected to ramp up production in order to be able to deliver 50,000 tests per day. Taiwan is developing a test that uses monoclonal antibodies (specially combined with nucleoshell protein (N protein) for the novel coronavirus), hoping that it will provide test results within 15 to 20 minutes like a rapid influenza test.
A literature review in March 2020 concluded that “in the early stages of the virus, the diagnostic value of the chest is very small, and the performance of CT [computed tomography] may be obvious, even before the symptoms appear.”
Typical features of CT include double-lung multileaf glass shadow, which has peripheral asymmetrical and posterior fabrics.
As the disease progresses, sub-pleural manifestations, gravel signs, and pulmonary changes will be developed.
A study comparing PCR and CT in Wuhan, where the initial outbreak of the current pandemic occurred, showed that CT was much more sensitive than PCR, but CT was less accurate, and many of its imaging characteristics overlapped with other pneumonias and diseases.
As of March 2020, the American College of Radiology recommended that “CT should not be used to screen for COVID-19 or as a first-line testing tool for diagnosing COVID-19.” As of March 2020, the CDC recommended the use of PCR for initial screening.
Part of the immune response to infection is to produce antibodies, including IgM and IgG.
These antibodies can be used to detect infection in individuals about 7 days after the onset of symptoms in order to determine immunity in the monitored population. Assays can be performed in a central laboratory (CLT) or either through instant testing (PoCT).
This can be measured in high-throughput automated systems in many clinical laboratories, but their availability depends on the productivity of each system.
For CLT, although the immune response can be tracked by sequence samples, it is usually measured using a peripheral blood sample.
For PoCT, a blood sample is typically obtained using skin piercings.
There is no need to perform extraction steps prior to the assay, which is different from the PCR method. On March 26, 2020, the FDA approved 29 entity-declared tests that are now capable of distributing their antibody test kits.
As of April 7, 2020, the FDA had approved only one test based on an emergency use authorization. In late March 2020, the test kits of Oumon Medical Experimental Diagnosis Company and Epitope Diagnostics were approved in Europe, and these kits can detect IgG and IgA antibodies against viruses in blood samples.
Hundreds of samples can be detected within hours of detection, so the determination of viral RNA is much faster than traditional PCR determination.
In general, antibodies can be detected 14 days after the start of the infection. In early April, the United Kingdom found that none of the antibody test kits it purchased were good enough.
Hong Kong has established a mechanism for suspected patients to stay at home, "the emergency room provides patients with a sample test tube", and the patient vomites the saliva into the test tube, and then sends the test tube back, and later receives the results. The British NHS announced that it is piloting a home-to-home test for suspected cases, so as to eliminate the risk of infecting others when patients go to the hospital, and can also eliminate the risk that patients must disinfect the ambulance after using an ambulance.
In Germany, the National Association of Statutory Health Insurance Physicians said on March 2 that non-hospital institutions have the ability to do about 12,000 tests a day, compared to 10,700 in the previous week.
If the test is carried out at the request of the doctor, the cost is borne by health insurance.
According to the director of the Robert Koch Institute in Germany, the total weekly detection capacity in Germany is 160,000.
As of March 19th, no drop-out testing has been provided in a number of major cities.
As of March 26, 2020, the total number of tests conducted in Germany is unknown, as only the number of people tested positive was reported.
According to an initial laboratory survey, as of the 12th week of 2020, at least 483,295 samples (including the 12th week of 2020) had been tested for at least 33,491 (6.9 percent) positive for SARS-CoV-2. In Israel, researchers at Technion and Rambam Hospital developed and tested a method for simultaneously detecting a sample of 64 patients, that is, a summary sample, which can only be tested further when the combined sample was found to be positive.
The laboratory was completed in five days under the supervision of Wang Jian, the founder of BGI, and the model shows that if the above detection capacity is not reached, the cases in Hubei will be 47% higher, and the corresponding processing and isolation costs will be doubled.
After the Wuhan laboratory, Shenzhen, Tianjin, Beijing and Shanghai immediately set up fire eye laboratories, and a total of 12 cities in China have established this laboratory.
As of March 4, 2020, the daily flux for laboratory tests totaled 50,000 copies per day. Origami Assays released an open-source, multi-route design that performed COVID19 tests on up to 1,122 patient samples with only 93 measurements. These balanced designs can operate in small laboratories without the need for an automatic pipette.
As of March, the shortage of reagents and insufficient numbers have become obstacles to large-scale testing in the EU, the United Kingdom and the United States.
As a result, some promoters have explored sample preparation solutions, which involve heating samples to 98 °C (208 °F) for 5 minutes to release RNA genomes for further testing. The United Arab Emirates announced on March 31 that it has now tested more of its population for new coronavirus than in any other country, and is expanding the level of testing to allow a large number of people to be tested.
The way to achieve this detection capability is to use drive-through testing on the one hand, and on the other hand to fund the establishment of high-throughput laboratories to deal with the needs of large-scale population testing from Group 42 and BGI (based on its “fire eye” emergency testing laboratory in China).
Completed within 14 days, the laboratory is capable of tens of thousands of RT-PCR tests per day, making it the world’s first laboratory of this size operating outside of China.
China, France, Germany, Hong Kong, Japan and the United States have all developed different detection methods for various parts of the genetic map of coronaviruses.
The World Health Organization has adopted the method of producing test kits in Germany, sending kits to low-income countries that do not have the resources to develop their own kits.
Germany's approach was unveiled on January 17, 2020; programs developed by the U.S. Centers for Disease Control and Prevention were available after Jan. 28, limiting U.S. detection capabilities. Both China and the U.S. encountered test kit reliability issues early in the outbreak, and both countries and Australia were unable to provide sufficient test kits to meet demand and testing recommendations from health experts.
In contrast, experts believe that South Korea's extensive testing is very helpful in reducing the spread of the new coronavirus.
Thanks to the testing capacity established by the South Korean government in the past few years, it is mainly concentrated in laboratories in the private sector.
On March 16, the World Health Organization (WHO) called for an increase in testing as the best way to slow the development of the COVID-19 epidemic. The widespread spread of the virus has led to a large demand for testing, which has led to a backlog of hundreds of thousands of tests in private laboratories in the United States, and the supply of swabs and chemical reagents has also begun to become strained.
In March 2020, China reported a problem with the accuracy of its test kits.
In the United States, test kits developed by the CDC had "deficiencies"; subsequently, the government removed bureaucratic hurdles hindering testing in private laboratories. Spain purchased test kits from the Chinese company Shenzhen Yirui Biotechnology Co., Ltd. and found that the test results were inaccurate.
The company explained that inaccuracies in the test results may have been caused by the incorrect collection of samples or the incorrect use of test kits.
Spain's cabinet has said it will recycle kits that have returned erroneous results and replace them with another kit supplied by Shenzhen Bioeasy. 80% of the test kits purchased by the Czech Republic from China have incorrect results. Slovakia purchased 1.2 million test kits from China, and it was found to be inaccurate.
Prime Minister Matovič suggested that these test boxes should be thrown into the Danube. Ateş Kara of the Turkish Ministry of Health said that Turkey's detection boxes purchased from China had a "high error rate" and did not "put them into use." The UK purchased 3.5 million detection boxes from China and announced in April 2020 that they could not be used.
Tests are carried out, and people who have tested positive are quarantined and traced the contact history of SARS-CoV-2-positive people to obtain positive results.
Researchers working in the Italian city of Vò (the site of death of the first case of COVID-19 patients in Italy) conducted two rounds of testing on groups of about 3,400 people, about ten days apart.
About half of them tested positive and asymptomatic, and all of the detected cases had been isolated.
New infections have been completely eliminated through the adoption of travel restrictions on communities.
Through large-scale tracing of contacts, restrictions on inbound travel, detection and isolation, the 2020 coronavirus is spreading far more slowly in Singapore than in other developed countries, and Singapore does not impose extreme restrictions, such as the mandatory closure of restaurants and retail locations.
Many activities have been cancelled in Singapore, and residents have been advised to stay at home since March 28, but the school will resume classes on time on March 23 after the holidays.
Several other countries, such as Ireland and South Korea, have also contained large-scale disease epidemics through large-scale contact tracing, restrictions on inbound travel, detection and isolation, and less aggressive lockdowns.
A statistical study found that countries with the greater the number of tests compared to the number of deaths, the lower their case mortality rate may be due to the fact that these countries are better able to detect groups with only mild or asymptomatic symptoms.
WHO recommends that countries that do not have testing capacity and have limited experience with COVID-19 at the national level send their first five positive and top ten negative COVID-19 samples to one of the 16 WHO reference laboratories for confirmation testing.
Of the 16 participating laboratories, seven are located in Asia, five in Europe, two in Africa, one in North America and one in Australia.
In the chart below, the “test positive %” list is affected by the detection policy of the host country.
In all other cases, compared with countries that test all citizens (whether they show symptoms or not), countries that only test for hospitalization have a higher rate of positive detection.
Washing hands, also known as hand cleaning, is the act of cleaning your hands to remove dust, grease, microorganisms, or other useless substances from your hands.
Handwashing with soap at certain “critical moments” of the day prevents the spread of many diseases, such as diarrhoea and cholera, which can be transmitted through fecal-oral channels.
If people do not wash their hands before touching their eyes, nose, or mouth (i.e. mucous membranes), they may also be infected with respiratory diseases, such as the flu or the common cold.
There are five key moments to wash your hands with soap throughout the day: before and after defecation, after cleaning a child’s hips or diapers, before feeding the child, before, before and after handling food, or before and after handling raw meat, fish, or poultry.
If there is no soap and water, you can clean your hands with grass ash. The World Health Organization recommends washing your hands in the following situations:
Prepare the food before, during and after.
Take care of the patient.
After replacing the diapers or cleaning the buttocks of children.
Wipe your nose, cough, or sneeze.
After touching animals, animal food, or animal feces.
Hand hygiene refers to hygiene practices related to medical procedures.
Washing your hands before taking medication or taking medical care can prevent or minimize the spread of the disease.
In the medical field, the main purpose of handwashing is to remove pathogens (bacteria, viruses or other microorganisms that can cause disease) and chemicals that may harm the human body or cause disease on their hands.
Handwashing is especially important for people who are engaged in food processing or working in the medical field, but it is also a very important practice for the public.
There are many health benefits of handwashing, including minimizing the spread of influenza, coronavirus and other infectious diseases; preventing infectious diarrhoea; and reducing respiratory infections.
It also reduces the infant mortality rate at home.
A 2013 study showed that improving handwashing habits contributed slightly to an increase in the height of children under the age of five.
In developing countries, child mortality related to respiratory and diarrhoeal diseases can be reduced by advocating for simple behavioural changes (e.g., washing hands with soap).
This simple behavior can reduce the mortality rate of these diseases by nearly 50%.
Promoting handwashing can reduce the incidence of diarrhoea by about one-third, which is equivalent to the provision of clean water sources in low-income areas.
Handwashing with soap can reduce diarrhoeal events by 48%. As spontaneous behavior in homes, schools, and communities around the world, handwashing with soap is the most effective and inexpensive way to prevent diarrhoea and acute respiratory infections (ARI).
Pneumonia, as a major acute respiratory infection, is the leading cause of death in children under the age of five, claiming the lives of about 1.8 million children every year.
Diarrhoea and pneumonia together kill nearly 3.5 million children each year.
Compared with any single vaccine or medical intervention, turning handwashing with soap before meals and after the toilet can save more lives, reducing diarrhoea deaths by nearly half and reducing deaths from acute respiratory infections by a quarter.
As part of the Water Supply, Sanitation and Hygiene (WASH) program, other sanitation interventions are often used while advocating for handwashing.
Handwashing can also prevent puscle disease transmitted through direct physical contact.
Washing hands can have a slight adverse effect, that is, frequent hand washing can lead to dry skin, which can cause skin damage.
A 2012 Danish study found that excessive hand washing can lead to itching and cracking of the skin, known as hand eczema or hand dermatitis, which is particularly common among healthcare workers.
Excessive handwashing is also seen as one of the symptoms of OCD.
In order to reduce the fecal-oral transmission of the disease, it is necessary to wash your hands with soap at five critical moments throughout the day: after the toilet (urinating, defecating), after cleaning a child’s hips (replaying diapers), feeding the child before, before, before and after handling food, or after handling raw meat, fish, or raw poultry.
In order to prevent the spread of the disease, hands should also be washed in the correct way in the following situations: before and after treating incisions or wounds; sneezing, coughing, or wiping their noses; touching animal feces or handling animals; and after touching the garbage.
In many countries/regions, the rate of handwashing with soap is very low.
A 2015 study of handwashing in 54 countries found that an average of 38.7 per cent of households had a habit of handwashing with soap. A 2014 study showed that Saudi Arabia had the highest rate at 97 percent; the U.S. was close to an average of 77 per cent; and China had the lowest rate at 23 per cent.
The Ministry of Education’s “basic health care plan” is an example of large-scale initiatives to improve children’s health and schooling.
At the heart of this national plan is deworming twice a year, supplemented by daily hand washing with soap and brushing your teeth with fluoride toothpaste.
In Indonesia, the plan was also successfully implemented.
The water is incorporated with soaps or cleaners to better remove microorganisms from the skin.
The main role of soaps and detergents is to reduce dissolving barriers and improve solubleness.
Using water alone does not effectively cleanse the skin because fats and proteins (an integral part of organic soil) are not easily dissolved in water.
However, washing hands requires reasonable water...
Because solid soap can be reused, it is possible to retain the bacteria left behind when used before.
A small number of studies have investigated the transfer of bacteria from contaminated solid soap, and concluded that the bacteria cannot be transferred because the bacteria will be washed away by bubbles.
However, the CDC still claims that “it is best to use non-contact liquid soap.”
Antibacterial soap has been vigorously promoted in the health-conscious population.
So far, there is no evidence that antibiotic-resistant microorganisms in nature have been selected using recommended antimicrobials or disinfectants.
However, antibacterial soaps contain common antibacterial agents such as trichlorosand, which have a large number of drug-resistant microbial strains.
Therefore, even if antibacterial soap is not selected for antibiotic-resistant strains, antibacterial soap may not be as effective as advertised.
In addition to surfactants and skin care agents, complex formulations may also contain acids (acetic acid, ascorbic acid, lactic acid), antibacterial active benzoic acid, and more skin conditioning agents (ale vera, vitamins, menthols, plant extracts). A comprehensive analysis conducted by the School of Public Health at the University of Oregon shows that ordinary soaps have the same effect as consumer-based antibacterial soaps containing triclosan in terms of preventing disease and removing bacteria from the hands.
Hot water is comfortable to wash, but its heat is not enough to kill bacteria.
Bacteria grow very quickly at body temperatures (37 °C).
However, when it comes to removing natural oils that contain soil and bacteria, warm soapy water is more effective than cold soapy water.
Scientific research has confirmed that the use of warm water has no effect on reducing microorganisms on the hands, contrary to the prevailing view.
Water-free sanitizers or non-cleansing sanitizers are non-hydrogen-based hand cleaners.
In the late 1990s and early 21st century, non-water-based alcoholic hand cleansers (also known as alcohol-based hand-rushing fluids, bactericidal-free hand sanitizers, or hand sanitizers) became popular.
Most of the formulations of this type of hand sanitizer contain isopropyol or ethanol, and then add thrift-forming gels such as cabom (acrylic polymers), or add moisturizers such as glycerin to form liquids, or are foam-like to be easy to use, and to relieve the dryness of the skin caused by alcohol.
Adding diluted hydrogen peroxide can further increase antimicrobial activity. Hand-free hand sanitizers containing at least 60% to 95% of alcohol can be effectively sterilized.
Alcohol rubs can kill bacteria, multi-pharmaceutical resistant bacteria (Mexicillin Staphylococccoccus aureus (MRSA) and anti-Vancomytomycoccus (VRE), tuberculosis and some viruses (including HIV, herpes, respiratory syncytial virus (RSV), rhinoviruses, bovine pox, influenza, and fungi.
After 30 seconds of use, 70 percent of the alcohol rubber can kill 99.97% of the bacteria on your hands (3.5 orders of magnitude reduced, similar to 35 decibels), and 99.99% to 99.999% (4 to 5 orders of magnitude) on the hands after 1 minute of rubbing the hand. Hand sanitizer is the most effective for bacteria, and for some viruses it is slightly less effective.
Alcohol hand hydration is almost completely ineffective against viruses such as norovirus (also known as Norwalk) (the most common cause of infectious gastroenteritis). It is necessary to use adequate hand sanitizer or alcohol hand sanitizer to thoroughly moisturize or cover both hands.
Wash your hands and hands, the back of your hands, as well as between your fingers, and the ends of all your fingers for about 30 seconds until the liquid, foam, or gel is dried.
The fingertips must also be adequately washed, and the fingertips must be washed on both palms. The U.S. Centers for Disease Control and Prevention recommends that it is best to wash your hands, rather than using no-wash hand sanitizer, especially when it looks dirty.
Due to the ease of use and rapid inactivation of microorganisms, this type of hand-rupting liquid is increasingly used; however, they should not be substituted for proper handwashing unless there is no soap and water available for use.
Frequent use of alcohol-free hand sanitizers can lead to dry skin unless emollients and/or moisturizers are added to the recipe.
Glycerine and/or other emollients can be added to the recipe to reduce or eliminate the dryness of the skin caused by alcohol.
In clinical trials, alcoholic hand sanitizers containing emollients can significantly reduce skin irritation and the occurrence of dry skin compared to soaps or antibacterial cleansers.
Allergic exposure to dermatitis, exposure to hives syndrome, or allergies to alcohol or additives in alcohol rupture is very rare.
Compared with washing hands with soap and water, alcohol hand sanitizer is less likely to induce irritating contact dermatitis, which is one reason for its attractiveness.
Although waterless detergent is effective, it does not remove organic substances from the hands, but only disinfects them.
As a result, hand sanitizer is not as effective as soap and water in preventing the spread of many pathogens, because these pathogens are still in the hands.
The efficacy of non-alcoholic hand sanitizer depends largely on ingredients and formulations, and its effect has historically been much worse than that of alcohol and alcohol rubbing.
More recently, formulations using ammonium benza chloride have been shown to have sustained, cumulative antimicrobial activity after use, which, unlike alcohol, has been shown to be less effective after reuse, possibly due to progressive adverse skin reactions.
Many people in low-income neighborhoods can’t afford soap and can be replaced with grass ash or soil.
Grass ash or soil works better than washing hands with water alone, but it is not as good as soap.
One of the problems is that if earth or grass ash is contaminated by microorganisms, it may accelerate the spread of the disease, rather than slowing the spread.
As with soap, grass ash is also a disinfectant, because it will form an alkaline solution after encountering water.
The WHO recommends that if soap is not available, soap can be substituted with graft ash or sand.
U.S. Centers for Disease Control and Prevention recommends the following steps to prevent the spread of the disease:
Wet your hands with flowing warm water or cold water.
It is recommended to use running water because the vertical basin may be contaminated, and the water temperature does not seem to have much to do with it.
The use of sufficient amount of soap is repeatedly washed to produce foam, while cleaning the back of the hand, between the fingers and the cracks of the nails.
Soap removes bacteria from the skin, and studies have shown that people tend to wash their hands more thoroughly when washing their hands with soap instead of water alone.
Wash for at least 20 seconds.
Washing can create friction, which helps to remove bacteria from the skin, and the longer you wash, the more bacteria are removed.
Completely cleaned with running water.
Washing hands with water basins can re-contamination.
Dry with a clean towel or dry naturally.
Moisturity in the hands is easier to re-contaminate. Thumbs, wrists, areas between fingers, and nail slits are the most commonly overlooked areas.
Artificial nails and cracked nail polish may hide microbes.
It is usually recommended to use moisturizing lotions to avoid dry hands; dry skin can cause skin damage, thereby increasing the risk of infection transmission.
Where tap water and/or soap are not available in developing countries, a variety of low-cost measures can be provided to facilitate handwashing, for example, by pouring water through a hanging bucket or horoused gourd, and/or by washing hands (if necessary). Water-saving hand-washing programmes, such as simple faucets and other low-cost methods, can be used where water supplies are limited (e.g. schools or rural areas in developing countries).
The technique of a simple faucet is very simple, hanging a water tank with a rope, and then pouring a small amount of water into the hand through a foot pedal, and then using soap.
Drying hands effectively is an important step in the hand hygiene process, but there is still some debate about the most effective ways to do it in public health.
More and more studies have shown that paper towels are more hygienic than the electric dry mobile phones provided in many toilets.
In 2008, the University of Westminster in London conducted a study sponsored by the European Tissue Symposium in the tissue industry, comparing the level of hygiene of paper towels, warm air dry mobile phones, and more modern spray dry mobile phones.
After washing his hands, he used a warm air-dried mobile phone to dry his hands, and found that the total amount of bacteria on the fingers increased by an average of 194%, and the total amount of bacteria on the hand increased by an average of 254%.
With the spray dry mobile phone, the total amount of bacteria on the fingers increased by an average of 42%, and the total amount of bacteria on the palm increased by an average of 15%.
After washing hands with paper towels, the total amount of bacteria on the fingers is reduced by an average of 76%, and the total number of palm bacteria is reduced by an average of 77%. The scientists also carried out various tests to determine whether a variety of dry hand methods may cause cross-infection of other bathroom users and bathroom facilities.
It is said that the jet dry mobile phone sprays the air at 180 meters per second (650 km/h; 400 miles per hour), which can blow away the microorganisms on the hands and the fuselage, which may pollute other bathroom users and toilet facilities within 2 meters.
The use of warm air dry mobile phones can transmit microorganisms 0.25 meters away from dry phones.
The results from the use of paper towels showed that there would be no serious microbial transmission. In 2005, TÜV Produkt und Umwelt conducted a study to evaluate different ways of working.
The following changes in the number of bacteria were observed after drying:
The study, which involved many different manufacturers of dry phones, compared these dry phones with dry hands with paper towels.
During the trip, in the absence of soap and water, you can choose to wash your hands with hand disinfectant wipes.
The alcohol content of alcohol-free sanitizer should reach at least 60%.
Ignaz Semmelweis, a Hungarian doctor, discovered in 1846 that handwashing prevented the spread of diseases in hospitals, but it took a long time before it was mandatory for medical staff to wash their hands.
Hospitals use electronic devices that can provide feedback to alert hospital staff when they forget to wash their hands.
One study found a decrease in infection rates after using these devices.
The medical staff wash their hands for at least 15 seconds, using sufficient amounts of soap and water or gels to create foam and rub various parts of handwashing.
The fingers of the hands are rubbed against each other.
If there is residue between the nail seams, it can be removed with a hard brush.
Since there may still be bacteria left in the remaining moisture on your hands, it must be fully cleaned and dried with a clean towel.
After drying, the faucet should be turned off using a tissue (if necessary, open the exit door with a paper towel).
This prevents re-contamination of the hands as a result of exposure to these surfaces.
In health facilities, the purpose of handwashing is to eliminate pathological microorganisms (“bacteria”) and to avoid transmission.
A report in the New England Journal of Medicine shows that in most medical settings, there are still situations where handwashing does not reach an acceptable level, and a large number of health care workers often forget to wash their hands before touching patients, resulting in microbial transmission.
One study showed that proper handwashing, coupled with other simple procedures, could reduce catheter-associated blood flow infections by 66 percent. The World Health Organization has published a worksheet that demonstrates standard methods of handwashing and rubbing at health facilities.
The organization has also published on its website a draft of hand hygiene guidelines to obtain public comments.
Whitby and others conducted a related review.
If regulatory compliance needs to be confirmed, commercial equipment can be measured and verified by counterpart health.
The World Health Organization recommends “five moments” for handwashing:
After exposure to blood/body fluids
Prior to sterile operations and
After taking care of the patient. Adding antibacterial chemicals to soaps (“pocropine soaps” or “antibacterial soaps”) can allow sanitizers to have bactericidal effects.
This bactericidal operation may be required before surgery or in an environment with a large number of antibiotic-resistant microorganisms. When "wiping" your hands for surgery, there must be a faucet that can be turned on and off without contact, a chlorhexidine or iodine hand sanitizer, sterile towels used to dry your hands, sterile brushes for scrubbing, and other sterile tools for cleaning nail seams.
All jewelry should be removed.
This procedure requires washing hands, forearms to the elbows, and is usually washed for 2–6 minutes.
It doesn’t take a long time to wash (10 minutes).
When rinsing, it is necessary to prevent the water on the forearm from flowing back to the hands.
After washing your hands, use a sterile cloth to dry your hands and put on surgical clothing.
In order to reduce the spread of bacteria, it is best to wash hands before and after contact with the patient, or to use non-sterilization hand sanitizer.
In order to control staphylococcus infection in hospitals, it has been found that the biggest benefit of cleaning the hands comes from the fact that when the frequency of handwashing reaches 20%, the further benefit is extremely limited when the frequency of cleaning hands increases to more than 35%.
Compared to the use of antibacterial soaps, the use of ordinary soap to wash hands leads to more than three times the proportion of bacterial infectious diseases transmitted to food. After comparing the use of alcohol-rubbed hand-washing with antibacterial soap (washing for 30 seconds respectively), it was found that the proportion of bacterial infections with alcohol-rubbed hand hydration was 26% lower than that of antibacterial soap.
However, soap and water are more effective than alcohol hand rubs for reducing H1N1 influenza (HI1N1 influenza A) viruses and Clostridium difficile spores, which are more effective than alcoholic hand rubs. Hand hygiene can be improved by training employees on how to wash their hands, increasing the supply of alcohol rubbling fluid, and providing written and oral reminders to employees.
For which interventions are most effective in a variety of medical settings, further research is needed.
In developing countries, handwashing with soap is considered an important tool that is cost-effective to achieve good health and even good nutrition.
However, the lack of reliable water supplies, soap or hand-washing facilities in people’s homes, schools and workplaces has made widespread handwashing a challenge.
For example, in most rural areas of Africa, it is very rare to have faucets in every private bathroom or near public toilets, even if there are cheap ways to build handwashing desks.
However, the low frequency of handwashing may also be caused by entrenched habits, rather than due to the lack of soap or water.
Promoting and advocating the use of soap for handwashing can influence policy-making, raise awareness of the benefits of washwashing, and lead to long-term behavioural changes in residents.
In order to be effective, monitoring and evaluation must be carried out.
A systematic assessment of 70 studies found that community-based approaches were effective in increasing handwashing in low- and middle-income countries (LMICs), while social marketing campaigns were less effective. An example of advocating for handwashing in schools was the “Samsung Approach” introduced by UNICEF, which, among other hygiene requirements, encouraged schools to take simple and inexpensive steps to ensure that students wash their hands with soap.
When the minimum standard is reached, the school can rise from one star to the highest three stars.
Handwashing campaigns may include the construction of handwashing stations to reduce disease and child mortality.
Another example of handwashing awareness is World Handwashing Day, which aims to try to achieve behavioural changes. As a result of the 2019-20 coronavirus pandemic, UNICEF promoted the use of an emoji for handwashing.
Very few studies have examined the relationship between the overall cost-effectiveness of handwashing in developing countries and the avoidance of disability-adjusted life years (DALY).
However, there have been reports of more cost-effectiveness in advocating for handwashing with soap than with other water and sanitation interventions.
The first to recognize the importance of handwashing to human health – especially to those who were physically weak (e.g., mothers or wounded soldiers who had just given birth in hospitals), were two pioneers in hand hygiene in the mid-19th century: Ignaz Semmelweis, a Hungarian physician working in Vienna, Austria, and Florence Nightingale, founder of Modern Nursing.
At that time, most people still thought that the infection was caused by a foul odor known as misture.
In the 1980s, foodborne disease outbreaks and medical infections prompted the U.S. Centers for Disease Control and Prevention to more actively advocate hand hygiene, emphasizing that hand hygiene is an important way to prevent the spread of infection.
The 2009 swine flu outbreak and the 2020 COVID-19 pandemic have made many countries more aware of the importance of handwashing with soap to protect people from such infectious diseases.
Germany, for example, hangs “correct handwashing” posters next to handwashing ponds in public toilets, office buildings and toilets at the airport.
The idiom “wash your hands” means that someone declares an unwillingness to take responsibility for something or is unwilling to participate in something.
It is derived from what is written in the Gospel of the Gospel of Matthew, where Pontius Pilate washed his hands after deciding to crucify Jesus, and later evolved into widely used idioms in some English-speaking groups.
In Shakespeare’s Macbeth, Lady Macbeth begins to forcibly wash her hands in an attempt to wash her imaginary stains, indicating that she feels guilty about the crimes she has committed and the crimes that her husband has committed.
Studies have shown that people who think of or envision unethical behavior tend to wash their hands more often than others, and tend to value handwashing facilities more often.
In addition, those who can wash their hands after consecuting unethical behavior are less likely to engage in other “purified” compensatory actions, such as volunteering.
Religions ask for handwashing for hygiene and symbolic purposes. Shining with water but without soap is part of many of the religion's signature hand-washing rituals, including the Bahá'í faith, Hinduism, tevilah in Judaism, and netilat yadayim cleansing rituals, handwashing rituals in Christianity, and a small purity in Islam. Religion also has hygiene regulations, especially after certain behaviors.
Hinduism, Judaism and Islam require washing their hands after access to toilets.
Hinduism, Buddhism, Sikhism, Judaism, and Islam require washing hands before and after meals.
COVID-19 Prevention and Control in the Workplace
COVID-19 workplace prevention and control refers to the use of dangerous occupational safety and health measures to prevent and control coronavirus disease 2019 (COVID-19).
The appropriateness of workplace risk prevention and control depends on the specific work site and work tasks, and should be determined based on the risk assessment of the source of the exposure, the severity of the disease in the community, and the risk factors for susceptible individuals who may be exposed to COVID-19.
According to the U.S. Occupational Safety and Health Administration (OSHA), low-exposure jobs have the least exposure to the public and other colleagues, so it is recommended to take basic infection precautions, including washing hands frequently, encouraging employees to stay at home after illness, observing respiratory etiquette, and daily cleaning and disinfection of the working environment.
Moderate-exposure risk work involves the need for regular or close contact with unknown or suspected COVID-19 patients who may be infected as a result of ongoing community transmission or international travel.
This includes people who are exposed to the general public, such as schools, high-density working environments, and some large-scale retail environments.
In addition to basic infection prevention measures, this group should also use high-efficiency air filters and sneezing protective devices for ventilation, wearing personal protective equipment and taking other risk prevention and control measures when exposed to COVID-19 patients.
OSHA believes there is a high risk of exposure to health care workers and tremor workers who come into contact with known or suspected COVID-19 patients, and the risk of exposure becomes extremely high if the staff performs an operation to produce an aerosol in a known or suspected COVID-19 patient, or if specimens are collected or processed from them.
Dangerous prevention and control measures applicable to such personnel include engineering controls, such as negative pressure ventilators and personal protective equipment suitable for their tasks.
The COVID-19 outbreak can have a serious impact in the workplace.
Workers may be absent due to illness, the need to take care of others, or the fear of exposure.
There may also be changes in business models, including changes in commodity demand, or changes in access to those goods (e.g., shopping during off-peak hours, or using delivery or drop-off delivery services).
Finally, the logistics of products from COVID-19 outbreak areas may be disrupted. Infectious disease prevention and response plans can be used to guide conservation actions.
These programs address the levels of risk associated with a variety of workplace and work tasks, including sources of exposure, risk factors from family and community settings, and risk factors for individual workers, such as the elderly or groups with chronic diseases.
These plans also outline the prevention and control measures needed to address these risks, as well as contingency plans for situations that may occur as a result of an outbreak.
Infectious disease prevention and response plans may need to comply with national or local recommendations.
Responsibilities to respond to the outbreak include reducing inter-employee transmission, protecting people at higher risk of adverse health complications, maintaining business operations, and minimizing adverse impacts on other entities in the supply chain.
In addition, the response measures will also be affected by the severity of the disease in the communities in which the business is located.
The level of hazard prevention and control is a widely used prevention and control framework in occupational safety and health, which is used to group risk prevention and control measures according to effectiveness.
If the hazards of COVID-19 cannot be completely eliminated, the most effective control measures are engineering controls, followed by administrative controls, and finally personal protective equipment.
Engineering control refers to isolating employees from work-related hazards, rather than relying on employee behavior control, which is also the least costly solution for implementation.
Administrative control refers to the change of work policy or procedure that requires the employee or employer to take action on its own.
The prevention and control effect of personal protective equipment (PPE) is lower than engineering control and administrative control, but helps to prevent some exposures.
All types of PPE must be selected according to the hazard level faced by the staff, equipped according to applicable conditions (such as respirators), and must always be worn correctly, regularly inspected and maintained, replaced if necessary, and properly unloaded, cleaned, stored or treated to avoid contamination.
According to the U.S. Occupational Safety and Health Administration (OSHA), low-exposure jobs have the fewest occupational exposures to the public and other colleagues.
It is recommended that all workplaces take basic infection precautions, including washing hands frequently, advising sick employees to observe home isolation, observing breathing etiquette when coughing or sneezing, providing paper towels and garbage cans, preparing for telecommuting if necessary, dissuading employees from using other people's tools and equipment, and maintaining daily cleaning and disinfection in the workplace.
The timely identification and isolation of potentially contagious individuals is key to protecting people, customers, visitors, and others in the workplace.
The U.S. Centers for Disease Control and Prevention (CDC) recommends that employees exhibiting acute respiratory symptoms should be quarantined at home for at least 24 hours until it is determined that there are no fever, fever signs, or any other symptoms, the sick leave policy should be implemented flexibly, allowing employees to take care of sick family members at home, and should clearly inform employees of this policy.
Depending on OSHA’s regulations, moderate-exposure work includes the need for frequent or close contact with unknown or suspected COVID-19 patients within 6 feet (1.8 meters), but may result in individuals being infected with SARS-CoV-2 as a result of ongoing community transmission in the vicinity of the workplace or because they may be infected with SARS-CoV-2 in areas where COVID-19 outbreaks may occur recently.
This type of staff includes those who work in schools, high-density working environments, and high-volume retail environments that are exposed to the general public. Engineering control measures for this group and high-risk groups include installing physical barriers such as high-efficiency air filters, improving ventilation effects, installing physical barriers such as transparent plastic sneezing protective devices, and installing parking-free windows to provide customer service.
It would be desirable for such workers to be equipped with respirators.
If a person is ill on the plane, protective measures should be taken to protect staff and other passengers, including placing the patient 6 feet away from the other passengers, assigning a crew member to serve the patient, providing a mask to the patient, or requiring the patient to cover the nose and mouth with a tissue when coughing or sneezing.
Crew members should wear disposable medical gloves when caring for sick travelers, touching body fluids, or potentially contaminated surfaces. Crew members should also wear additional personal protective equipment in the event of symptoms such as fever, persistent cough, or difficulty breathing.
Gloves and other disposable items should be disposed of in a biohazard bag, followed by cleaning and disinfection of contaminated surfaces. For commercial transportation, including cruise ships and other passenger ships, danger prevention and control measures also include delaying travel when the disease occurs. In the event of fever or other symptoms on board, self-isolation should be carried out and the medical room on board should be notified immediately.
Ideally, medical follow-up should be carried out in the cabins of isolated persons. For schools and places of childcare, the CDC recommends that if an infected person has been present at school, the corresponding schools and childcare places should be shut down for a short period of time, regardless of the spread in the community, for thorough cleaning or disinfection.
For low- and medium-level community transmission, social distancing strategies can be implemented, such as the cancellation of large gatherings such as field trips, gatherings and sports classes, choral classes or cafeteria meals, increasing the distance between desks, peak arrivals and departures, restrictions on non-essential visitor visits, and arranging separate health offices for children with flu-like symptoms.
If community transmission is severe, in addition to the implementation of a social distancing strategy, you can also consider prolonging the suspension period. For law enforcement officers who carry out daily routine activities, the CDC believes that the direct health risks they face are lower.
It is recommended that law enforcement personnel who must be exposed to COVID-19 confirmed patients or suspected individuals should act in accordance with the same level of protection as emergency medical technicians, including with appropriate personal protective equipment.
In the event of close contact in the course of the arrest, the staff should first clean and disinfect their service belts and equipment before they can use household cleaning spray or rag cleaning, and should comply with the standards of personal protective equipment sealing and disposal procedures and clothing sealing and cleaning procedures.
OSHA believes that some health care and tremor workers are at high or very high risk of exposure.
High-exposure workers include health care, support, laboratory, and medical transportation workers who are actually exposed to known or suspected COVID-19 infected people.
The risk of exposure becomes extremely high if a staff member performs droplet generation procedures on patients known or suspected COVID-19, or collects or processes specimens of an infected or suspected infected person.
Droplet generation procedures include intubation, cough induction procedures, bronchoscopy, some dental procedures and examinations, or cavity specimen collection.
High-exposure risk mortuary staff included staff involved in collating the bodies of the deceased, who had been identified or suspected by COVID-19, and had a higher risk of exposure if autopsies were performed. Additional engineering measures for this risk group also included isolation rooms prepared for COVID-19 confirmed or suspected cases, including when carrying out droplet generation procedures.
Some health care facilities and morgues can also be equipped with specialized negative pressure ventilation systems.
Biosafety Level 3 precautions should be taken when dealing with specimens.
The World Health Organization (WHO) recommends that different waiting areas be established for patients based on suspected cases of COVID-19. In addition to personal protective equipment, the Occupational Safety and Health Administration also recommends that respirators should be worn within 6 feet of patients known or suspected to be infected with SARS-CoV-2, as well as those who perform droplet generation procedures.
The U.S. requires NIOSH-approved N95 filter masks or better masks to be used in a comprehensive written respiratory protection program environment such as health testing, training, and medical examinations.
Other types of respirators can provide better protection and improve the comfort of their staff. WHO does not recommend the use of protective clothing, because COVID-19 is a respiratory disease and is not transmitted through body fluids.
The WHO recommends that screeners at entry points only need to wear medical surgical masks.
For workers who need to collect respiratory specimens from COVID-19 patients and provide care or transfer services to patients without performing droplet generation procedures, the WHO recommends the use of medical surgical masks, goggles or masks, robes and gloves.
If you want to perform the droplet generation procedure, you must replace the surgical mask with an N95 or FFP2 mask.
In view of the lack of supply of personal protective equipment around the world, WHO recommends reducing the need for personal protective equipment through physical barriers such as telemedicine and transparent windows, allowing only direct caregivers to enter the rooms of COVID-19 patients, using personal protective equipment only when personal protective equipment is indeed necessary for specific tasks, continuing to use the same respirator when caring for multiple patients with the same diagnosis, monitoring and coordinating the personal protective equipment supply chain, and not encouraging asymptomatic personnel to use masks.
Source: Katherine Maher, CEO of the Wikimedia Foundation
Recipient: All Wikimedia Foundation staff
Theme: [Covid-19] lightly loaded to prepare for the future
Delivery date/time: March 14, 2020 00:24 (Coordinated Universal Time)
Licence: CC0: No rights reserved
We have been in some difficult situations this month.
In the face of the COVID-19 epidemic, we clearly feel that the fate of all mankind is in solidarity, and we also recognize our responsibilities to each other.
We’re faced with unprecedented challenges, but we know that the best way to address them requires people around the world to empathize, work collaboratively, and build communities, and that’s at the heart of Wikimedia.
All our co-workers convey fraternity and care to each other via email, phone and chat, which is a testament to the difference between our colleagues and how fortunate we are to work with you.
I am incredibly grateful and proud to be friends with all of you.
In the past week, some people have expressed their appreciation for our work.
They made me realize how meaningful it is to have access to Wikipedia all over the world right now, as well as the symbolic power that this critical resource can bring to people around the world.
Regardless of whether your job is responsible for the operation of the website, salary payments, or community security, Wikimedia's normal operation is inseparable from your hard work.
Today’s world needs information from Wikipedia more than ever before.
At this stage, the content of our work, and even the way of work, will have an important impact on the world.
Given the importance of this mission and the responsibilities of our colleagues, we will make some important changes to our collaborative approach starting next week.
Modality and timetable adjustments
As Robyn mentioned earlier, executive teams met last night to discuss how the company works and schedules over the next few days to months.
At the meeting, we took into account what we see as an appropriate response to the company’s current challenges, as well as best practices to ensure the company’s sustainability during an epidemic.
The senior management team unanimously agreed to reduce the pressure on the company's employees, so that everyone can support the company's mission for a long time.
We support you to reduce the pressure on your work properly.
For all employees, contractors and contractors:
Our day-to-day working hours will be adjusted to 4 hours a day (or 20 hours per week), and subsequent changes will be notified.
This adjustment is not a holiday notification. If you can work longer each day, we'll arrange tasks for you based on your working hours.
However, given the unpredictability of the current state of the world, we give priority to taking care of your personal needs, such as taking care of your family, going out for shopping, or seeing a doctor.
We don’t keep track of your working hours.
If you are sick, put down your work.
Although this is self-evident, but we still want to remind you, please rest assured.
You don’t need to take sick or paid leave, but you need to inform the manager and help your team modify the working calendar and schedule to make sure that key areas of work aren’t affected.
(If you have been diagnosed with COVID-19 positive, please inform Bryan of the talent and cultural operations department, which will support you and ensure that your situation receives proper attention from management.)
For hourly employees, we will pay their salaries in full.
We’ve already said this and recommit ourselves to our commitments to contractors and hourly colleagues.
The salary we pay to everyone will continue to be calculated on the basis of the remuneration we have received for completing normal working hours under normal circumstances.
It’s not possible if you’re sick or unable to work.
If you are willing to work, we will support you.
Many people choose to communicate with the outside world through their work, releasing their own pressure.
“Our work can deliver incredible returns, especially in times like this.”
But taking care of yourself is still the first priority.
We ask that you communicate with your manager so that we can keep you updated and adjust accordingly.
Prioritize important tasks.
We have to keep some of the important jobs working properly.
The work carried out by website reliability engineering teams, human resources operations teams, trust and security teams, and fundraising teams (and others) is critical and may require additional support.
We will begin working with all departments to assess current objectives and shift our focus to supporting a number of things that are critical to our mission.
We all have a lot of work to do, but we all need to focus our work on the most important projects.
The temporary slowdown will not affect future developments.
We do not intend to “work overtime” after the epidemic.
We’re not going to ask you to work overtime to catch up, because that’s impractical right now.
We accept that the current environment has changed, and we will set new goals and timetables as appropriate.
What will happen to the APP (Annual Plan)?
In order to adapt to the new realities and day-to-day time requirements, we plan to adjust the delivery schedule of the 2020-2021 annual plan.
We hope to propose an extension of the 2019-2020 plan in exchange for more time to set the budget, so that employees can prioritize key tasks and take care of themselves and their families, and adjust their working arrangements for employees who need or want to reduce their workload in the next few weeks.
Extending the schedule can significantly reduce the organization’s current planned workload and reduce work pressure.
We'll make this proposal to the board next week. If the proposal is approved, we will update delegates and teams as soon as possible.
Thank you to the app team for their leadership during this process.
Office status, exposure and cleaning
Last week, we learned that a colleague in a San Francisco office may have been exposed to the COVID-19 virus.
However, out of prudence, we hired some antiviral cleaners to fully sterilize the San Francisco office.
Using hospital-level disinfection solutions, they sterilize a variety of surfaces as well as halls and elevators that lead to our floors.
Office buildings are in the process of fulfilling their duty of care agreement, using the corresponding products to ensure the safety of tenants.
We believe that when we decided to return to work, the office was fully prepared to welcome everyone.
Our office in Washington, D.C. uses the WeWork office, and WeWork shares the COVID-19 agreement with us, as well as all our Washington, D.C. staff.
As of last week, the office in Washington, D.C., had moved to full telecommuting in line with the guidance we shared with the San Francisco office.
At the same time, as some colleagues in the New York office already know, we’ve also been talking about leasing locations in Brooklyn.
These discussions are continuing, but they may be postponed.
For the first time, some colleagues were exposed to telecommuting.
Other long-term telecommuting colleagues wanted to provide some advice to these colleagues in terms of adjusting their working methods:
The meeting time should be controlled within one to two hours.
For a longer period of time, please consider dispersing the contents of the meeting into meetings on multiple dates.
Clearly define the objectives of the meeting, set the agenda of the meeting, and send reading materials in advance.
Use video conferencing as the default mode of meeting, while using tools such as Google Docs and Zoom for online collaboration and networking.
In each meeting, arrange a moderator to chair the meeting, arrange for one person to monitor the questions in the chat window and track changes in the speaker's list, and arrange for the other person to be responsible (or use a multi-person collaborative approach) to help make the minutes of the meeting.
If you need a comfortable headset, please contact Technical Support by email.
Use your health allowance to buy snacks.
Join the #remoties channel on Slack to discuss distributed work with colleagues
The human resources operations team is working on ergonomic guidance in the form of webinars to help all of Foundation’s colleagues improve their productivity during a distributed office.
Last week, we asked all community recipients to cancel public events funded by Wikimedia, such as the editorial pine, before the WHO announced the end of the outbreak.
These requests and other restrictions regarding the cancellation of activities may result in these community recipients not being able to complete their original donation activities, and we have informed them that we understand this, while also promising that none of them will be penalized for postponing or modifying these goals.
Over the next week, we will continue to provide more guidance at Wikimania and other regional community meetings and themed community meetings.
The global community appears to be shrouded in grief as a result of the devastation of the epidemic, but it also reflects the relief of being able to clearly keep an eye on their respective communities, Wikimedia, and others.
In the future, the CRT team will create a page on Meta-Wiki to provide a space for the community to monitor impact and track how the community communicates with us.
Keep in touch and communicate COVID-19).
We’re planning a special staff meeting next Thursday at 14:00 (Coordinated Universal Time)/07:00 (Pacific Time), and we’ll send invitations to your calendar later.
At the meeting, we will share some updates, answer your questions, and leave some time for everyone to discuss.
We are united and wholeheartedly helping you here.
At the same time, you can learn more about the information mentioned in this email and other important information related to COVID-19).
The CRT team updates these pages in a timely manner and concentrates all the information in one place.
“We are seeking to maintain regular communication with employees living in countries that are currently severely affected by the outbreak.
If you have any questions about travel, activities, major workflows, or insurance, or if you need help from others, please feel free to contact the CRT team and work with them.
We are happy to provide you with the support and contacts you need.
If you have secret or sensitive issues, please contact Bryan Judan, Head of Global Human Resources Operations.
Although we are faced with these changes, we cannot give up our work and obligations as a result.
On the contrary, this outbreak allows us to realize that we currently may need to make adjustments to our work and obligations in ways not seen in the past.
We believe that these measures are necessary to enable us to support each other in continuing to do their work, to provide the support we need for our actions, and to continue to bring the services that the world depends on.
If you plan at all times, you can’t be afraid of challenges.
Now, please support each other and work together to create space for important work in the coming weeks and even months.
We need everyone to contribute to achieving this goal, so please take care of yourself and your family in order to stay at their best when needed.
Finally, please remember to wash your hands and don’t touch your face.
Katherine, the CRT team (Amanda K, Amy V, Bryan J, Doreen D, Gregory V, Jaime V, Joel L, Lynette L, Ryan M, and Tony S) and other members of the leadership team (Grant I, Heather W, Jaime V, Janeen U, Lisa S, Robyn A, Ryan M and Toby N).
Angiotensin-converting Enzyme 2 (ACE2) is an enzyme attached to the outer surface of cells (cell membranes) in the lungs, arteries, hearts, kidneys and intestines.
ACE2 inhibits the activity of associated angiotensin-converting Enzyme (ACE) by reducing the content of angiotensin II and increasing the content of Ang(1-7), which is expected to become a drug target for the treatment of cardiovascular disease. ACE2 is also the entry point for certain coronaviruses into cells.
The human version of this enzyme is often referred to as hACE2.
The angiotensin-converting enzyme 2 is a zinc-containing metallic enzyme located on the surface of endothelial cells and other cells.
The ACE2 protein contains an N-terminal peptide enzyme M2 domain and a C-terminal Collectrin kidney amino acid transporter domain.
ACE2 is a single-pass I membrane protein whose enzyme-active domain is exposed to the cell surface of the lungs and other tissues.
Another enzyme, known as sheddase, flices the extracellular domain of ACE2 from the transmembrane domain, resulting in the release of soluble proteins into the bloodstream and eventually excreted into the urine.
ACE2 is present in most organs: ACE2 is attached to the cell membrane, which is mainly type II alveoli cells of the lungs, intestinal epithelial cells of small intestines, arterial and intravenous endothelial cells of most organs, and arterial smooth muscle cells.
ACE2 mRNA expression has also been found in the cerebral cortex, striatum, hypothalamus, and brain stems.
ACE2’s main function is to balance ACEs.
ACE resolves angiotensin I cleavage into an angiotensin II with vasostriction activity.
ACE2 then cleavated angiotensin II (Asp-Arg-Val-Tyr-Ile-His-Pro-Phe) of the carboxyl-terminal amino acid phenylalanine and hydrolyzed it into vasodilation-active angiotensin (1-7), (H-Asp-Arg-Val-Tyr-Ile-His-Pro-OH).
ACE2 can also cleavage many other peptides, including de-arginine 9 hysteriopeptides, apelins, neurohypertensive proteins, morphines A, and gastrophins.
ACE2 also regulates membrane transportation of the neutral amino acid transport protein SLC6A19 and is associated with Hartnup disease.
As a transmembrane protein, ACE2 is the main entry point for certain coronaviruses entering cells, including HCoV-NL63; SARS-CoV (the virus that causes SARS); and SARS-CoV-2 (the virus that causes COVID-19).
More specifically, SARS-CoV and SARS-CoV2’s S1 spike protein combined with ACE2’s enzymatic domain on the cell’s surface, resulting in endowalting that allowed viruses and enzymes to be co-transposed into the nucleosomes within cells.
This process of entry also requires the host serine protease TMPRSS2 to trigger the activation of the S protein, which is currently being studied whether inhibition of the process can be used as a potential treatment. Some have speculated that lowering ACE2 levels in cells may help combat infection.
However, a number of professional associations and regulators have recommended the continued use of standard ACE inhibitors and ARB therapies.
A systematic review and meta-analysis published on July 11, 2012, found that “the use of ACE inhibitors significantly reduced the risk of pneumonia by 34% compared to the control group.”
Moreover, "for patients with a higher risk of pneumonia, especially patients with stroke and heart failure, the use of ACE inhibitors will also reduce the risk of pneumonia."
The use of ACE inhibitors has also been associated with a reduction in pneumonia-related mortality, but the results are not as reliable as reducing the overall risk of pneumonia.”
Recombinant person ACE2 (rhACE2) is thought to be a new way to treat acute pulmonary injury, which seems to improve pulmonary hemorrhagic dynamics and blood oxygen saturation in piglets caused by lipopolysaccharides.
rhACE2 has a half-life of about 10 hours in the human body with an effect time of 30 minutes, and the course of action (duration) is 24 hours.
Some of the findings suggest that rhACE2 may be a promising drug for patients who are intolerant to classical renin-angiotensin system (RAS) inhibitors or diseases with elevated levels of circulating angiotensin II. The efficacy of rhACE2 infusions for acute respiratory distress syndrome has been evaluated in clinical trials.
b’COVID-19 application is a mobile software designed to respond to the 2019-20 coronavirus outbreak, helping to trace close contacts and identifying individuals who may have been in contact with an infected person (“contacts”).
Many applications have been developed or proposed in some regions and jurisdictions with the support of official governments.
Many of the frameworks used to build close contact tracing applications have been established.
Privacy issues have also attracted a lot of attention, especially since some systems need to track the geographic location of app users.
The less intrusive alternative includes using a Bluetooth signal to record the distance between users and other phones.
On April 10, 2020, Google and Apple jointly announced that they will integrate this Bluetooth-enabled application directly into the Android and iOS operating systems.
In China, the government has jointly developed an app that enables citizens to self-exact whether they have been exposed to COVID-19 patients.
The program is currently used in more than 200 cities in China. Singapore has launched an app called TraceTogether.
Developed by the local IT community, the app is released as open source software and will be handed over to the government. North Macedonia has launched a Bluetooth-based app "StopKorona!" to track the exposure of potential infected people and help healthcare organizations respond quickly.
The application was developed by the North Macedonian Ministry of Communication Technology and the Ministry of Health.
As of April 14, 2020, the app is awaiting approval from the Google Play Store and the Apple App Store.
On 12 April, the government announced that the close contact tracing app has entered the advanced stage of development and will be in use in the coming weeks. Ireland and France also plan to launch similar applications ("StopCovid").
Both Australia and New Zealand are considering developing similar apps based on Singapore's TraceTogether app and BlueTrace protocol. Russia plans to launch a geofencing app for COVID-19 diagnosed patients living in Moscow to ensure they don't leave their homes.
Ross Anderson, a professor of safety engineering at the University of Cambridge, cites some practical problems that may exist in the application system, including false positives, and the possible lack of effectiveness if only a small percentage of people use the app.
In order to prevent the spread of misleading or harmful “coronavirus” apps, Apple has imposed restrictions on the types of organizations that can publish coronavirus-related apps in the Apple App Store, which is limited to “official” or other well-reputed organizations.
Google and Amazon also imposed similar restrictions.
Privacy advocates said they were concerned about the use of coronavirus apps for large-scale surveillance, especially if the surveillance infrastructure built in response to the coronavirus epidemic should be dismantled after the threat of the epidemic was eliminated.
Amnesty International and more than 100 other organizations have called for restrictions on such surveillance.
These organizations have announced eight conditions to be met for government projects:
Surveillance must be “legitimate, necessary and proportionate”;
Monitoring and monitoring extensions must have a “sunset” clause;
The data collected is limited to COVID-19).
Data security and anonymized information must be protected, and there is evidence that it is effectively protected;
Digital surveillance must avoid exacerbating discrimination and marginalization;
Any data sharing with third parties shall be defined in accordance with the law;
safeguards must be provided for the prevention of data misuse and for citizens to have the right to respond to data abuse;
Requiring all “relevant stakeholders” to participate “effectively,” including public health experts and marginalized groups, was asked to participate. The German Chaos Computer Club (CCCC) and Reporter ohne Grenzen (RSF) also released checklists.
Google/Apple’s proposed plan is designed to address ongoing monitoring issues and remove it from the device’s operating system once it is no longer necessary.
Some countries have adopted web-based rather than application-based location tracking, eliminating the need for users to download apps, while also avoiding being tracked.
In Israel, web-based tracking has been approved.
A network solution with access to raw location data may have serious privacy concerns.
However, not all systems that come with a central server need access to personal location data; many privacy protection systems that communicate with each other only using central servers have now been created (see below for details).
South Korea has introduced a system that is not based on an application to perform contact tracing tasks.
Rather than using specialized applications, the system chose to collect tracking information from a variety of sources, including tracking data from mobile devices and credit card transaction data, and then combined that information to send notifications to potential infected people via text messages.
In addition to using this information to alert potential close contacts, the government has also been granted permission to disclose location information because the country has made significant changes to the information privacy law in the wake of the MERS outbreak.
The public has access to this information through many applications and websites, and there are also countries (including Germany) considering the simultaneous use of centralized systems and privacy protection systems.
As of April 6, 2020, no specific details have been released.
Privacy-preserving close contact tracing is an accepted concept, dating back to at least 2013. As of April 7, 2020, more than a dozen experts are working on privacy protection solutions, such as using Bluetooth Low Energy (BLE) to record the distance between users and other phones.
PEPP-PT is a coordinated solution with a centralized and decentralized approach and is not a single protocol. Decentralized protocols include decentralized privacy-preserving proximity tracking (DP-PPT/DP-3T), temporary contact numbers (TCN, originally referred to as contact event numbers, CEN), privacy-sensitive protocols, and mobile contact tracing mechanisms (PACTs).
According to these agreements, identifiable personal data will never leave the device, and all matches will take place on the device.
MIT’s Media Lab’s privacy team has been working on the SafePaths platform, which uses privacy-preserving techniques when collecting and using location or path cross-data to track COVID-19.
The platform is based on the research content development of a white paper, "Application Rogue: Maintaining Personal Privacy in an Epidemic," released in March 2020. In addition, Enigma MPC, a company that was originally founded at the MIT Media Lab and dedicated to developing privacy technology, is also developing a similar platform called SafeTrace.
SafeTrace leverages security hardware technology to enable users to share sensitive location and health data with other users and officials without compromising data privacy.
On April 5, 2020, a number of groups joined forces to form the Global TCN Alliance around essentially the same methods and basically overlapping protocols, with the goal of reducing fragmented information and supporting global interoperability of tracking and alerting applications, which is a key to achieving widespread adoption.
On April 9, 2020, the Government of Singapore announced that its official government application was using the open source BlueTrace protocol.
On April 10, 2020, Google and Apple, which control Android and iOS mobile platforms, respectively, announced a close contact tracing plan, claiming that the program combines Bluetooth low energy technology with privacy-preserving encryption technology, which can play a role in protecting privacy.
In addition, they published the core technical specifications used in the system.
Apple and Google say the system is scheduled to be rolled out in three phases:
Launch of tools to support the government’s creation of official coronavirus-tracking apps that protect privacy
Integrating this function directly into iOS and Android systems, Google and Apple plan to launch the system through operating system updates first, and the system will be deleted in the same way after the threat of the epidemic, so as to solve the follow-up monitoring problems.
Drug retargeting (also known as drug reuse, re-evaluation, redistribution, or treatment conversion) refers to the conversion of an approved drug for other purposes for the treatment of a disease or medical condition that is different from the purpose for which it was originally developed.
It is currently a scientific research route for the development of safe and effective treatments for COVID-19.
Other research interests include the development of the COVID-19 vaccine and plasma transfusions during the recovery period. SARS-CoV-2 has about 66 proteins that can be used in pharmaceuticals, each of which has multiple ligand binding sites.
Analyzing these binding sites provides a reasonable solution for developing effective antiviral drugs against the COVID-19 protein.
The most important SARS-CoV-2 target proteins include papaya-like proteases, RNA-dependent RNA polymerases, hydrases, S proteins, and ADP ribose diphosphatases.
Hussein A A et al. studied several candidate compounds, and subsequently controlled the approved drugs for the highest similarity of drugs, optimized and analyzed the skeleton similarity of these candidate compounds in order to accelerate the development of effective anti-SARS-CoV-2 drugs in their pre-clinical trials, and recommended them in clinical research design.
Chloroquine is an antimalarial drug that is also used to treat some autoimmune diseases.
On March 18, the World Health Organization (WHO) announced that four drugs, including chloroquine and related hydroxychloroquine, will be studied as part of the solidarity clinical trials.
New York Governor Andrew Cuomo announced that New York State chloroquine and hydroxychloroquine trials will begin on March 24. On March 28, the FDA issued an Emergency Use Authorization (EUA), which allows the use of hydroxychloroquine sulfate and chloroquine phosphate.
The therapy has not yet been approved by the FDA’s clinical trial process and is only approved by EUA as an experimental treatment for urgent use by patients who have been hospitalized but are unable to be treated in clinical trials.
According to the Center for Disease Control and Prevention (CDC), “the use, dosage, or timing of hydroxychloroquine used to prevent or treat SARS-CoV-2 infection” has not yet been determined.
Doctors said they used the drug "when there was no alternative."
A Turkish research team in Istanbul is working on a small study of chloroquine in combination with zinc, vitamin A, vitamin C and vitamin D.
Duke University and the University of Oxford are conducting large-scale research.
New York University’s Langoni School of Medicine is conducting a trial on the safety and efficacy of the preventive use of hydroxychloroquine.
Clinical trials conducted by China in Wuhan and Shenzhen said fapillavir was “clearly effective.”
In Shenzhen, 35 patients tested negative within an average of 4 days, compared with 45 patients who were not treated with the drug for 11 days.
In a study of 240 patients with pneumonia conducted in Wuhan, half received fapillavir, and the other half received Abidor.
The Italian Food and Drug Administration reminded the public that there is insufficient and preliminary evidence in support of the drug.
On April 2, Germany announced that it would purchase the drug from Japan as a reserve and use the military to transport the drug to a university hospital to use the drug to treat COVID-19 patients in the hospital.
According to the South China Morning Post, Abe has proposed to the Trump administration to buy the drug. Among the severe patients whose viruses are already multiplying, the efficacy of the drug may be reduced.
It may not be safe for pregnant women or those who are ready to use the drug during pregnancy.
A study of lopinavir/ritonavir (Kaletra), a combination of antiviral drugs lopinavir and ritonavir, concluded that “no benefits have been observed.”
These drugs are designed to inhibit the replication of the Human Immunodeficiency virus (HIV) by combining with proteases.
A team of researchers at the University of Colorado is trying to modify drugs to find compounds that can bind to SARS-CoV-2 proteases. The scientific community has criticized the use of resources for reusing drugs developed specifically for HIV/Acquired Immunity Syndrome (AIDS).
The World Health Organization has incorporated lopinavir/ritonavir into international solidarity experiments.
Redcivir is a drug created and developed by Gilead Sciences to treat Ebola virus and Marlborough virus infections. Gilead Scientific subsequently discovered that redesivir has antiviral activity against a variety of filaviruses, pneumoniaviruses, substick viruses, and coronaviruses in vitro.
One problem with antiviral therapy is that resistance produced by mutations can lead to more severe disease and spread.
A number of earlier pre-trial studies have shown that radcivir may have a higher drug-resistant genetic barrier. Multiple clinical trials are currently underway, two of which were conducted by Cleveland University Hospital; one for patients with moderate symptoms and the other for patients with more severe symptoms.
There are currently three clinical trials of vitamin C intravenous for patients with severe COVID-19 hospitalizations; two placebo-controlled trials (China, Canada) and one non-controlled trial (Italy).
On March 24, 2020, New York State began a trial of the antibiotic azithromycin.
Japan’s National Center for Global Health and Medicine (NCGM) is planning clinical trials of Teijin’s Alvesco, an inhaled corticoid used to treat patients infected with the new coronavirus.
A Phase II trial of an angiotensin-converting enzyme 2 is underway, which will enroll 200 patients in severe hospitalizations in Denmark, Germany, and Austria to determine the effectiveness of the therapy.
Researchers at the Institute of Cardiology in Montreal, Canada, are currently working on the role of cycondrine in reducing inflammation and pulmonary complications in patients with COVID-19 mild illness.
The COLCORONA study is enrolling 6,000 adults aged 40 and older who have been diagnosed with COVID-19 with mild symptoms that do not require hospitalization.
Pregnant women, lactating, or women who did not take effective contraception were not eligible.
Several anticoagulants are being tested in Italy.
Low-molecular heparin is widely used to treat patients, which prompted the Italian Food and Drug Administration to issue guidelines for its use.
On April 14, Italy announced a multi-centre study of 300 patients to investigate the use of the prevention and treatment dose of hepacin.
Because SARS-CoV-2 is a virus, the reuse of approved antiviral drugs, which were developed for previous outbreaks such as Middle East Respiratory Syndrome (MERS), Severe Acute Respiratory Syndrome (SARS) and West Nile virus, has attracted considerable scientific attention.
ribavirin: according to the guidelines of the 7th edition of China, the use of ribavirin treatment COVID-19 is recommended
Abidoral Hydrochloride: according to the guidelines of the seventh edition of China, the use of Abidol hydrochloride is recommended to treat COVID-19
Some potentially reusable antibiotics that have been identified for COVID-19 treatments:
Tocilizumab (anti-IL-6 receptor): approved in China.
Experiments have also been carried out in Italy and China. See also Tocilizumab#COVID-19.
b'A COVID-19 vaccine refers to an imaginary vaccine against the 2019 coronavirus disease (COVID-19).
Although there have been no clinical trials, multiple attempts to develop this vaccine are still underway.
In late February 2020, the World Health Organization (WHO) said it did not expect a vaccine against SARS-CoV-2 for the disease-causing virus within 18 months.
In April, five vaccine candidates entered the Phase I safety study.
COVID-19 was discovered in December 2019.
In 2020, a large-scale epidemic spread around the world, triggering a lot of investment and research activities in the development of vaccines.
Many organizations are using published viral genomes to develop possible SARS-CoV-2 vaccines.
CEPI’s vaccine development plan was launched in April and said its top priorities are speed, production capacity, large-scale deployment, and global accessibility.
In April, CEPI’s scientists reported that 10 different technology platforms were researching and developing effective COVID-19 vaccines in early 2020.
The main platform targets for entering Phase I safety research include:
nucleic acids (DNA and RNA) (Phase I developers and vaccine candidates: Moderna, mRNA-1273)
Viral vectors (stage I developers and vaccine candidates: CanSino Biologics, Type 5 adenovirus vectors)
CEPI scientists reported in April that a total of 115 vaccine candidates were in the early stages of development, 78 of which had been identified as ongoing projects (79 according to the Milken Institute) and 37 had been announced, with virtually no public information (presumably being planned or designed).
The Phase I-II trial undergoes initial safety and immunogenicity tests, usually randomized, placebo-controlled multi-point trials, while determining more accurate and effective doses.
Phase III trials typically involved more participants, including a control group, and tested the effectiveness of the vaccine to prevent the disease while monitoring adverse reactions at optimal doses.
Of the 79 vaccine candidates actively developed (the confirmation period up to the beginning of April 2020), 74 have not yet undergone human reviews (still in “pre-clinical” studies).
Around January 24, 2020, the University of Queensland, Australia, announced that it is studying the potential of a molecular pliers vaccine, which can genetically modify viral proteins to stimulate the immune response.
Around January 24, 2020, the International Vaccine Center (VIDO-InterVac) at the University of Saskatchewan in Canada announced the beginning of the development of the vaccine, with the goal of starting human trials in 2021.
The Chinese Center for Disease Control and Prevention and the University of Hong Kong announced vaccine development projects on 26 January and 28 January 2020, respectively.
Around January 29, 2020, Janssen, led by Hanneke Schuitemaker, announced that it had begun developing a vaccine.
Janssen is working on an oral vaccine in collaboration with its biotechnology partner Vaxart.
On March 18, 2020, Emergent BioSolutions announced a production partnership with Vaxart to jointly develop the vaccine.
On February 8, 2020, the OncoGen laboratory in Romania published a paper on the design of a vaccine, which uses techniques similar to those used in neoantigen immunotherapy for cancer.
On March 25, the director of the institute announced that they had completed the synthesis of the vaccine and began vaccine testing.
On February 27, 2020, Generex subsidiary NuGenerex Immuno-Oncology announced that it was embarking on a vaccine project to develop an Ii-Key peptide vaccine against COVID-19.
They hope to develop a vaccine candidate and conduct human trials “within 90 days.”
On March 5, 2020, Washington University in St. Louis announced its vaccine development program.
On March 5, 2020, the U.S. Army Medical Research and Materials Command at Fort Detrick and the Walter Reed Army Research Institute in Silver Spring, both located in western Maryland, announced that a vaccine was being developed.
Around March 10, 2020, Emergent Biosolutions announced a partnership with Novavax Inc.
development and production of vaccines.
The two organizations further announced plans to conduct preclinical trials and Phase I clinical trials by July 2020.
On March 12, 2020, India’s Ministry of Health announced that it is working on 11 isolates, and even if progress is accelerated, it will take at least a year and a half to two years to develop the vaccine.
On March 12, 2020, Medicago, a biotechnology company based in Québec, reported the development of coronavirus-like particles with partial funding from the Canadian Institutes of Health.
The vaccine candidate is in laboratory research and is scheduled to enter human trials in July or August 2020.
Earlier this week, the Guardian reported that U.S. President Donald Trump had provided CureVac with “a lot of money” to get exclusive access to the COVID-19 vaccine, which the German government protested.
On March 17, 2020, U.S. pharmaceutical company Pfizer announced a collaboration with German company BioNTech to develop a mRNA-based vaccine.
The mRNA-based vaccine candidate BNT162 is currently in preclinical testing and is expected to begin clinical trials in April 2020.
On March 17, 2020, Italian biotechnology company Takis Biotech announced that they will obtain preclinical test results in April 2020, and its final vaccine candidate may begin human trials in the fall.
On March 19, 2020, in France, the Epidemic Prevention Innovation Alliance (CEPI) announced an investment of $4.9 million to establish a COVID-19 vaccine research consortium consisting of the Pasteur Institute, Themis Bioscience (Vienna, Austria) and the University of Pittsburgh, bringing CEPI’s total investment in COVID-19 vaccine R&D to $29 million.
CEPI’s other investment partners in COVID-19 vaccine development include Moderna, Curevac, Inovio, Novavax, the University of Hong Kong, the University of Oxford and the University of Queensland.
On March 20, 2020, Russian health officials announced that scientists had begun animal testing of six different vaccine candidates.
Researchers at Imperial College London announced on March 20, 2020 that a self-amplified RNA vaccine for COVID-19 is being developed.
The vaccine candidate was developed within 14 days of receiving the virus sequence issued by China.
In late March, the Government of Canada announced $275 million to fund 96 COVID-19 medical countermeasures research projects, including numerous vaccine candidates from Canadian companies and universities, such as the Medicago and Saskatchewan University programs.
Around the same time, the Canadian government announced $192 million earmarked for the development of the COVID-19 vaccine, and plans to establish a national "vaccine bank", including several new vaccines, so that these vaccines can be put into use when the coronavirus outbreak occurs again.
On April 2, 2020, researchers at the University of Pittsburgh School of Medicine reported tests for PittCoVacc, a COVID-19 vaccine that may be used in mice, adding that “the SARS-CoV-2 S1 subunit vaccine provided by MNA enables [mice] to produce a strong antigen-specific antibody response, which begins to appear two weeks after immunity.”
On April 16, 2020, the School of Pharmacy at the University of Waterloo in Canada announced the design of a DNA-based vaccine candidate as a possible nasal spray.
Using bacteriophages, the researchers designed DNA to replicate inside human bacteria to produce harmless virus-like particles, which could stimulate the immune system to produce antibodies against the SARS-CoV-2 virus.
In March 2020, the U.S. government, industry, and three universities pooled their resources and combined cloud computing resources from HP, Amazon, Microsoft, and Google to access IBM’s supercomputers.
Some vaccines have heterogeneous effects, also known as non-specific effects.
This means that in addition to preventing diseases, they have other benefits.
Further randomized trials conducted in Australia are recruiting 4,170 health care personnel.
Vaccines under development may be unsafe or ineffective.
Using COVID-19-specific animal models, such as ACE2 genetically modified mice, other laboratory animals, and non-human primates, early studies evaluating vaccine efficacy have shown the need for biosafety level 3 containment measures for the treatment of living viruses and international coordination to ensure compliance with standardized safety procedures.
Vaccinations against SARS and MERS have been tested in non-human animal models.
As of 2020, there has been no safe and effective SARS treatment or preventive vaccine for humans.
According to research papers published in 2005 and 2006, novel vaccines and drugs for the treatment of SARS have been identified and developed as top priorities for governments and public health agencies around the world. In addition, there are currently no effective vaccines against MERS.
When MERS became endemic, it was thought that existing SARS research could provide a useful template for developing vaccines and therapies against MERS-CoV infections.
As of March 2020, one (DNA-based) MERS vaccine has completed Phase I human clinical trials, and three more vaccines are under development, all of which are viral vector vaccines, two are adenovirus vector vaccines (ChAdOx1-MERS, BVRS-GamVac), and one is the MVA vector vaccine (MVA-MERS-S).
A variety of social media articles have fueled conspiracy theories claiming that the virus behind COVID-19 has been found and that vaccines are already available.
Patents cited in various social media articles refer to patents for the genetic sequences and vaccines of other strains of existing coronaviruses, such as the SARS coronavirus.
2019 Coronavirus disease (COVID-19) is an infectious disease caused by severe acute respiratory syndrome (SARS-CoV-2).
Common symptoms include fever, coughing, and shortness of breath.
Other symptoms may include weakness, muscle soreness, diarrhoea, soreness, loss of smell, and abdominal pain.
The incubation period ranging from contact with the patient to onset ranges from 2 to 14 days, usually about 5 days.
Although the symptoms are mild in most cases, some cases develop viral pneumonia and multi-organ failure.
As of April 17, 2020, the total number of cases reported in 210 countries and regions around the world exceeded 2.24 million, and the number of deaths exceeded 153,000.
At present, more than 568,000 people have been treated and recovered. The virus is mainly transmitted from person to person through close contact, often through droplets caused by coughing, sneezing, or speaking.
Although the exhalation will produce droplets, the droplets usually fall to the ground or on the surface of various objects, which will not cause long-distance transmission.
In addition, if someone touches the surface contaminated by the virus, and then touches the mouth of their eyes and nose, they may also be infected with the virus.
The virus can survive on the surface of an object for up to 72 hours.
Although it may also spread before and after recovery, it is most contagious three days prior to onset. The standard diagnostic method is a real-time reverse transcription polymerase chain reaction (rRT-PCR) test for nasopharyngeal swabs.
It is recommended that people with suspected viral infections and their caregivers wear masks.
Suggestions for the wearing of masks by the public vary from place to place, with some authorities opposing the wearing of masks, while others have called for them to be worn.
There is currently no COVID-19 vaccine or specific antiviral regimen.
The vast majority of countries in all six WHO regions have reported local transmission of the disease.
People infected with the virus may be asymptomatic and may also have suspected flu symptoms, such as fever, cough, weakness and shortness of breath.
Emergency symptoms include difficulty breathing, persistent chest pain or chest tightness, confusion of consciousness, difficulty in waking up, and bruises on the face or lips; if these symptoms occur, it is recommended to seek medical attention immediately.
A small number of cases may be accompanied by upper respiratory tract symptoms, such as sneezing, runny nose, or sore throats.
Different proportions of gastrointestinal symptoms, such as nausea, vomiting and diarrhea, were also monitored.
In some cases in China, only symptoms of chest tightness and palpitations appear at the beginning.
In some cases, it may develop into viral pneumonia, multi-organ failure, and even death.
Let’s call this the incubation period.
COVID-19 usually has an incubation period of 5-6 days, but may range from 2 to 14 days.
97.5% of infected people experience symptoms within 11.5 days of infection. The report states that not all infected people experience symptoms.
The role played by asymptomatic infected persons in the spread of the epidemic has not yet been fully clarified; however, preliminary evidence suggests that asymptomatic infected persons may promote the spread of the disease.
Studies have not yet been determined for asymptomatic infections, and studies are still being intensified. The Korean Center for Disease Control and Prevention (KCDC) reports that 20% of confirmed cases do not show any symptoms while hospitalized.
China's National Health Commission has included asymptomatic cases in daily notifications since April 1st; 130 (78 percent) of the 166 infected people on that day were asymptomatic when tested.
Both sputum and saliva can carry a large number of viruses.
Speak out loud and splash more droplets than normal speech.
A study in Singapore found that an unobstructed cough can cause droplets to spread as far as 4.5 meters (15 feet).
Although such viruses generally do not spread through the air, the National Academy of Sciences has determined that it may be transmitted through biological aerosols, and air collectors in the corridors outside the ward have detected positive samples of viral RNAs.
Medical procedures such as intubation and cardiopulmonary resuscitation (CPR) can cause respiratory secretions to fog, resulting in airborne transmission.
There are also concerns that the virus may be transmitted through feces, but this risk is considered to be lower. The virus is the most contagious when a patient develops symptoms; although it may spread before symptoms appear, the risk is lower.
According to the European Center for Disease Control and Prevention (ECDC), although the spread of the new coronavirus is not yet completely clear, the average person can infect 2-3 people. The survival time of the virus on the surface of the object ranges from a few hours to a few days.
Specifically, it is known that the new coronavirus survives one day on cardboard, up to three days on plastic (polypropylene) and stainless steel (AISI 304), and up to four hours on 99% pure copper products.
However, its survival time is also affected by humidity and temperature.
If used correctly, soaps and cleaners can also have the effect of killing the virus. Soap products can degrade the virus's fat protective layer, making it inactive, while also removing the virus from the skin and other surfaces.
Other solutions, such as ammonium benza and chlorhexidine gluconate (a surgical disinfectant), are less effective. A study in Hong Kong, China, showed that saliva samples were collected on an average of two days after admission to the hospital.
5/6 of patients had the highest viral load in the first sample, and one in six had the highest viral load detected the next day.
Serious Acute Respiratory Syndrome (SARS-CoV-2) is a new type of severe acute respiratory syndrome coronavirus that was first isolated from three pneumonia patients in a group of acute respiratory diseases in Wuhan.
All the characteristics of this new SARS-CoV-2 virus are present in the relevant coronaviruses in nature.
Before the virus enters the human body, it can be killed with household soap, because the soap can destroy the virus's protective sac membrane. SARS-CoV-2 is closely related to the original SARS-CoV.
The lungs are the organ most affected by COVID-19 because this virus enters the host cell through angiotensin-converting enzyme 2 (ACE2), which has the highest levels in the lung’s Type II alveoli cells.
The virus binds to ACE2 and enters the host cell through a special surface glycoprotein known as a “streak (cystic particles).”
Among the patients admitted to hospitals in Wuhan, China, 12% of infected people were found to have acute myocardial damage, and the proportion of severe patients was higher.
Due to the deterioration of the disease, the systemic inflammatory response and immune system disorders are caused, and the incidence of cardiovascular symptoms is higher, but acute heart muscle injury may also be related to ACE2 receptors in the heart.
ACE2 receptors are highly expressed in the heart and are involved in regulating heart function.
It is reported that the incidence of blood clots (31%) and venous thrembolism (25%) in COVID-19 infected people in ICU custody may be related to poor prognosis. An autopsies in patients with COVID-19 found diffuse alveolar injury (DAD), which is seen in the pulmonary.
Although SARS-COV-2 has nevorence to respiratory epithelial cells expressing ACE2, patients with severe COVID-19 have an over-inflammatory response throughout the body.
In particular, pathogenic GM-CSF secreted T cells in patients with COVID-19 were associated with inflammatory IL-6 secretive monocytes and severe lung pathology.
The autopsy report also saw lymphocyte infiltration.
The WHO has published several disease detection programs.
The standard detection method uses real-time reverse transcription polymerase chain reaction (rRT-PCR).
This test usually detects respiratory tract samples obtained through nasopharyngeal swabs; however, nasal swabs or sputum samples can also be used.
In general, the results can be obtained within a few hours to two days.
It is also possible to carry out blood tests, but it is necessary to collect two blood samples at an interval of two weeks, and the results have almost no immediate value.
Chinese scientists have been able to isolate coronavirus strains and publish genetic sequences so that laboratories around the world can independently develop polymerase chain reaction (PCR) reagents to detect whether they are infected with the virus.
As of April 4, 2020, antibody testing (not only to detect active infections, but also to detect whether an individual has been infected with the virus in the past) is still in development, but it has not yet been promoted.
Based on China’s experience, the detection accuracy is only 60% to 70%.
On March 21, 2020, the U.S. FDA approved the first real-time diagnostic reagent, which was put into use by the end of the month. The diagnostic guidelines issued by Zhongnan Hospital of Wuhan University propose methods for detecting infections based on clinical characteristics and epidemiological risks.
The early infection is more common with double-lung polyleafly glass shadow, which has periphery asymmetrical and post-part fabrics.
With the development of the virus, there may be sub-pleural manifestations, gravel road signs (small leaf spacing thickening with different degrees of alveoli filling) and lung changes.
There is very little information about COVID-19 micropathological injuries and pathophysiology.
The main pathological findings of the autopsy are as follows:
Examination with the naked eye: pleuritis, pocarditis, pulmonary transformation and pulmonary edema
Viral Pneumonia of four severe levels were observed:
Mild Pneumonia: pulmonary edema, pulmonary cell proliferation, a large number of atypical lung cells, interstitial inflammation with lymphocyte infiltration and the formation of polynucleocytes
Severe Pneumonia: Plurative alveoli injury (DAD) with diffuse alveoli exudation.
DAD is the cause of acute respiratory distress syndrome (ARDS) and severe hypoxemia.
Restorency Pneumonia: Alveoli Cavity Expulsion Fluidization and Lung Interstitial Fibrification
Blood: Dispersive intravascular coagulation (DICs); young white-young red blood cells
In order to reduce the risk of infection, preventive measures can be taken include: staying at home; avoiding entering crowded areas; washing hands frequently with soap and water for at least 20 seconds; maintaining good respiratory hygiene; avoiding touching your eyes, nose, or mouth without washing your hands.
The U.S. Centers for Disease Control and Prevention (CDC) recommends covering your nose and mouth with a tissue when coughing or sneezing; if you don’t, it’s recommended to cover the inside of your elbow.
It is recommended to properly clean hand hygiene after coughing or sneezing.
The CDC recommends wearing cloth masks in public to prevent asymptomatic patients from spreading the virus to some extent. At the heart of social distancing strategies lies in closing schools and workplaces, restricting travel, and canceling large public gatherings, thereby reducing the chance that infected people will come into contact with large groups.
In addition, the social distancing guidelines provide for a distance of at least 6 feet (1.8 meters) in interpersonal communication.
There are currently no drugs to effectively prevent COVID-19). Given that the vaccine is not expected to be developed until at least 2021, efforts to reduce the peak of the epidemic are key to controlling the spread of COVID-19, which is what we know as the "leveling epidemiological curve."
At the same time, the CDC advises people to wash their hands regularly with soap and tap water for at least 20 seconds, especially after going to the toilet or when there is obvious dirt on their hands, before meals, and after rubbing their nostals, coughing, or sneezing.
In addition, it is recommended to use alcohol-containing hand sanitizers with at least 60% alcohol, but only when there is no soap and tap water around. For areas that are unable to buy water-free sanitizers, WHO provides two formulations for local production.
These formulations stimulate antimicrobial activity through ethanol or isopropanol.
The use of hydrogen peroxide to help eliminate bacterial spores in alcohol; hydrogen peroxide is not a “hand-sterilized active substance.”
Add glycerin as a moisturizer.
Provide supportive care, which may include infusion therapy, oxygen support, and other critical organ support that is affected.
The CDC recommends that people with suspected virus carriers wear simple masks.
External membrane oxygenation (ECMO) has been used to relieve symptoms of respiratory failure, but its benefits remain to be studied.
It is recommended to maintain personal hygiene and promote a healthy lifestyle and eating habits, thereby improving immunity.
Supportive therapies may be effective for patients with mild illness in the early stages of infection. The WHO and the National Health and Health Commission of China have launched an initiative calling for the proper care of COVID-19 patients hospitalized.
U.S. experts in critical care and lung experts have compiled treatment recommendations from various agencies and made them freely available through IBCC.
As of April 2020, COVID-19 efficacy therapy has not yet been developed.
In response to some symptoms, some medical experts recommend the use of paracetamol (acetaminophen) instead of ibuprofen for first-line treatment.
It is necessary to take preventive measures to minimize the risk of virus transmission, and special attention should be paid to protection in medical environments where aerosols may occur, such as intubation or when ventilating.
For healthcare workers caring for COVID-19 patients, the CDC recommends that, in addition to standard preventive measures, exposure precautions, and airborne prevention measures, patients should be transferred to the Airborne Infectious Disease Isolation Room (AIIR). The CDC outlined guidelines for the use of personal protective equipment (PPE) during outbreaks.
Recommended equipment: PPE protective clothing, respirators or masks, eye protection and medical gloves. If possible, it is best to use a respirator (rather than a mask).
N95 masks are allowed to be used in industrial settings, but the FDA has authorized the use of such masks in accordance with the Emergency Use Authorization (EUA).
It is mainly used to prevent inhalation of suspended particles (such as dust), but it cannot be used for other purposes to effectively isolate specific biological agents.
If there is no mask, the CDC recommends using a mask, and if necessary, you can also wear a homemade mask.
Most COVID-19 cases have mild symptoms and do not require the use of mechanical ventilation or other options, but a certain proportion of cases require such measures.
Healthcare workers are actively conducting research to provide respiratory support to COVID-19 patients with symptoms of respiratory failure. There has been evidence that hyperinfluenced oxygen therapy or bipolar positive airway ventilation can avoid intubation.
It is not clear whether these two treatment options can produce the same efficacy for severe patients.
If conditions permit, some doctors tend to use innovative mechanical ventilation, because this technology can also limit the spread of aerosol particles compared to high-flow oxygen therapy through the nose. Severe cases are dominated by the elderly (more than 60 years old, especially those over the age of 80).
Many developed countries have insufficient beds per capita, and in view of medical conditions constraints, the medical system is unable to cope with the severe situation caused by the surge in the number of COVID-19 cases.
A Chinese study found that 5% of patients need to be sent to intensive care units, 2.3% need mechanical ventilation, and 1.4% of patients will die.
About 30 percent of COVID-19 admissions patients in China were eventually admitted to the ICU.
With the development of acute respiratory distress syndrome (ARDS) caused by COVID-19, the complexity of mechanical ventilation has further increased, and it is becoming more and more difficult.
At that time, it is necessary to use a ventilator that supports pressure control mode and high PEEP to maximize the delivery of oxygen, while minimizing the risk of lung damage and pneumoconiosis caused by the ventilator.
Older ventilators may not be able to achieve high PEEP.
Potential therapies have been studied since January 2020, and several antivirals have entered clinical trials.
Redcivir appears to be one of the most promising drugs available today.
Although new drugs may not be developed until 2021, several of the drugs tested have either been approved for other purposes or have entered the advanced testing phase.
Severely ill patients may attempt to use antiviral drugs.
The WHO calls on volunteers to participate in potential therapeutic efficacy and safety trials. The FDA has provisionally authorized recovery plasma transfusions as an experimental treatment to treat critically ill patients who are dying.
There are currently no necessary clinical studies to prove that the therapy can safely and effectively treat the disease.
In February 2020, China released a mobile application to contain the outbreak.
Users are required to enter their name and ID number as required.
The app is able to detect “close contact” by monitoring data to determine the potential risk of infection.
Each user can also check the status of the other three users.
Once potential risks are detected, the app not only recommends self-isolation, but also alerts local health officials. Comprehensive use of mobile data big data analytics, facial recognition technology, mobile phone tracking, and artificial intelligence to track infected people and those they come into contact with in South Korea, Taiwan, and Singapore.
In March 2020, the Israeli government approved security agencies to track mobile phone data of suspected coronavirus carriers.
The aim of this measure is to enforce isolation and to protect groups that may be exposed to infected citizens.
Also in March 2020, Deutsche Telekom shared aggregated mobile phone location data with the Robert Koch Institute, Germany’s federal government agency, in order to study and prevent the spread of the virus.
Russia deploys facial recognition technology to detect people who violate quarantine rules.
Giulio Gallera, Italy’s regional health commissioner, said mobile phone operator data showed that “40 percent of citizens are still walking around.”
The German government held a 48-hour weekend hackathon with more than 42,000 participants.
In addition, Estonian President Kersti Kaljulaid has called for creative solutions to be adopted around the world to prevent the spread of the coronavirus.
Isolation, travel restrictions, treatment of side effects, or fear of an infection, all of which can be troubling.
Rory O'Connor said: “Social isolation, loneliness, health anxiety, stress and sluggishness are the perfect storms to harm people’s mental health and well-being.”
The disease is milder, with few or no symptoms, similar to other common upper respiratory tract diseases, such as the common cold.
Moderate cases can usually recover within two weeks, while severe or critical cases can take three to six weeks to recover.
