In the most affected areas, COVID-19 may rapidly develop into acute respiratory distress syndrome (ARDS), which in turn leads to respiratory failure, septic shock, or multi-organ failure.
COVID-19-related complications include sepsis, clotting abnormalities, and heart, kidney, and liver damage.
6% of COVID-19 admitted patients had clotting abnormalities, in particular prolonged prothrombin, and 4% of patients in this group had abnormal kidney function.
About 20-30% of patients with COVID-19 had an increase in hepaticase (conaminase).
According to the same report, the average time from morbidity to death is 10 days, of which the average length of hospitalization is five days.
However, patients transferred to the ICU were admitted to death for an average of seven days.
Early case studies found an average of 14 days from the onset of early symptoms to death, with an overall time range of 6 to 41 days.
A study by China’s National Health Commission (NHC) found that male mortality was 2.8 percent, compared with 1.7 percent for women.
