Of the deaths reported by China’s National Health Commission, 11.8 per cent were due to elevated troponin levels or cardiac arrest.
According to data released in the U.S. in March, 89% of admitted patients had a prior history. The availability of medical resources and regional socio-economic conditions may also affect mortality.
Depending on regional differences and methodological difficulties, disease mortality estimates vary.
Underestimating cases of mild illness may lead to an overestimation of mortality.
However, if the case death is caused by a previous infection, it may mean that the current mortality rate is underestimated.
Compared to non-smokers, smokers are 1.4 times more likely to develop COVID-19 critically ill patients, and are about 2.4 times more likely to die from intensive care or death than non-smokers. There are concerns about long-term sequelae of the disease.
China’s Hong Kong Hospital Authority found that lung capacity in some rehabilitated people fell by 20 to 30 percent, and lung scans showed organ damage.
In addition, it may face post-intensive care syndrome after rehabilitation.
As of March 2020, it is uncertain whether previous infections will lead to effective long-term immunity for those who have recovered.
Immunity may have been determined based on behavioral characteristics of other coronaviruses, but there have been reports that some COVID-19 cases tested positive for the coronavirus shortly after recovery.
It is believed that these cases are persistently worsening rather than re-infecting.
This virus is believed to be a natural virus that originates in animals and is infected through overflows.
The actual origin has not yet been determined, but as of December 2019, almost all of the infections were human-to-human transmission.
A study of the first batch of 41 confirmed cases of COVID-19 published by The Lancet in January 2020 showed that the earliest onset date was December 1, 2019.
The earliest onset date reported in the official WHO publication is December 8, 2019.
There are usually several ways to quantify mortality.
These numbers vary from region to region, change over time, and are influenced by population characteristics such as detection levels, the quality of health systems, treatment options, the timing of the first outbreak, and age, gender, and overall health status.
At the end of 2019, WHO assigned the ICD-10 emergency disease code U07.1 to laboratory-confirmed SARS-CoV-2 infection deaths and U07.2 to clinically or epidemiologically diagnosed COVID-19 deaths without laboratory-confirmed SARS-CoV-2 deaths. The case fatality rate is equal to the total number of deaths within a given time frame divided by the total number of confirmed cases.
According to statistics from Johns Hopkins University, as of April 17, 2020, the global case fatality rate was 6.9% (153,822/2,240,191).
Other measures include case mortality (CFR) and infection mortality (IFR), which reflects the number of disease deaths as a percentage of the number of confirmed cases, while the latter reflects the number of disease deaths as a percentage of the number of infected people, including those diagnosed and undiagnosed.
There is no time limit for these statistics, and it tracks the entire process from infection to case resolution in a particular population.
Although not all infected people will produce antibodies, the presence of antibodies can provide information on the number of infected people.
Castiglione d'Adda, Italy’s epicentre of the outbreak, is a town of only 4,600 people, and 80 people (1.7%) have died.
In Gangelt, the epidemic has spread through various carnival festivals, even affecting young people, but the mortality rate is relatively low, and not all COVID-19 deaths are officially classified.
In addition, the German medical system is overwhelmed.
According to blood donors, about 3% of Dutch citizens may have antibodies.
To date, it has been confirmed that 69 people (0.004% of the total population) have died from COVID-19.
The impact of the epidemic and the mortality rate vary by gender.
Studies in China and Italy have shown that male mortality is higher than that of women.
Men aged 50-60 have the highest risk of death, with a similar gender gap in the 90-year-old age group.
The male mortality rate in China is 2.8%, and the female mortality rate is 1.7%.
The exact cause of this gender difference is unclear, but it may be caused by genetic and behavioral factors.
On the basis of gender-based immunological differences, smoking rates are lower in women and comorbidities (e.g., high blood pressure) are lower in men than in women, resulting in a higher male mortality rate than women.
In Europe, 57 per cent were infected by men, compared with 72 per cent of all deaths due to COVID-19.
As of April 2020, the U.S. government has not tracked gender-related data on COVID-19 infections.
Studies have shown that viral diseases such as Ebola virus, HIV, influenza and SARS have different effects on men and women.
The proportion of female health workers (especially nurses) is higher, and the risk of exposure to the virus is also higher.
On February 11, 2020, the World Health Organization (WHO) announced that the disease was officially named "COVID-19".
Tedros Adhanom Ghebreyesus, head of the WHO, explained this: CO stands for “corona”, VI stands for “virus”, D stands for “disease,” and 19 represents the time of the outbreak’s first outbreak (December 31, 2019).
This name was chosen to avoid citing specific geographic locations (e.g., China), animal species, or populations to comply with international nomenclature recommendations to prevent stigmatization. The virus that causes COVID-19 is known as Severe Acute Respiratory Syndrome Coronavirus Type 2 (SARS-CoV-2).
In addition, the WHO also uses “COVID-19 viruses” and “COVID-19 viruses” in mass transmission.
Diseases and viruses are commonly referred to as “coronavirus.”
During the initial outbreak in Wuhan, China, viruses and diseases were widely referred to as “coronavirus” and “Wuhan coronavirus.”
In January 2020, the WHO recommended 2019-nCov and 2019-nCoV acute respiratory diseases as temporary virus and disease names, in accordance with the 2015 guidelines on the use of locations in the names of diseases and viruses.
On February 11, 2020, the official names COVID-19 and SARS-CoV-2 were released.
Given capacity constraints in standard supply chains, some digital manufacturers have started printing health care materials such as nasal swabs and respirator parts.
For example, an Italian hospital is in desperate need of ventilation valves, suppliers are unable to deliver parts within the requested time, and a local start-up company reverse-engineers to print out the 100 ventilation valves they need overnight.
In the aftermath of the initial COVID-19 outbreak, conspiracy theories, misinformation and disinformation about the disease’s origin, scale, prevention, treatment, and other aspects spread rapidly through the network.
Humans seem to be able to spread the virus to other animals.
Studies have not found evidence of viral replication in pigs, ducks and chickens.
At present, no drugs or vaccines have been approved for the treatment of this disease.
Government agencies, academic groups, and industry researchers are actively conducting international research to develop vaccines and drugs that treat COVID-19.
In March, the World Health Organization launched a “solidarity trial” to evaluate the therapeutic effects of four existing antiviral compounds that are currently most likely to produce efficacy.
At present, there is no vaccine, but institutions at all levels are actively developing vaccine candidates.
Because both SARS-CoV and SARS-CoV-2 enter human cells through the ACE2 receptor, it is necessary to take advantage of SARS-CoV’s previous work.
Three vaccine strategies are being studied.
First of all, the researchers plan to develop an all-virus vaccine.
The use of such viruses, whether inactive or dead viruses, is intended to provoke a rapid immune response in the human body when COVID-19 is newly infected.
The second strategy is the development of subunit vaccines aimed at developing a vaccine that stimulates the immune system’s sensitive response to certain subunits of the virus.
In SARS-CoV-2 cases, such studies focused on assistant viruses invading ACE2 enzyme receptacle proteins.
The third strategy is the development of nucleic acid vaccines (DNA vaccine or RNA vaccine, a new type of vaccine development technology).
It is necessary to test the safety and effectiveness of the experimental vaccines developed by the above strategies. On March 16, 2020, four Seattle volunteers launched the first vaccine clinical trial.
Vaccines contain harmless genetic sequences copied from disease-causing viruses. It has long been argued that antibody-dependent-enhancing effects could be a major challenge in the development of SARS-COV-2 vaccines.
As of April 2020, there are more than 300 clinical trials conducted.
Seven trials were evaluated and approved for the treatment of malaria, including four studies on hydroxychloroquine or chloroquine.
Transforming antiviral drug use accounts for the vast majority of research in China, and nine Phase III clinical trials on redesivir will be conducted in a number of countries by the end of April.
As of April 2020, a dynamic review of the clinical development of the COVID-19 vaccine and drug candidates has been initiated. Several existing antiviral drugs to treat COVID-19 are currently being evaluated, including redcivir, chloroquine and hydroxychloroquine, lopinavir/ritonavir, and lopinavir/ritonavir combination interferon β.
As of March 2020, the efficacy of redcivir has been preliminarily confirmed.
After observation, it was observed that the clinical symptoms of patients treated with sympathetic drugs were alleviated.
Phase III clinical trials are underway in the U.S., China, and Italy. In February 2020, China launched chloroquine (past used to treat malaria) studies with preliminary results.
However, there have been calls for peer review of this research.
Korean and Chinese health authorities recommended the use of chloroquine.
However, the Wuhan Institute of Virology recommends taking 1 gram per day, while cautioning that twice the dose is very dangerous and possibly even deadly.
On March 28, 2020, the FDA issued an emergency use authorization for chloroquine and hydroxychloroquine, but doctors have the authority to decide whether to use both of these drugs to treat COVID-19 patients. China's seventh edition of the guidelines also proposed the use of interferons, ribavirin, or abidol hydrochloride for COVID-19.
Preliminary data suggest that high doses of ribavirin have an inhibitory effect on SARS-CoV-2 in vitro.
It is recommended to carry out further in vivo studies using nitrazonet after confirming the inhibition of low concentrations of SARS-CoV-2. Studies have shown that the original spike protein caused by transmembrane protease serine 2 (TMPRSS2) is a necessary condition for SARS-CoV-2 to interact with ACE2 receptors into the human body.
There are serious limitations in chloroquine and hydroxychloroquine studies, whether in combination with azithromycin or not, and these therapies cannot be accepted by the medical community unless further research has been conducted. Ostamivir has no inhibitory effect on SARS-CoV-2 in vitro and has not been found to have therapeutic effects on COVID-19.
Cytokine storms can be a complication of later treatment for patients with severe COVID-19.
There is evidence that hydroxychloroquine may have anti-cytokine storm properties. Following the completion of a small study, the Chinese National Health and Health Commission included tozuzumab in treatment guidelines.
Following the positive test results for critically ill patients, Italy is conducting a state-level non-randomized trial.
Combining seroferritin blood testing to recognize cytokine storms, designed to curb disease progression, cytokine storms are believed to result in the death of some infected people.
In 2017, with the support of retrospective case studies, the FDA approved the use of interleukin 6 receptor antagonists to treat steroid refractory cytokine-releasing syndromes caused by other causes, known as CAR T cell therapy.
To date, there has been no random-controlled evidence that tocilizumab is effective in treating CRS.
